00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.400
So, the first question is, 
“What is a database?”

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.000
You’ll often see databases 
abbreviated as DB.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.600
So DB would stand for database.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.500
And, a database is any collection 
of related information.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.900
What I’m sharing with you guys here is the absolute 
most general definition of this word.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.800
A database really, if you want to get down to it, 
is just any collection of related information.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.800
So, that could be something like a phonebook, 

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.100
a shopping lists, a to-do list, 
your five best friends, Facebook’s userbase.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.900
All of these are examples of databases.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.600
All of these are collections of information 
that store related stuff, right?

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.400
So, the phone book, right?

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.600
This stores people’s phone numbers.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.200
Their names and their phone numbers.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.300
It’s a collection of related information.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.100
A to-do list – this stores a list of things 
that you want to do during the day, right?

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.100
It’s related information.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.800
Your five best friends.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.200
That’s information.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.800
It’s information that you’re storing somewhere.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.500
You have – you know, if I came up to you 
and asked you who your five best friends were, 

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:25.300
I’m sure you could list them off.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.900
That, in essence, is a database.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.700
It’s a collection of related information.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.000
And then Facebook’s userbase, right?

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.800
So, all of the users that Facebook 
keeps track of and stores.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.700
That’s another example of a database.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.500
So, databases can be stored in different ways.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.500
So, you could store a database on paper.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.400
If I had a shopping list or a to-do list, I might 
just scribble that down on a piece of paper.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.400
You could store a database in your mind.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.500
So, your five best friends, for example.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.500
If I came up to you and I asked you, 
