It’s Computers + Databases equals heart.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.200
Now, the point I’m trying to drive home 
with this slide is that storing a database, 

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:09.100
storing a collection of related information 
on a computer is extremely useful.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.500
And computers are actually great 
for storing databases.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.500
So, I have here two things.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.000
We have over here on the left 
Amazon vs a Shopping List.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.900
So, these are two examples of situations 
where we have a database.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.900
We kind of talked about how 
a shopping list is a database.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.200
It’s a collection of related information, right?

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.600
A collection of products that you 
want to buy from the store.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.000
Amazon.com is also a database.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.700
Amazon is storing all this product information, 
all this user information.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.700
They’re storing reviews on the products, 
the prices of the products.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.700
They’re storing all of this information.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:45.400
So, let’s take a look and we'll compare 
and contrast these two databases 

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.600
and we'll see why computers 
are the preferred medium for storing data.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.300
So, Amazon.com keeps track of products, reviews, 
purchase orders, credit cards, users, media, etc.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:59.300
So, Amazon is keeping track of so much information.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:04.200
Trillions of pieces of information 
need to be stored and readily available.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:05.600
Over here we have a shopping list.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.300
A shopping list keeps track of consumer products 
that need to be purchased, right?

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.900
We're talking 10 to 20 pieces of information 
need to be stored and readily available.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.800
So, Amazon, we need to store 
trillions of pieces of information.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.100
With the shopping list, 
we need to store 10 or 20.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.900
Both of these are databases, 
but the one we have trillions of stuff.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.100
The other one we have 10 to 20 things.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:33.000
And so, over here on Amazon, the information 
on Amazon is extremely valuable 

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.100
and it’s critical to Amazon.com’s functioning, right?

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:40.200
So, the information that Amazon is storing 
it’s database is absolutely essential.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:44.800
And another thing is that security is essential, right?

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.700
Amazon stores people’s personal information, like 
social security number or credit card, address, phone.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.300
Like that information needs to be 
locked down and secure.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:58.200
A shopping list on the other hand, the information 
is for convenience’s sake only, right?

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.800
It’s not absolutely necessary for shopping.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.700
Like if you didn’t have a shopping list, you could still 
go to the store and, for the most part, 

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.500
you’d be able to find everything that you need.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:11.600
Also, with the shopping list, 
security is just not important at all, right?

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.800
If you drop your shopping list on the ground – 
I mean if somebody else was to pick it up 

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:17.000
and look at it, it’s like not a big deal.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:17.800
It’s a shopping list.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:23.900
Finally, over here on Amazon, 
the information is stored on a computer.

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:28.400
Whereas with the shopping list, the information 
is stored maybe on a piece of paper, 

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.600
or it could even just be stored 
in someone’s memory, right?

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.400
So, maybe you just memorized your shopping list 

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.600
and that’s where you’re storing 
your shopping list database.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.100
So, what I’m trying to show you guys is that 
a database doesn’t just have to be on a computer 

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.900
and it doesn’t just have to be like in your memory 
or on a piece of paper, right?

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.600
Databases are in all of these different environments.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.900
But here’s the thing, for an application like Amazon, 
storing information on something like a computer 

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.700
makes it really easy to store 
trillions of pieces of information.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:04.000
And really easy to secure the data 
and make sure that security is taken care of.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.800
And it also makes it really easy 
to back up that information 

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.900
and duplicate that information and store 
that information on different computers.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:18.100
So, the main point of this slide is that computers are 
great at keeping track of large amounts of information.

00:09:18.100 --> 00:09:24.600
And so, going forward, we're going to be talking about 
how can we create databases on the computers 

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:28.200
because computers are so good 
at storing that information.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:34.100
So, let’s talk about how we can go about 
creating databases on a computer.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:40.200
Now, a database could be as simple 
as like a text file where you store information.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.600
Or it could be like an Excel file, right?

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:44.200
Microsoft Excel file.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.900
But generally, if you’re going to be using 
a database with an application 

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.300
or you’re going to be using a database 
to store huge amounts of information, 

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:56.000
a lot of times what people will do is 
they’ll use special software 

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.900
which is designed to help you create 
and maintain the database.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.900
This is called database management systems.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:06.700
So a database management system 
is a special software program 

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:11.000
that helps users create and maintain a database 
on a computer.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.000
So, it makes it really easy for us 
to manage large amounts of information.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.300
So, if you’re a company like Amazon 
and you have trillions of pieces of information 

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:21.900
that you need to keep track of, well, 
the database management system 

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.800
can make it pretty easy for you to store 
trillions of pieces of information, right?

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.200
It’s not like all that information 
is just in like some single text file.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.200
The database management system 
will make it really easy for you to store that.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.100
Database management systems 
can also handle security.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:41.600
So, they can make it so only certain people with the 
usernames and passwords can access the data.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:47.400
It’ll also help you to backup your data and import 
and export data from other sources.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.700
So, if you have a bunch of information 
and you want to back it up, 

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.500
a database management system can help you do that.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.400
Database management systems 
can also interact with software applications.

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:00.200
So, you know, Amazon.com is a website.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.000
And it’s interacting with the Amazon database 

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.500
which is stored mostly likely 
using a database management system.

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.900
So, you could write a program that could interact 
with the database management system.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:15.800
All right, so let’s take a look at 
this quick little diagram that I have here.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.400
So, we have Amazon over here.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.200
And this would be like Amazon.com.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.700
And Amazon is communicating 
with a database management system.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:26.900
And I don’t work for Amazon so I’m not 100% sure 
exactly how they're doing this.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.500
But for the most part, this is the general use case.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.000
So, we have our database management system 
which is this little box here.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:39.900
And the database management system is creating 
and storing and keeping track of a database.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.900
So, the database management system 
isn’t the actual database.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:46.100
The database management system 
is the software application 

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:52.800
that is creating, maintaining, updating, deleting 
information from the actual database.

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:56.200
So, Amazon.com will interact with 
the database management system 

00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:00.700
in order to create, read, update, 
and delete information.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:05.800
So, Amazon isn’t creating or reading 
or updating this information directly.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.600
Amazon is telling the database management system 
to do that for it.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.400
And by going through 
the database management system, 

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.600
we can be sure that all the data 
is getting stored correctly 

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:20.000
and there’s going to be no problems with the data.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.700
So, let’s talk about C.R.U.D.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:24.700
And this is an acronym that you’re going to hear a lot.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.100
C-R-U-D.

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:29.500
It stands for Create, Read, Update, and Delete.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:33.400
You’ll also hear people call this 
Create, Retrieve, Update, and Delete.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:39.500
Now, CRUD represents the four main operations 
that we're going to be doing with the database.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.300
So, you’re going to be creating information 
in the database.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.700
So, creating new database entries.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.700
You’re going to be reading information 
from the database.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:50.800
So, you know, retrieving or getting 
the information that you already stored in there.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.400
You’re going to be updating the existing information.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.500
And you're going to be deleting the information 
that’s already in there.

00:12:56.500 --> 00:13:02.300
And these are the core four operations that we want 
the database management system to perform for us.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:07.600
So, any good database management system 
is going to be able to do all four of these things.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:11.000
So, now that we've talked a little bit about 
database management systems, 

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:17.500
I want to talk to you guys about the two main types 
of databases that you'll find in computing.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.500
So, the first is called a relational database.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:26.000
You’ll hear people refer to these as SQL[Sequel] 
databases or S-Q-L databases.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.600
And then we also have what are called 
non-relational databases.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:36.100
And you’ll hear people refer to non-relational 
databases as no-SQL or not just SQL databases.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:40.200
We're going to talk about what SQL is in a second.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.400
But I want to show you guys these two types.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:48.100
So, a relational database which we have over here 
on the left, organizes data into one or more tables.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:54.100
So, each table has columns and rows 
and a unique key identifies each row.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:59.200
Now, relational databases are by far 
the most popular types of databases.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.200
And relational databases are going to store 
everything inside of these tables.

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:07.900
So, we'll organize all the data that we want 
to store inside of these predefined tables 

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.500
and then we can just insert information into there.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.500
A relational database is a lot like an Excel spreadsheet.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:17.000
So, if you’ve ever used a spreadsheet 
where we have columns and rows 

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:20.800
and we're storing information, 
that’s essentially what a relational database is.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.700
And then over here on the right 
we have non-relational databases.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:30.100
And a non-relational database is basically just any 
type of database that’s not a relational database.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:34.000
So, like I said, relational databases 
are by far the most popular type.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:38.800
And so, because they’re so popular, 
any other type of database 

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:43.100
that’s not technically relational is just referred 
to as non-relational.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.900
And non-relational databases, you know, 
it’s very general.

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:51.400
Because any database that’s not relational 
is getting put into this category.

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:56.200
So, non-relational database is organized database 
in anything but a traditional table.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:59.500
So, you’ll have things like key-value stores documents 

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:02.300
which would be kind of like 
JavaScript Object Notation.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:03.300
Or like XMl.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.300
They’ll store data in graphs.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.600
Or even flexible tables.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.400
So, really non-relational database 
is a very general category.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:13.900
It’s just anything that’s not relational.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:16.500
So, let’s take a look real quick at a relational database.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:20.300
Or you’ll hear people, like I said, 
refer to this as a SQL database.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.400
So, over here, we have two examples of tables.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.800
So, this would be basically how we would store 
information in a relational database.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:29.000
So, over here I have a student table.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.100
And this student table might store individual students.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.500
So, you’ll see over here we have 
an ID, a name, and a major.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:39.900
So, for every single one of my students, 
I have an entry just like this.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:43.800
And you'll notice over here, 
I’m giving each of these entries an ID.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:48.400
And that ID will uniquely identify that row in the table.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:50.500
And over here, we have a users table.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.300
So, maybe you're creating users for your application.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:57.400
So, they have a username, 
a password, and then an email, right?

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:00.200
So, the username is something 
that’s going to be unique.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:03.400
It’s something that uniquely identifies 
each row in the table.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:06.500
And then we're also storing like password and email.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:10.300
So, we kind of talked about how 
relational databases store data in tables.

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:14.100
And when we want to create a relational database, 

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:18.600
we can use a 
relational database management system.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:22.200
And a relational database management system, 
or an RDBMS 

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:27.500
is just a database management system that helps you 
create and maintain a relational database.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:34.000
And some of the most popular are my MySQL, 
Oracle, PostgreSQL, and MariaDB.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.600
There’s a bunch of these that are, 
you know, extremely popular.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:42.700
And relational database management systems use 
something called Structured Query Language, or SQL.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:50.200
And SQL is a standardized language for interacting 
with relational database management systems.

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.000
So, remember, 
a relational database management system 

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:59.300
is just a software application that we can use 
in order to create, maintain 

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.700
and do different things to our relational database.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:08.200
And SQL or Structured Query Language 
is the language that we can use 

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:11.800
to interact with those relational 
database management systems.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:17.500
So, we can use SQL to perform CRUD operations 
as well as other administrative tasks 

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:20.500
like user management, security, backup, etc.

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:25.000
And we can use SQL to define tables and structures.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.000
So, a relational database uses tables 
in order to organize its information.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.300
And we can use SQL to define those tables.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:34.700
And then insert information into those tables.

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:39.300
And SQL is a standardized language 
which means it’s pretty much used 

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.800
on every relational database management system.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:48.400
However, different relational database management 
systems will implement SQL just a little bit differently.

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:54.400
So, not all SQL code that you use on one 
relational database management system 

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.500
will port over to another one 
without slight modification.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:01.700
So, now let’s talk a little bit 
about non-relational databases.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:06.000
And in non-relational databases 
is just anything that’s not relational.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:12.000
So, a non-relational database stores data 
in anything but a, you know, a static table.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:16.700
So, I’m showing you guys a couple different 
examples of non-relational databases 

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:18.500
and how they would store data.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:20.700
So, over here we have a document database.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:25.800
And this would store information inside of like 
little objects or documents.

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.200
So, we're talking about things like JSON or XML.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:34.700
Basically, I think JSON is kind of a very popular format 
and that’s kind of what I have up here.

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.100
So, JSON is JavaScript Object Notation.

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:43.200
So, there’s a lot of non-relational databases 
that will store data in documents just like this.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:45.600
They’ll also store data in graphs.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.900
So, over here we have graphs.

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:49.300
And there’s like these little nodes.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:53.300
And then there’s, you know, connecting lines between 
the nodes which would represent like relationships.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:56.200
And then over here we have a key value hash.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:58.800
So, we would have a key 
and it gets mapped to a value.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.400
And that could really be anything.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:00.700
It could be a string.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:01.400
It could be JSON.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:03.000
It could be a graph.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.400
It could be anything like that.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:07.600
So, these are just three common examples 

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:12.700
of what SQL or non-relational databases 
might look like or how they might store their data.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:19.200
And just like with relational databases we can have 
non-relational database management systems.

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:24.200
And these help users to create 
and maintain non-relational databases.

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:30.100
So, some examples would be mongoDB, 
DynamoDB, apache cassandra, firebase.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:33.500
There’s a bunch of these popular noSQL databases 

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:36.200
that have been sprouting up 
over the last decade or so.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:38.600
And these are all implementation specific.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:43.000
So, unlike relational database management systems 
where we have SQL 

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.500
which is just a standard language 
that can interact with all of them, 

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:52.500
there isn’t a standard language for interacting with 
non-relational database management system.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:55.400
So, generally, the non-relational 
database management system 

00:19:55.400 --> 00:20:02.100
will implement their own language for performing 
CRUD and administrative operations on the database.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:04.500
So, now that we've looked at the different types 
of databases, 

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.900
I want to talk to you guys about 
some core concepts within databases.

00:20:07.900 --> 00:20:12.200
So, one of the most common things 
that you’re going to be doing are queries.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:17.400
So, a database query is a request that’s made 
to the database management system 

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.100
for specific information.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.700
So, you’ll hear the word query thrown around a lot.

00:20:21.700 --> 00:20:25.900
And generally, a query is you’re just asking 
the database management system 

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.100
to give you some information.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.200
So, as a databases structure becomes 
more and more complex, 

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:36.000
it becomes more difficult to get 
the specific pieces of information that we want.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:40.500
And if you have a very complex 
database layout or schema, 

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:45.400
then getting a specific piece of information 
can be a little bit tricky.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:48.200
And that’s why we write database queries.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:53.900
So, can write a very complex database query 
which in a lot of ways is like a program, 

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:56.500
if you’ve ever used a programming language before.

00:20:56.500 --> 00:21:00.900
And that query will then instruct the 
relational database management system 

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:06.400
to grab a specific piece or specific pieces 
of information from the database.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:11.300
So, a good way to think of a query, 
is it’s kind of like a Google search, right?

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:16.300
So, if I go on Google and I would generally 
type in the specific information that I want.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:20.600
And then Google would give me the information 
that matches that search query.

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:22.600
That’s a lot like a database query.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:26.800
Except with a database query we're 
not going to be writing the query in English 

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:28.200
like we would with Google.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.900
So, I can, you know, go up to my Google search bar 

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:34.500
and I can type something in in English or Spanish 
or whatever language you speak.

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:40.200
But with a relational database management system 
we have to write our queries using either SQL 

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:45.000
or a specific language that’s meant for that 
database management system.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:46.900
So, let’s wrap this up real quick.

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:50.100
We kind of covered a lot of 
the main points with databases.

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:52.900
So, a database is any collection 
of related information.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.100
It’s a very general term.

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:59.600
And computers are great for storing databases 
because computers are really fast.

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:01.400
They can store lots of pieces of information.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:05.100
And they can handle things like security really easily.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:09.500
And database management system 
make it easy to create, maintain, 

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:12.400
and secure a database on a computer.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:16.400
Database management systems will allow you 
to perform the CRUD operations 

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.800
and other administrative tasks on the database.

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:23.300
There’s two main types of databases, relational 
and non-relational databases.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:28.700
Relational databases use SQL and they store data 
in tables with rows and columns.

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:33.200
Non-relational databases store data 
using other data structures.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:37.200
So, things that aren’t relational database tables.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:39.500
And then we talked a little bit about querying.

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:44.700
A query is just a require that you would make 
to the database management system 

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.200
for a specific piece of information.

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:52.600
So, that is kind of the basics, 
the overall high-level basics of databases.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:55.300
And really, if you understand everything 
in this PowerPoint, 

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:58.500
then you have a good grasp of database fundamentals.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:04.200
And as we go forward, we're going to learn more and 
more things about databases like how to create them,

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:09.600
 how to store data, and how to organize our data
 so that it’s easy to retrieve when we want it.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:13.300
[Tables and Keys]

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:18.100
In this tutorial, we're going to look at some 
of the core concepts in relational databases.

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:22.600
So, we're going to talk about how we store 
information in relational databases.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:23.800
Namely, in tables.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:26.600
Let’s talk about the different things 
about those tables.

00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:28.400
We're going to talk about things called keys.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.300
We're also going to look at rows and columns.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:34.100
We'll talk about different tables can be related 
to each other 

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.700
and how we can define relationships between tables.

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:42.200
So, this will really just give you guys a full introduction 
into some of the core concepts of relational databases 

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:46.700
which you can then apply when 
creating your own relational database 

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:49.700
using a relational database management system.

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:50.900
So, let’s get started.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:53.500
Over here I have an example of a table.

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.800
And this is a student table.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:00.800
And so, the student table defines 
specific information about a student.

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:03.200
So, up here we have the column names.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:07.600
So, the student ID, the same of the student, 
and then the major.

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.900
So, I’m storing three pieces of information 
about each student, right?

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:14.700
They have their ID, their name, and their major.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:16.300
So, over here we have Jack.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:17.700
His student ID is 1.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:19.200
And he’s majoring in biology.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:20.800
Down here, we have Claire.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.200
Her student ID is 3 and she is majoring in English.

00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:27.400
So, all tables in relational databases 
are going to have two things.

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:30.100
They’re going to have columns 
and they're going to have rows.

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:33.200
Now, columns are these vertical sections right here.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:36.500
So, a column would define a single attribute, right?

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:37.700
So, we have our name column.

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:40.700
And inside the name column 
we have the names of all the students.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.100
We have the major column.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:44.800
Inside the major column we have 
the majors of all the students, right?

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:45.800
Makes sense.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.200
And then we have rows.

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:51.400
And a row is an individual entry 
in the student table.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:56.800
So, a row or a horizontal entry 
would represent a single student, right?

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:01.100
So, in a single row we're storing 
the student ID, the name, and the major.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:08.400
So, the columns represent a single attribute and 
the row represents an entry or an actual student.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:12.200
Whenever we're creating a table 
in a relational database 

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:17.100
we always want to have one very special column 
which is called the primary key.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:24.400
And a primary key is basically an attribute which 
uniquely defines the row in the database.

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:28.800
So, it’s an attribute about a specific entry 
which will uniquely define it.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.200
So, over here, we have student ID.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.900
And notice that I colored this in red 
and I also underlined it.

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:41.000
And this underline is basically going to specify 
that this column or this attribute of the student 

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.200
is going to be the primary key.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:47.700
In other words, we can use this attribute 
to uniquely identify a specific row.

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:53.600
So, over here we have Kate 
and she’s a sociology major and her ID is 1.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:57.800
So, we would say that Kate’s primary key is 1.

00:25:57.800 --> 00:26:00.500
Now, let me show you guys why primary keys 
can come in handy.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:04.800
So, down here, inside of these gold squares 
I have two entries in this database.

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:07.800
So, we have two students, both of which 
are named Jack.

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:10.400
And both of which are biology majors.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.000
Now, this isn’t that, you know, special of a case, right?

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.900
You can imagine that in a school you might have 
two students with the same name 

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:18.300
who are the same major.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:24.200
But in this case, how can we differentiate 
between this guy and this guy?

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:27.000
Well, we can use this primary key.

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:32.300
So, even though the name and the major 
of this row in the table 

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:36.900
are the same as the name and the major of this row, 
the student ID is different.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.200
The primary key is different.

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:41.200
And that’s the whole point of a primary key, 

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:45.600
is that the primary key is always 
going to be unique for each row in the table.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:50.500
So, even if all of the attributes of the row 
are the same, the primary key won’t be.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:54.700
And therefore we can differentiate 
between the two rows.

00:26:54.700 --> 00:27:00.800
So, I can differentiate between this Jack and this Jack
 by referring to their student IDs.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:03.800
And so, generally, whenever we have a table 
in a relational database, 

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:06.600
you always want to define a primary key.

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:08.400
And a primary key can be anything.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:09.200
It can be a number.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:10.900
It could be a string of text.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:13.100
It could really be anything that you want.

00:27:13.100 --> 00:27:16.700
But it has to uniquely identify the specific row.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:21.600
So, in other words, another row in this student table 
couldn't have a primary key of 2.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:24.200
So, down here I have another example 
of a database table.

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.000
This is a user table.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:30.500
So, just like that student table, 
except it’s storing information about users.

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:34.100
So, over here we have email, password, 
date created and type.

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:36.600
So, email is going to be our primary key.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:39.400
You can see I put that in red and I also underlined it.

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:44.000
And these emails are unique to each entry 
in the table, right?

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.300
So, fakemail@fake.co.

00:27:46.300 --> 00:27:49.700
No, other rows in this table could have 
that exact email.

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:51.700
And then over here we're storing the password.

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:53.300
We're also storing a date.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:57.000
So, a lot of times in databases 
you can store date values or time values.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:59.400
And then over here we're storing a type.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:03.900
And so, basically whenever you want to store data, 
what you’re going to do is define a table.

00:28:03.900 --> 00:28:07.900
So, you’re going to define, you know, 
all this information up here 

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:12.000
and then you'll just insert specific pieces 
of information into that table.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:16.600
And so, over here we have one more example 
of a database table.

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:19.800
And we're actually going to be looking 
at this example as we go forward.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:22.900
So, this is an example of an employee.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:27.100
So, imagine that we have like a company database 
and we were storing information about employees.

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:29.800
So, we have the employee ID which is the primary key.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:33.400
We're storing first name, 
last name, birthdate, sex, and salary.

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:37.000
And we're storing all of this specific information 
about an employee.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:39.900
Now, I want you guys to notice 
the primary key over here.

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:44.100
So, employee ID, this is just a, you know, 
some sort of random number 

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.500
that we’ve assigned to each employee, right?

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.500
This employee’s ID is 100.

00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:50.900
This employee ID is 101, etc.

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:56.800
And this over here, this employee ID, 
this is what we would call a surrogate key.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:01.500
And a surrogate key is basically a key that has no 
mapping to anything in the real world, right?

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:05.700
It’s essentially just, you know, like in this case a 
random number that we assign to an employee, right?

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:09.200
So this, employee Jan has an employee ID of 100.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:10.800
That doesn’t necessarily mean anything.

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:16.200
100 is just a value that we're using to represent 
Jan inside of the database.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:21.300
And so, we would refer to that as a surrogate key, 
which is just a type of primary key.

00:29:21.300 --> 00:29:24.400
Surrogate key is a key that has no mapping to 
anything in the real world.

00:29:24.400 --> 00:29:26.800
We can also have something called a natural key.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:30.100
And over here you'll see that 
I have the same exact table 

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:33.900
except instead of having employee ID, 
I have employee SSN.

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:36.300
And SSN stands for Social Security Number.

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:40.800
So, Social Security Number is a number 
that we use here in the United States 

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.600
in order to uniquely identify each citizen.

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:51.500
So, in this case we're using the Social Security Number 
in order to uniquely identify each row in the table.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:57.200
In other words, we're using the Social Security Number 
as the primary key of the table.

00:29:57.200 --> 00:30:00.800
And this is an example of 
what we would call a natural key.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:08.500
And this is a key that has a mapping or has a purpose 
in the real world, not just in the database.

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:14.000
So, a lot of times you'll hear people refer to surrogate 
keys or natural keys and that’s the difference.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.800
A surrogate key is a primary key 
that has no mapping to the real world.

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:25.100
And a natural key is a key that has a mapping to the 
real world, just like Social Security Number.

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:29.500
So, those are kind of the two different types of 
primary keys that you might see being stored.

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:33.600
Another thing I want to talk to you guys 
about are what are called foreign keys.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:39.200
And a foreign key is basically an attribute 
that we can store on a database table 

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:43.900
that will link us to another database table.

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:46.900
So, over here I have this same exact employee table.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:51.600
And then I also have this  
other attribute over here, branch_id.

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:54.000
And you notice that I colored this in green.

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:57.300
And this is what we would call a foreign key.

00:30:57.300 --> 00:31:04.900
And a foreign key stores the primary key 
of a row in another database table.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:09.400
So, here we have our employee table 
and I’m defining information about the employee.

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:14.100
But let’s say that an employee belongs 
to a specific branch in our company, right?

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:16.100
So, a company might have different branches.

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:23.600
And we can store the information about what branch 
the employee belongs to inside of a foreign key.

00:31:23.600 --> 00:31:29.200
So, the foreign key over here is actually 
a primary key inside of another table.

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:30.600
In our case, a branch table.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:32.300
So, let me show you guys that.

00:31:32.300 --> 00:31:35.900
So, down here, we have this branch table 
in our database, right?

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:38.300
So, the branch is it’s own separate table.

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:42.400
And we have – the branch has a primary key 
over here which is 2, 3, 1.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:45.600
And the branch names are Scranton, Stamford, 
and Corporate.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:49.300
So this in itself is its own table, right?

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:49.900
It’s the branch table.

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:54.800
And over here we can define which branch 
a specific employee belongs to, 

00:31:54.800 --> 00:32:00.200
by referring to the ID, the primary key of the branch.

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:07.700
So, here, Jan Levinson, her branch ID is 1, which 
means she is in the corporate branch, right?

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:10.800
Because branch ID number 1 is corporate.

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:16.800
Michael Scott, his branch ID is 2, 
which means he’s in the Scranton branch.

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:23.100
So this number is mapping this row over here 
into this other table.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:24.900
And that’s what a foreign key does.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:31.200
A foreign key is essentially just a way that we can 
define relationships between the two tables.

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:35.100
So, a foreign key is just a primary key of another table.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:37.300
So, Andy Bernard, right?

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:41.700
His branch ID is 3 which means he belongs 
to the Stamford branch.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:44.000
And so, that’s what a foreign key does.

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:49.000
A foreign key allows us to link up or define 
relationships between tables.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:56.100
I can say that Andy belongs to the Stamford branch 
and I can define that using the foreign key.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:33:02.000
And over here you'll see on the branch table, I also 
defined another foreign key which is manager ID.

00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:04.800
mgr_id, that would stand for manager ID.

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:10.500
And now this is actually going to be a foreign key 
which connects branch to the employee table.

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:16.700
So, manager ID is going to be the ID of a particular 
employee who is the manager of the branch.

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.000
So, let’s take a look at the Scranton branch.

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:20.400
So, the branch ID is 2.

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:24.000
The name of the branch is Scranton, 
and the manager ID is 101.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:25.600
So, let’s go check it out.

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:29.800
So, over here in the employee table,
 employee 101 is Michael Scott.

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:36.000
What that means is that Michael Scott 
is the manager of the Scranton branch, right?

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:40.300
We were able to define that relational 
by using these foreign keys.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:44.300
So, down here, the Stamford branch, 
the manager is 102.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:48.200
If we come up here, we can see 
employee 102 is Josh Porter.

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:51.200
So, Josh Porter is the manager 
of the Stamford branch.

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:54.200
Corporate over here, the manager ID is 108.

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:57.900
So, over here we didn’t actually include 
108 over there.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:01.500
So, obviously, this isn’t like, you know,
 a huge table of employees.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:07.000
But the manager for the corporate branch 
would be the employee with the ID of 108, 

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.600
whatever that would be inside of this table.

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:11.600
So, that’s what a foreign key does.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:16.100
A foreign key is able to help us to define relationships 
between the tables, right?

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:20.500
I can define which branch 
a specific employee belongs to 

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:26.600
by including the branch’s ID as a foreign key 
in the employee table.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:30.900
Similarly, I can define which employee 
is the manager of a branch 

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:36.700
by including that employee’s ID as a foreign key 
in the branch table.

00:34:36.700 --> 00:34:40.200
And so, that’s really why foreign keys 
are going to come in handy.

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:45.200
And it’s also important to note that a particular table 
can have more than one foreign key on it.

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:47.600
So, ever here I have my employee table, right?

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.300
And it’s the same exact thing as we have before.

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:55.100
But I also added another attribute 
or other column over here which is super_id.

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:58.200
And that stands for supervisor ID.

00:34:58.200 --> 00:35:04.400
And so, what this is going to do is it’s going to define 
who is the supervisor of a particular employee.

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:09.500
And what’s cool about this foreign key 
is it’s actually relating back to the same table.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:13.900
So, an employee can be a supervisor 
of another employee.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:15.800
So, over here we have Angela Martin, right?

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:17.800
Her employee ID is 103.

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:20.800
And her supervisor ID is 101.

00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:26.100
And that means her supervisor 
is the employee with the ID 101.

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:28.100
So, if we come over here we can look.

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:32.800
Angela Martin’s supervisor is Michael Scott because 
Michael Scott has an employee ID of 101.

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:34.400
So, Michael Scott.

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:36.800
Michael Scott’s super ID is 100.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:43.500
That means Michael Scott’s supervisor is the 
employee with ID 100, which is Jan Levison.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:47.500
So, we can these this foreign key over here 
on the employee table 

00:35:47.500 --> 00:35:50.000
to define relationships between employees.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:54.800
So, before we define the relationship between 
the employee table and the branch table, 

00:35:54.800 --> 00:36:00.100
and now we're defining a relationship between the 
employee table and the employee table, right?

00:36:00.100 --> 00:36:03.700
And so, what’s cool about this is 
then basically inside of this table, 

00:36:03.700 --> 00:36:08.800
we can tell what the different supervisor 
or supervisee relationships are, right?

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:12.400
I can tell that Josh Porter’s supervisor is Jan Levinson.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:15.300
And I can tell that Angela Martin’s supervisor 
is Michael Scott.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:19.100
Defining that information by using a foreign key.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.500
All right, so let’s take a look at another table.

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:23.400
I actually added in another table down here.

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.800
So, over here we have employee, 
just like we had before.

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:26.600
We have branch.

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:28.900
And then we also have branch supplier.

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:33.300
And branch supplier is another table 
that we can look at.

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:39.000
So, a branch supplier would basically define 
who the suppliers are for specific branches.

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.000
So, we have these branches.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:43.000
Maybe they're selling a certain product.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:44.900
Maybe they're selling like paper products.

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.700
And the branch suppliers would define 
who are the suppliers for that branch.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:54.200
And you'll notice up here, the primary key actually 
consists of two columns.

00:36:54.200 --> 00:36:58.200
And this is what we would call 
a composite key or a composite key.

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:03.900
And a composite key is basically a key 
that needs two attributes.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:07.700
So, this key is made up of two columns, right?

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:10.800
It’s made up of branch_id and supplier_name.

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:15.200
So, branch_id is actually going to refer 
to the specific branch.

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:20.300
And supplier_name is going to refer 
to the specific supplier, right?

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:24.900
So, we can say over here that Hammer Mill 
supplies paper to branch number 2.

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:28.600
So Hammer Mill supplies paper to Scranton.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:33.500
Uni-ball supplies writing utensils to 
branch ID number 2, which is Scranton.

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:38.500
Patriot Paper supplies paper to 
branch ID number 3 which is Stamford.

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:40.600
So, inside of this branch supplier table, 

00:37:40.600 --> 00:37:46.800
I’m able to define which different suppliers are 
supplying what to which different branches.

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:51.900
Now, the reason that I need this composite key 
or this composite key, 

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:56.900
is because the supplier name doesn’t 
uniquely identify each row.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:38:00.000
And the branch_id doesn’t uniquely identify each row.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.400
Only together can they 
uniquely identify each row, right?

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:07.100
So, for example, supplier name, Hammer Mill.

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:10.300
You’ll notice that Hammer Mill shows up here 
and it also shows up here.

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:12.500
So, Hammer Mill supplies to branch number 2.

00:38:12.500 --> 00:38:14.800
And they also supply to branch number 3.

00:38:14.800 --> 00:38:19.200
Uni-ball supplies to branch number 3 
and Uni-ball supplies to branch number 2.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:22.800
So, this column has repeated values, right?

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:25.500
This column has Hammer Mill repeated.

00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:26.900
It has Uni-ball repeated.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:30.100
branch_id also has those things repeated, right?

00:38:30.100 --> 00:38:32.800
So, branch_id 2 shows up here 
a bunch of times.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:35.500
branch_id 3 shows up here a bunch of times, right?

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:40.500
And so, the branch_id column 
can’t uniquely identify the table.

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:44.200
And the supplier name column 
can’t uniquely identify the table.

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:47.800
Only together can they uniquely identify it, right?

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:50.400
So, we would say Hammer Mill supplies branch 2.

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:53.100
That combination only shows up once.

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:57.900
We wouldn't put that combination again 
because we’re already defining it.

00:38:57.900 --> 00:38:59.900
So, Hammer Mill supplies branch 2.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:01.600
Hammer Mill supplies branch 3.

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:07.200
These two rows are uniquely identified by the 
identified by the supplier name and the branch ID.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:13.600
And so, over here in these employee and the branch 
table, we only find one column as the primary key.

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:18.800
But over here, we define two columns as 
the primary key, which would be a composite key.

00:39:18.800 --> 00:39:23.700
And that’s actually pretty common, where we're going 
to have two columns uniquely identifying each row.

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:26.700
All right, so down here I want 
to show you guys one more example.

00:39:26.700 --> 00:39:32.200
And this is going to show you one other way that we 
can define like different relationships with these tables.

00:39:32.200 --> 00:39:34.600
So, I actually added in two other tables.

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:38.200
We have a client table 
and we have this Works With table.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:40.300
So, let me kind of walk you guys 
through this a little bit.

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.100
We still have employee.
We still have branch.

00:39:42.100 --> 00:39:43.700
But over here, we're defining clients.

00:39:43.700 --> 00:39:46.100
And so, a client might be like a customer, right?

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:53.000
So, a client would buy paper products or, you know, 
whatever products from the branch and employee.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:54.800
So, the client has a client ID.

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:56.600
They have a client name.

00:39:56.600 --> 00:39:59.700
And then they also have a foreign key 
which is a branch ID.

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:03.900
So, a client is going to be associated 
with a specific branch, right?

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:07.600
So, we would say Lackawana County 
is associated with branch 2.

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:12.800
Which means the Lackawana County 
is going to buy products from the Scranton branch.

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:18.700
The John Daly Law, LLC is going to 
buy products from branch ID number 3.

00:40:18.700 --> 00:40:21.700
So, they're going to buy products 
from the Stamford branch, right?

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.400
So, this is a client table.

00:40:23.400 --> 00:40:27.000
And down here, we have this Works_With table.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:31.200
And what the Works_With table is doing 
is it’s defining the relationships 

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:33.700
between the employees and the clients.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:38.700
Namely, how much paper 
an employee sells to a specific client.

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:41.200
So over here we have employee ID.

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:42.400
We have client ID.

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:43.800
And we have total sales.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:48.000
So, the employee ID is going to refer 
to an employee in the employee table.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:51.500
The client ID is going to refer 
to a client in the client table.

00:40:51.500 --> 00:40:58.000
And then we're going to define how much in product 
the client has bought from the employee.

00:40:58.000 --> 00:41:01.600
So, employee 101, for example, that is Michael Scott.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:05.700
Michael Scott has sold client ID 401.

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:12.100
Michael Scott has sold Lackawana County 
$267,000 worth of product, right?

00:41:12.100 --> 00:41:13.700
How about this one, 104.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:23.100
So, Andy Bernard has sold client number 403 – 
has sold John Daly Law $5000 in paper products.

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:30.200
And so, this Works_With table is able to define how 
much an employee has sold to a client.

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:33.200
And you'll notice over here 
we have a composite key.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:36.900
And the composite key 
is employee ID and client ID.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:40.400
And this is actually a special type of composite key 

00:41:40.400 --> 00:41:45.300
because both of these columns 
are actually foreign keys.

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.700
So, employee ID is a foreign key, right?

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:49.300
It relates to the employee table.

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:51.800
Client ID is also a foreign key.

00:41:51.800 --> 00:41:53.800
It relates to the client table.

00:41:53.800 --> 00:42:00.700
And both of those foreign keys together 
makes up the primary key of the table.

00:42:00.700 --> 00:42:05.700
And that is actually a very useful way 
to define a primary key.

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:09.000
Because when we do something like this, 
we can define a relationship, right?

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:14.000
So, I can define how much product 
the client has bought from the employee.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:17.700
And that is something that can 
because were useful to keep track of.

00:42:17.700 --> 00:42:22.900
So, as you can see, like these tables can either 
be very simple or they can be very complex.

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:28.600
And the more complex your database design 
or your database schema is, 

00:42:28.600 --> 00:42:32.800
the more complex these tables 
and these keys are going to have to be, right?

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:35.700
So, this employee table has a primary key 
and two foreign keys.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.500
The client table has a primary key and a foreign key.

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:42.400
This Works_With table has a composite key.

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:43.200
You know what I mean?

00:42:43.200 --> 00:42:44.500
Both of which are foreign keys.

00:42:44.500 --> 00:42:49.800
Like it can get very complex, but we can use 
the primary keys and the foreign keys 

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.200
in order to define different relationships.

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:58.100
But really, that is just a, you know, I think a pretty 
good introduction into kind of how tables work.

00:42:58.100 --> 00:42:59.500
We talked about rows and columns.

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:01.300
We talked about primary keys.

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:04.500
We talked about surrogate keys and natural keys.

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:08.800
You know, the different types of keys 
that might map to the real world or not.

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:10.200
We talked about foreign keys.

00:43:10.200 --> 00:43:11.400
We talked about composite keys.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:16.700
And I kind of showed you guys a few different 
examples of ways that things might work.

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:19.500
So, this would be like our company database.

00:43:19.500 --> 00:43:22.300
And obviously, we would have 
a lot more information in here.

00:43:22.300 --> 00:43:26.600
But this is kind of a good example of 
how we might define different tables 

00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:30.800
and then define the relationships between 
those tables in a relational database.

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:34.900
[SQL Basics]

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:41.000
In this tutorial I’m going to walk you guys through SQL 
which stands for Structured Query Language.

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:45.200
So, SQL is a term that you're going to hear 
thrown around all the time 

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:46.800
when you're talking about databases.

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:49.000
And SQL actually is a language.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:55.700
And it’s a language that’s used for interacting with 
relational database management systems.

00:43:55.700 --> 00:44:00.000
So, SQL is – it’s similar to –
 kind of like a programming language.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:03.600
A lot of times you’ll hear people refer to SQL 
as a programming language.

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:07.900
It’s not technically a programming language 
in the tradition sense.

00:44:07.900 --> 00:44:13.500
Although, you can use SQL to provide instructions 
to a relational database management system.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.800
So, I guess if you want, you could call it 
a programming language.

00:44:16.800 --> 00:44:19.800
So, like I said, SQL, it’s a language that’s used 

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:22.200
for interacting with relational database 
management systems.

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:25.500
A relational database management system 
is a piece of software.

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:32.600
It’s a software application that you can use to create 
and maintain a relational database.

00:44:32.600 --> 00:44:35.600
You might use a relational database 
management system 

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:39.600
to create a database for an application 
that you're building.

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:42.300
And then that relational database 
management system 

00:44:42.300 --> 00:44:45.600
can make sure that the database 
is structured correctly 

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:48.800
and store everything the way 
that it needs to be stored.

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:53.900
So, in order to interact with a relational 
database management system, 

00:44:53.900 --> 00:44:55.900
we need to use a special language.

00:44:55.900 --> 00:44:58.300
So, I can’t just talk to it in English.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:01.600
I can’t just be like, “Hey, relational database 
management system, 

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:03.600
get me this piece of information.”

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:06.800
Relational database management systems 
don’t speak English.

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:09.900
But what they do speak is a language called SQL.

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:12.100
They speak Structured Query Language.

00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:17.100
And so, if we want to ask a relational database 
management system to do something for us, 

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:23.200
for example, like store a piece of information 
or create a table, update a piece of information.

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:28.800
We can ask the relational database management 
system to do that using SQL.

00:45:28.800 --> 00:45:35.400
So, SQL is the language that we can use to 
communicate with the database management system.

00:45:35.400 --> 00:45:41.100
And, you know, we can use SQL to get the relational 
database management system to do stuff for us, 

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:43.300
like create, retrieve, update, and delete data.

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:45.700
Create and manage different databases.

00:45:45.700 --> 00:45:49.000
Design and create database tables.

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:54.200
So, we can define like a database schema 
which would just be like the overall like table design.

00:45:54.200 --> 00:45:56.800
And perform administrative tasks.

00:45:56.800 --> 00:45:58.700
So, things like security.

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:02.500
We could do like user management, importing, 
exporting, backup, all that stuff.

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:09.300
So, SQL can be used to tell the relational database 
management system to do all of that stuff for us.

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:13.600
Now, here’s the thing, SQL implementation 
can vary between the systems.

00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:18.800
So, SQL, the actual language actually does 
have a formal specification.

00:46:18.800 --> 00:46:25.700
So, there is like an overall formal specification 
which defines, you know, how SQL needs to be used 

00:46:25.700 --> 00:46:27.700
and all the different commands that can be used.

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:32.400
But the problem is that there’s a bunch of these 
relational database management system.

00:46:32.400 --> 00:46:39.000
Some of the popular ones we hear about, like 
Postegres, MySQL, Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server.

00:46:39.000 --> 00:46:42.800
Like all of these relational database 
management systems 

00:46:42.800 --> 00:46:46.500
are going implement SQL just a little bit differently.

00:46:46.500 --> 00:46:52.100
So, you could write SQL code that would work on 
one relational database management system, 

00:46:52.100 --> 00:46:56.400
but then if you tried to use it on another one, 
it might not work 100%.

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:59.000
Now, for the most part, everything 
should be the same, 

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:01.400
but you might just need to tweak 
a couple little things.

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:03.900
So, one thing you want to keep in mind with SQL 

00:47:03.900 --> 00:47:09.300
is that, you know, SQL is used on all of the major 
relationship database management systems, 

00:47:09.300 --> 00:47:12.600
but it’s used slightly differently.

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:16.200
So, certain instructions might work 
on one database management system 

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:18.400
and they might not work on another, or vice versa.

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:21.900
Or you might do things just 
a little bit differently, depending.

00:47:21.900 --> 00:47:26.700
But for the most part, they’re all implementing SQL 
which means it’s all basically the same.

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:31.600
So, the concepts are the same 
but the implementation may vary just a little bit.

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:33.600
So, let’s talk a little bit more about SQL.

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:35.600
It’s actually a hybrid language.

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:39.700
So, it’s basically four types of languages in one.

00:47:39.700 --> 00:47:43.900
And you know, you don’t need to 
worry too much about all this stuff, 

00:47:43.900 --> 00:47:48.000
but you are going to hear people talking 
about these different aspects of SQL.

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:50.800
And so, I want to introduce them to you 
so you understand what they are.

00:47:50.800 --> 00:47:56.700
So, SQL, you know like I said, it’s four types of 
languages all mashed into one single language.

00:47:56.700 --> 00:48:04.600
So, SQL is a data query language, which means it can
 be used to query the database for information.

00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:10.400
So, you can write queries in SQL which tell the 
relational database management system 

00:48:10.400 --> 00:48:14.100
what pieces of information 
that you want to get from the database.

00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:19.700
And so, a data query language is used to get data 
that’s already stored in the database.

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:22.400
SQL is also a data definition language.

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:27.400
And what that means is you can use 
SQL to define database schemas.

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:31.300
Now, a schema is basically just like the 
overall layout of the database.

00:48:31.300 --> 00:48:37.100
Like what tables are going to be in the database, 
what columns those tables are going to have, 

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:39.800
and the data types that those columns 
are going to be able to store.

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:47.100
So, we can use SQL to define data in the different, 
like I said, database schemas.

00:48:47.100 --> 00:48:50.500
SQL is also a data control language which means

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:53.900
 it’s used for controlling access 
to the data in the database.

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:57.600
Basically, you can use it to configure 
like users and permissions.

00:48:57.600 --> 00:49:01.600
So, I could define a bunch of different users 
for the database.

00:49:01.600 --> 00:49:04.900
And I can say like, okay, 
this user can write to this table.

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:07.900
Or this user can read information from this table.

00:49:07.900 --> 00:49:11.500
Or this user can update 
and delete information from this table.

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:16.800
So, you can use SQL to control the access to the data.

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:20.200
And SQL is also a data manipulation language.

00:49:20.200 --> 00:49:26.500
So, it’s used for inserting, updating, 
and deleting data from the database.

00:49:26.500 --> 00:49:30.500
So, these are the four different types 
of things that you can do with SQL.

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:34.500
And you'll hear people using these terms kind of a lot.

00:49:34.500 --> 00:49:39.400
And even like database management systems 
might throw error messages or certain things.

00:49:39.400 --> 00:49:42.600
And they'll say like data query language 
or data definition language.

00:49:42.600 --> 00:49:48.100
So, it is good to just understand these 
different types of things that SQL can do 

00:49:48.100 --> 00:49:52.600
and how they're broken up into these four 
like broad types of languages.

00:49:52.600 --> 00:49:56.400
But basically, the whole point of this slide 
is that SQL can do a bunch of stuff.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.200
And it’s super powerful.

00:49:58.200 --> 00:50:03.700
And that’s one of the reasons why it’s used in 
all these relational database management systems.

00:50:03.700 --> 00:50:05.300
So, now let’s talk about queries.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:09.200
And one of the things that we're going to be 
doing a done of in this course, 

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:12.200
and you know, if you’re working with databases, 
you’re going to be doing this all the time, 

00:50:12.200 --> 00:50:13.200
which is querying.

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:17.200
So, query is a set of instructions given to the 
relational database management system 

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:24.900
generally written in SQL, that tell the RDBMS 
what information you want it to retrieve for you.

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:31.200
So, if you have a database that has like millions 
or billions of records in it, like there’s tons of data.

00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:36.600
And a lot of times that data is, you know, 
spread out across different tables, right?

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:39.200
It’s sort of hidden in this complex schema.

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:40.200
You know what I mean?

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:42.700
Like you have one piece of information 
stored over here 

00:50:42.700 --> 00:50:45.900
and then another piece over here, 
another piece over here in different tables.

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:49.500
And you want to kind of grab all that information 
and organize it in a specific way.

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:55.000
We can use queries in order to tell 
the RDBMS to do that for us.

00:50:55.000 --> 00:51:01.900
So, the goal of writing a query is that we only want 
to get the information that we need, right?

00:51:01.900 --> 00:51:04.900
Imagine that if you, you know, 
every time you wanted a piece of information 

00:51:04.900 --> 00:51:09.200
from the database you had to grab all of the 
information in the database, parse through it, 

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:11.400
and then find the information that you want.

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:12.800
We don’t have to do that.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:19.700
Instead, you can write a query which will tell the 
RDBMS exactly what piece of information you want 

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:23.300
and it’ll be able to grab just that information 
and send it back to you.

00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:26.200
So, over here I just have a little example of a query.

00:51:26.200 --> 00:51:32.000
So, this would be like maybe we're trying to get – 
this would be SELECT employee.name, employee.age.

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:36.300
So, select the employee’s name and the age 
from the employee table 

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:39.500
where the employee’s salary is greater than $30,000.

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:43.000
So, what this will do is it’ll give us every employee 
in an employee table 

00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:45.600
who makes more than $30,000, right?

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:46.800
That’s kind of like a general query.

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:50.200
Don’t worry too much about specifically 
what all this stuff means.

00:51:50.200 --> 00:51:52.300
We're going to get into that in this course.

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:54.500
But that’s kind of like what a query would look like.

00:51:54.500 --> 00:52:01.200
We're telling the RDBMS what information we want 
and then it only gives us back that information.

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:03.800
Not like all the information in the database.

00:52:03.800 --> 00:52:06.600
All right, so that is kind of an overview of SQL.

00:52:06.600 --> 00:52:08.900
I mean obviously I didn’t get into everything.

00:52:08.900 --> 00:52:11.700
We're going to look at all of these, you know, 
little specific things 

00:52:11.700 --> 00:52:14.700
that we can do with SQL as we go forward.

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:19.000
But for now, that should kind of give you 
an idea of what SQL is, why it’s useful, 

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:25.000
and you know, sort of what it’s doing for us 
and how we can use it to work with our databases.

