01:15:48.500 --> 01:15:52.700
[Creating Tables]

01:15:52.700 --> 01:15:55.600
In this tutorial I’m going to 
talk to you guys about datatypes.

01:15:55.600 --> 01:15:57.200
We're going to talk about creating tables.

01:15:57.200 --> 01:16:00.300
We're also going to talk about 
altering and deleting tables.

01:16:00.300 --> 01:16:02.800
So, in the last tutorial we set everything up.

01:16:02.800 --> 01:16:04.400
So, we got MySQL set up.

01:16:04.400 --> 01:16:06.700
We also downloaded this program PopSQL 

01:16:06.700 --> 01:16:12.300
which is basically just a text editor that we can use 
to write all of our SQL code and all that stuff.

01:16:12.300 --> 01:16:17.800
So, what I want to do in this tutorial 
is show you guys how to create tables, right?

01:16:17.800 --> 01:16:19.100
So, we created a database.

01:16:19.100 --> 01:16:22.800
In our case, we created a database 
named giraffe in the last tutorial.

01:16:22.800 --> 01:16:27.300
And now what I want to do is start populating 
that database with different tables.

01:16:27.300 --> 01:16:30.600
In other words, I want to start defining 
our database schema.

01:16:30.600 --> 01:16:34.100
Now whenever you're working with 
a relational database management system, 

01:16:34.100 --> 01:16:37.000
your first step is always to create tables.

01:16:37.000 --> 01:16:42.400
So, before we can start inserting any information, 
before we can start querying for information, 

01:16:42.400 --> 01:16:46.700
we actually have to create the physical tables 
inside of our database.

01:16:46.700 --> 01:16:49.600
And we can do that by defining the tables layout.

01:16:49.600 --> 01:16:55.600
And so, what we want to do is we basically want to 
use a SQL command which is called Create Table.

01:16:55.600 --> 01:17:00.100
And then inside of that command 
we can pass in all the different attributes 

01:17:00.100 --> 01:17:03.600
or all the different columns 
that we want our table to have.

01:17:03.600 --> 01:17:07.700
And so, the first thing I want to show you guys 
is the different types of data 

01:17:07.700 --> 01:17:10.200
that we can store inside of our database.

01:17:10.200 --> 01:17:13.100
So, over here I actually just have a little list.

01:17:13.100 --> 01:17:15.800
And I’m just going to paste it in here.

01:17:15.800 --> 01:17:19.900
So, these are all of the basic data types 
that we're going to be using in SQL.

01:17:19.900 --> 01:17:22.400
Now, these aren’t all of the datatypes.

01:17:22.400 --> 01:17:24.200
There are actually tons of datatypes.

01:17:24.200 --> 01:17:27.900
And depending on the relational database 
management system that you're using, 

01:17:27.900 --> 01:17:31.200
a lot of them have different datatypes 
to do different things.

01:17:31.200 --> 01:17:38.100
I would say that these six datatypes 
right here make up like the core SQL datatypes.

01:17:38.100 --> 01:17:41.300
Like these are probably the most 
common datatypes that you're going to see.

01:17:41.300 --> 01:17:43.600
But just keep in mind that there are a few others.

01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:45.700
Now, we're using the MySQL database.

01:17:45.700 --> 01:17:50.800
And all of these datatypes are going to be able to be 
used in the MySQL database management system.

01:17:50.800 --> 01:17:54.600
And all of these are going to be used, for the most 
part, in any relational database management system.

01:17:54.600 --> 01:18:02.200
But like I said, specific database management systems 
will allow you to use different datatypes 

01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:04.500
depending on how they want to implement things.

01:18:04.500 --> 01:18:08.600
So, let’s go through these different datatypes 
and I’ll kind of talk you guys through how to use them.

01:18:08.600 --> 01:18:09.800
So, INT.

01:18:09.800 --> 01:18:11.900
This is actually going to refer to an integer.

01:18:11.900 --> 01:18:16.600
So, anytime you see INT just like that, I-N-T, 
that basically means any whole number, right?

01:18:16.600 --> 01:18:18.500
So, this is any sort of whole number.

01:18:18.500 --> 01:18:20.500
But it can’t have decimal places after it.

01:18:20.500 --> 01:18:23.400
If you want to be able to store decimals, 
you can use this decimal datatype.

01:18:23.400 --> 01:18:26.700
And the decimal datatype 
will allow you to store decimals.

01:18:26.700 --> 01:18:30.200
And you'll see, we have 
this parenthesis here after decimal.

01:18:30.200 --> 01:18:32.500
And I have M and N in here.

01:18:32.500 --> 01:18:35.200
Now these are actually both going to 
end up being numbers.

01:18:35.200 --> 01:18:39.900
So, M is going to be the total number of digits 
that you want to store for this number.

01:18:39.900 --> 01:18:44.100
And N is going to be the number of digits 
that you want to store after the decimal point.

01:18:44.100 --> 01:18:50.400
So, when we're working with databases you have to 
be very specific about the different information.

01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:54.000
And specifically, the amount of digits 
that you want to store for a number.

01:18:54.000 --> 01:18:58.400
So, what we could do is I could say like 10 here 
and I could put a 4 over here.

01:18:58.400 --> 01:19:02.400
And what this means is we want to store
 a decimal number with 10 total digits 

01:19:02.400 --> 01:19:06.100
and 4 of those digits coming after the decimal place.

01:19:06.100 --> 01:19:12.300
So, you can specify, you know, depending on how 
accurate you want these numbers to be stored, 

01:19:12.300 --> 01:19:13.900
you can modify those numbers.

01:19:13.900 --> 01:19:15.400
Down here we have VARCHAR.

01:19:15.400 --> 01:19:19.100
And this actually stands for Variable Char 
or Variable Character.

01:19:19.100 --> 01:19:22.000
This is basically a way that we can store 
a string of text.

01:19:22.000 --> 01:19:25.700
So, VARCHAR, we have these parenthesis after here.

01:19:25.700 --> 01:19:26.900
And you can put a number in here.

01:19:26.900 --> 01:19:32.300
So, if I put a 1 in here, then this is going to 
store a string of text, length 1.

01:19:32.300 --> 01:19:38.300
If I put (100) in here, this is going to 
store a string of text with 100 characters.

01:19:38.300 --> 01:19:40.900
So, that means that the maximum amount 
of characters 

01:19:40.900 --> 01:19:45.200
that you can store inside of a VARCHAR(100) 
would be 100.

01:19:45.200 --> 01:19:49.600
Down here we have BLOB 
which stands for Binary Large Object.

01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:55.100
And this is basically just a structure 
that can store large amounts of binary data.

01:19:55.100 --> 01:19:58.900
So, if you have – a lot of people will use these 
for like images or files.

01:19:58.900 --> 01:20:01.800
Like you can store those inside of a BLOB.

01:20:01.800 --> 01:20:04.900
And they'll be able to be stored in the database.

01:20:04.900 --> 01:20:06.100
We also have DATE.

01:20:06.100 --> 01:20:08.400
So, DATE will be like a specific date and time.

01:20:08.400 --> 01:20:13.700
And you can see we format a date like YYYY-MM-DD.

01:20:13.700 --> 01:20:17.200
So, this would be the year, the 2-digit month, 
and then the 2-digit day.

01:20:17.200 --> 01:20:20.800
You can also have a TIMESTAMP, 
which is similar to date, 

01:20:20.800 --> 01:20:23.800
but it’s generally used for recording 
like when things happen.

01:20:23.800 --> 01:20:28.600
So, you can record like when an item got inserted 
into the database or something like that.

01:20:28.600 --> 01:20:36.000
So, over here it’s just YYYY-MM-DD 
and then we have the hours, minutes, and the seconds.

01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:39.700
So, those are all, like I said, the main datatypes 
that you're going to be using.

01:20:39.700 --> 01:20:41.000
But these aren’t all the datatypes.

01:20:41.000 --> 01:20:43.600
So, depending on the database management system 
that you're using, 

01:20:43.600 --> 01:20:47.700
you want to check to see 
what specific datatypes they offer.

01:20:47.700 --> 01:20:50.800
But like I said, for the most part, 
these should work in just about any system.

01:20:50.800 --> 01:20:54.100
So, now I want to talk to you guys 
about creating database tables.

01:20:54.100 --> 01:20:57.000
So, what we can actually do is we can create tables 

01:20:57.000 --> 01:21:02.000
and we're going to have use those datatypes to tell 
the relational database management system 

01:21:02.000 --> 01:21:05.500
what types of data we want to store 
in each column in the table.

01:21:05.500 --> 01:21:09.800
So, in order to create a table, 
we're actually going to type out some SQL.

01:21:09.800 --> 01:21:13.500
Now, I’m just going to type out CREATE TABLE.

01:21:13.500 --> 01:21:16.600
And these are two SQL reserve words.

01:21:16.600 --> 01:21:18.500
CREATE and TABLE.

01:21:18.500 --> 01:21:21.600
And you'll notice that I typed them 
in all capital letters.

01:21:21.600 --> 01:21:26.200
Now, this is actually a pretty common convention 
when writing SQL queries.

01:21:26.200 --> 01:21:31.100
A lot of people will write these reserve words 
in SQL in all capitals.

01:21:31.100 --> 01:21:32.100
But you don’t have to.

01:21:32.100 --> 01:21:35.200
If I wanted, I could write create table just like that.

01:21:35.200 --> 01:21:37.500
And you’ll see it’s getting highlighted the same way.

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:40.300
The reason that people write these in all caps is 

01:21:40.300 --> 01:21:46.700
because then it’s easy to distinguish the SQL 
from any other text that we might be writing.

01:21:46.700 --> 01:21:51.900
So, I would say for the most part, just stick with 
making these all uppercase and you should be fine.

01:21:51.900 --> 01:21:53.300
So, I’m going to say CREATE TABLE.

01:21:53.300 --> 01:21:56.000
And then I want to type in the name of the table 
that I want to create.

01:21:56.000 --> 01:21:59.500
So, what we're going to do is we're going to 
actually create a database table.

01:21:59.500 --> 01:22:01.800
So, I actually have a database table set up over here.

01:22:01.800 --> 01:22:04.100
You'll see it’s a student table.

01:22:04.100 --> 01:22:09.600
And it’s storing just a list of students 
for like maybe in a college or university.

01:22:09.600 --> 01:22:12.500
So, there’s three things that we're storing 
about this student.

01:22:12.500 --> 01:22:15.600
The student’s ID, the student’s name, 
and the student’s major.

01:22:15.600 --> 01:22:19.300
So, we're storing all of this information 
about this student.

01:22:19.300 --> 01:22:25.300
So, what I can do now is I can actually create 
this exact table inside of my database.

01:22:25.300 --> 01:22:26.500
So, I can say CREATE TABLE.

01:22:26.500 --> 01:22:28.000
We'll call it student.

01:22:28.000 --> 01:22:30.900
And you'll notice that I made this lowercase.

01:22:30.900 --> 01:22:35.000
So, this isn’t an SQL reserve word, 
so I’m not going to make it uppercase.

01:22:35.000 --> 01:22:39.500
And then what I can do is I can just make an open 
and closes parenthesis and a semicolon.

01:22:39.500 --> 01:22:44.300
Now, any command that you write in SQL 
is always going to end with a semicolon.

01:22:44.300 --> 01:22:48.200
And if you're just starting out, 
you might forget to put that semicolon in there, 

01:22:48.200 --> 01:22:51.100
but you want to make sure that you always 
put it in there otherwise it’s not valid SQL.

01:22:51.100 --> 01:22:52.500
So, I’m just going to click Enter.

01:22:52.500 --> 01:22:54.800
And I’m going to down here 
in-between these parenthesis.

01:22:54.800 --> 01:22:58.700
And what I’m going to is I’m going to start defining 
the different columns 

01:22:58.700 --> 01:23:01.400
or the different attributes on this table.

01:23:01.400 --> 01:23:06.300
So, what we can do is we can define a column name 
and then a column datatype.

01:23:06.300 --> 01:23:10.500
So, I can come over here 
and I can say the first attribute.

01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:14.600
So, in my case, the first attribute for this table 
is going to be student ID.

01:23:14.600 --> 01:23:18.100
And you'll also notice that student ID is a primary key.

01:23:18.100 --> 01:23:21.600
So, on this table, student ID is the primary key.

01:23:21.600 --> 01:23:26.800
Meaning it’s the column on the table 
which will uniquely identify the specific row.

01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:29.500
So, I'm just going to call this student_id.

01:23:29.500 --> 01:23:32.500
And now I need to give this a datatype.

01:23:32.500 --> 01:23:38.500
So, I’m going to give this a datatype of INT because all 
of these ID’s as you can see, are integers, right?

01:23:38.500 --> 01:23:40.000
They’re just integer numbers.

01:23:40.000 --> 01:23:46.600
Now because this is going to be the primary key 
for this table, I need to denote that.

01:23:46.600 --> 01:23:50.200
So, over here I can just say PRIMARY KEY, 
just like that.

01:23:50.200 --> 01:23:54.700
And what that’s going to do is it’s going to tell MySQL 
that this is going to be the primary key for the table.

01:23:54.700 --> 01:23:57.100
The next thing we want to store is the student’s name.

01:23:57.100 --> 01:23:59.000
So, I’m just going to say name.

01:23:59.000 --> 01:24:00.800
And the name is actually going to be a string.

01:24:00.800 --> 01:24:04.300
So, this could be like Jack or Tommy or Kara, right?

01:24:04.300 --> 01:24:07.000
It’s a name that we're storing about this table.

01:24:07.000 --> 01:24:10.100
So, over here I’m going to make this a VARCHAR.

01:24:10.100 --> 01:24:12.700
And then I’m going to make an open 
and closed parenthesis.

01:24:12.700 --> 01:24:16.500
Now, remember, with the VARCHAR datatype 
we have to tell MySQL 

01:24:16.500 --> 01:24:19.400
how many characters we want this to be able to store.

01:24:19.400 --> 01:24:22.200
So, with someone’s name, what you want to do 

01:24:22.200 --> 01:24:25.700
is you basically just want to think like how many 
characters do we really want to allocate 

01:24:25.700 --> 01:24:28.200
to storing someone’s name?

01:24:28.200 --> 01:24:33.600
Because here’s the thing, if I allocated like 1000 
characters for someone’s name, 

01:24:33.600 --> 01:24:38.100
will in reality, normal people don’t have a name 
with 1000 characters, right?

01:24:38.100 --> 01:24:42.700
I mean maybe your name would be 20 characters 
or 30 if you're really pushing it, 

01:24:42.700 --> 01:24:46.200
but 1000 is just totally out of the bounds of reality.

01:24:46.200 --> 01:24:49.300
And you have to think if you're storing 
like millions of names, 

01:24:49.300 --> 01:24:55.200
potentially, you know, allocating 1000 characters 
to each name when you only need 20 

01:24:55.200 --> 01:24:57.700
is going to take up a lot of extra space 
in your database.

01:24:57.700 --> 01:25:00.400
So, I think what we should do is maybe say like 20.

01:25:00.400 --> 01:25:05.400
So, let’s say that the maximum length of someone’s 
name we want to store is going to be 20 characters.

01:25:05.400 --> 01:25:09.900
And really, you know, depending on the domain 
of the application that you're building, 

01:25:09.900 --> 01:25:11.500
that’s going to be different.

01:25:11.500 --> 01:25:13.000
But in my case, let’s just say it’s 20.

01:25:13.000 --> 01:25:15.700
And then finally we wanted to store 
the student’s major.

01:25:15.700 --> 01:25:17.100
So, I can say over here, major.

01:25:17.100 --> 01:25:20.000
And this is also going to be a VARCHAR.

01:25:20.000 --> 01:25:23.500
So, why don’t we also allocate 20 characters 
to their major.

01:25:23.500 --> 01:25:24.900
And that should be enough.

01:25:24.900 --> 01:25:29.100
So, you'll notice that I’m defining each of the 
attributes, each of the columns on the table.

01:25:29.100 --> 01:25:30.900
And then I’m putting a comma.

01:25:30.900 --> 01:25:33.200
And then I’m defining the next column.

01:25:33.200 --> 01:25:35.300
And then I’m putting a comma.

01:25:35.300 --> 01:25:36.500
And then the final column.

01:25:36.500 --> 01:25:39.000
So, this right here, this CREATE TABLE statement is 

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:42.300
basically going to create 
this table over here for us, right?

01:25:42.300 --> 01:25:44.100
We have the primary key, which is an INT.

01:25:44.100 --> 01:25:47.400
And we have a name and a major which are strings.

01:25:47.400 --> 01:25:49.900
So, that’s going to go ahead and do that for us.

01:25:49.900 --> 01:25:54.800
Now, from inside PopSQL, what’s really cool 
is we can just click on this query right here.

01:25:54.800 --> 01:25:56.700
Click on this SQL statement.

01:25:56.700 --> 01:25:59.000
And I can come over here and click Run.

01:25:59.000 --> 01:26:03.800
And what this will do is it’ll automatically 
run this SQL on our MySQL server.

01:26:03.800 --> 01:26:05.100
So, I'm going to click Run.

01:26:05.100 --> 01:26:09.800
And you'll see over here we get a success message 
that says Rows affected:  0.

01:26:09.800 --> 01:26:15.600
So, that actually was able to create 
that new table in our database.

01:26:15.600 --> 01:26:18.900
So, that is basically how we could create a table.

01:26:18.900 --> 01:26:21.200
And now we have this table stored in our database.

01:26:21.200 --> 01:26:22.800
I want to show you guys another thing we can do.

01:26:22.800 --> 01:26:25.700
So, over here I’m defining the primary key.

01:26:25.700 --> 01:26:28.400
So, student_id was the primary key, right?

01:26:28.400 --> 01:26:31.900
It’s the one column that’s going to uniquely 
identify each row.

01:26:31.900 --> 01:26:36.800
And if you want, you can put primary key over here 
after like student_id.

01:26:36.800 --> 01:26:41.200
Or what you can do is you can get rid of this 
and you can define this down below.

01:26:41.200 --> 01:26:45.100
So, instead of defining the primary key up here 
next to student_id, 

01:26:45.100 --> 01:26:47.100
I can come down here and I could change it.

01:26:47.100 --> 01:26:52.500
So, I could say now PRIMARY KEY, 
and then I can make an open and closed parenthesis.

01:26:52.500 --> 01:26:55.800
And in here, I could put the name of the column 
that I want to be the primary key.

01:26:55.800 --> 01:26:57.900
So, in my case, I’ll just say student_id.

01:26:57.900 --> 01:27:02.400
So, now what this is system is that the primary key 
is going to be student_id.

01:27:02.400 --> 01:27:04.900
So, this is a equivalent to what I was doing up here.

01:27:04.900 --> 01:27:08.200
It’s just another way that you can do it.

01:27:08.200 --> 01:27:12.500
And so, now we already have our table created, 
so I’m not going to go ahead and create it again.

01:27:12.500 --> 01:27:14.800
All right, so now that we have 
our student table created, 

01:27:14.800 --> 01:27:16.800
let’s go ahead and make sure they created correctly.

01:27:16.800 --> 01:27:19.100
So, what I’m going to do 
is I’m going to come down here 

01:27:19.100 --> 01:27:21.000
and I’m going to type out another MySQL command.

01:27:21.000 --> 01:27:23.200
So, I’m just going to type out DESCRIBE.

01:27:23.200 --> 01:27:25.100
And then I’m going to type in the name of the table.

01:27:25.100 --> 01:27:27.100
So, in our case it’s going to be student.

01:27:27.100 --> 01:27:33.500
And you'll notice with PopSQL, if I click over here on 
this query, it gets highlighted in that blue color.

01:27:33.500 --> 01:27:36.000
And if I click down here on this query, 
it gets highlighted.

01:27:36.000 --> 01:27:39.500
So, what you can do with PopSQL 
is you can have like a query up here.

01:27:39.500 --> 01:27:43.800
And you can run that query or you can click 
on this query down here and run it.

01:27:43.800 --> 01:27:45.300
So, I’m going to click on DESCRIBE student.

01:27:45.300 --> 01:27:46.500
And then I’m going click Run.

01:27:46.500 --> 01:27:49.400
So, you'll see that it’s actually describing 
the table that we just created.

01:27:49.400 --> 01:27:51.200
So, there’s a list of all these different fields.

01:27:51.200 --> 01:27:53.300
We have student_id, name, and major.

01:27:53.300 --> 01:27:55.600
It’s telling us the datatypes that we're storing.

01:27:55.600 --> 01:27:57.600
So, like varchar(20).

01:27:57.600 --> 01:28:01.800
And then it’s telling us a bunch of other information which we're going to get into later.

01:28:01.800 --> 01:28:05.500
So, what this is, it basically described 
everything about this table.

01:28:05.500 --> 01:28:09.600
So, now what I want to show you guys 
how to do is delete and modify a table.

01:28:09.600 --> 01:28:13.500
So, now that we've created a table 
and we saw that it got created correctly, 

01:28:13.500 --> 01:28:15.600
if you wanted, you could delete the table.

01:28:15.600 --> 01:28:19.300
So, what I could do is I can come down here 
and type out DROP TABLE 

01:28:19.300 --> 01:28:20.900
and I can just type the name of the table.

01:28:20.900 --> 01:28:23.400
So, in our case, it’s the student table, and a semicolon.

01:28:23.400 --> 01:28:25.700
And now I’m going to click on here and I’ll click run.

01:28:25.700 --> 01:28:28.400
And what this is going to do 
is it’s going to drop that table.

01:28:28.400 --> 01:28:31.300
So, now if I came up here 
and I click on describe student, 

01:28:31.300 --> 01:28:33.700
in other words, I’m saying 
I want to execute this command.

01:28:33.700 --> 01:28:36.800
And I click run, you'll see that it says 
“NO_SUCH_TABLE”.

01:28:36.800 --> 01:28:42.600
Table ‘giraffe.student’ doesn’t exist 
because remember, we just dropped it.

01:28:42.600 --> 01:28:45.700
So, what we can do, is we can actually create again.

01:28:45.700 --> 01:28:48.600
So, I’m going to click on this CREATE TABLE query 
and I’ll click Run.

01:28:48.600 --> 01:28:50.600
And that will go ahead and create it.

01:28:50.600 --> 01:28:55.000
So, now if we click DESCRIBE student, you’ll see 
we get the table back because we created it again.

01:28:55.000 --> 01:28:56.400
So, you can drop the table.

01:28:56.400 --> 01:28:57.700
You can also modify the table.

01:28:57.700 --> 01:29:02.200
So, let’s say that after the table was created, 
you wanted to give it another column.

01:29:02.200 --> 01:29:05.300
What I could do is I could add another column.

01:29:05.300 --> 01:29:07.400
So, I could say ALTER TABLE.

01:29:07.400 --> 01:29:11.800
And then say student, 
because that’s the name of the table.

01:29:11.800 --> 01:29:13.000
And then I could say ADD.

01:29:13.000 --> 01:29:16.100
So, what this is going to do 
is it’s going to add an extra column onto the table.

01:29:16.100 --> 01:29:17.900
So, why don’t we add a column for GPA.

01:29:17.900 --> 01:29:22.300
So we could store a student’s GPA 
and that’s going to be a decimal.

01:29:22.300 --> 01:29:24.900
So, I’m just going to click a semicolon.

01:29:24.900 --> 01:29:28.700
And this is going to go ahead 
and end off this command.

01:29:28.700 --> 01:29:34.000
Now, with decimal, remember I showed you 
that you could put numbers after here, like 1 and 2.

01:29:34.000 --> 01:29:39.800
So, what we're going to do is generally a GPA 
would be like 3. something something.

01:29:39.800 --> 01:29:41.000
Or 4. something something.

01:29:41.000 --> 01:29:47.000
So, we're going to have this be three total digits with 
two of the digits occurring after the decimal point.

01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:51.800
So, now I can go ahead and run this command 
and you'll see it says success down here.

01:29:51.800 --> 01:29:54.700
So, if we were to describe the student table again, 

01:29:54.700 --> 01:30:00.600
if I just click on this and click Run, now you'll see 
we have that extra column in here, GPA.

01:30:00.600 --> 01:30:02.700
And it’s a decimal (3,2).

01:30:02.700 --> 01:30:08.900
So, that is how we can drop a table 
and that’s also how we can alter a specific column.

01:30:08.900 --> 01:30:11.600
If you wanted, you could also drop a specific column.

01:30:11.600 --> 01:30:14.200
So, I could say ALTER TABLE student.

01:30:14.200 --> 01:30:20.000
And over here, I can just say DROP COLUMN 
and just type in the name of the column.

01:30:20.000 --> 01:30:23.100
So, what this will do is it’ll drop that GPA column 
from the table.

01:30:23.100 --> 01:30:24.800
So, let’s go ahead and run.

01:30:24.800 --> 01:30:25.800
We get this success message.

01:30:25.800 --> 01:30:32.000
Now, if I describe the table and I clicked run, 
you'll see that the GPA field went away.

01:30:32.000 --> 01:30:33.400
So, I actually dropped that field.

01:30:33.400 --> 01:30:34.900
So, you can create tables.

01:30:34.900 --> 01:30:35.900
We can add columns onto tables.

01:30:35.900 --> 01:30:38.400
We can remove columns from tables.

01:30:38.400 --> 01:30:40.600
And then we can just remove the table altogether.

01:30:40.600 --> 01:30:46.000
So, hopefully that show you guys some of the basics 
of doing those operations on tables.

01:30:46.000 --> 01:30:48.300
Now, obviously whenever 
you're creating your database, 

01:30:48.300 --> 01:30:51.200
the first thing that you want to do 
is define your database schema.

01:30:51.200 --> 01:30:54.000
In other words, you want to create 
all the different tables 

01:30:54.000 --> 01:30:57.800
and then you can start inserting data 
into the tables as such.

01:30:57.800 --> 01:31:01.900
So, in the next tutorial I'm going to talk to you guys 
about inserting data into tables, 

01:31:01.900 --> 01:31:04.600
so we'll actually insert some student information 
into our student table.

01:31:04.600 --> 01:31:09.100
[Inserting Data]

01:31:09.100 --> 01:31:13.200
In this tutorial, I’m going to talk to you guys 
about inserting data into a database tables.

01:31:13.200 --> 01:31:17.600
So, in the last tutorial we set up our first table 
which is this student table.

01:31:17.600 --> 01:31:18.800
And you can see it up here.

01:31:18.800 --> 01:31:21.900
And then down here we described the student table.

01:31:21.900 --> 01:31:24.800
So, it has student_id, name, major, etc.

01:31:24.800 --> 01:31:30.100
So, now that we have our table set up, we want to 
actually start inputting information, right?

01:31:30.100 --> 01:31:32.100
We want to start storing some different data.

01:31:32.100 --> 01:31:33.600
So, I’m going to show you guys how to do that.

01:31:33.600 --> 01:31:35.900
I’m going to leave this 
CREATE TABLE statement up here 

01:31:35.900 --> 01:31:37.900
because we're actually going to 
come back to that in a sec.

01:31:37.900 --> 01:31:40.800
But down here, I’m going to 
show you guys how to insert values.

01:31:40.800 --> 01:31:46.400
So, in order to insert a piece of information into 
a table, we just want to type out INSERT INTO.

01:31:46.400 --> 01:31:48.700
And now I want to type out the name of the table.

01:31:48.700 --> 01:31:51.000
So, in our case, we have this student table.

01:31:51.000 --> 01:31:54.100
And then I just want to type out VALUES.

01:31:54.100 --> 01:31:58.600
So, basically, we're saying insert 
into the student table the values.

01:31:58.600 --> 01:32:02.600
And then over here I’m going to make an open 
and closed parenthesis and a semicolon.

01:32:02.600 --> 01:32:05.300
So, now this is actually a full statement.

01:32:05.300 --> 01:32:07.800
So, remember, we need to 
end this off with a semicolon.

01:32:07.800 --> 01:32:12.900
Inside of these parenthesis I can actually 
put the information that I want to store.

01:32:12.900 --> 01:32:20.800
So, the way that the table is set up, we have the 
student_id first, then the name, then the major.

01:32:20.800 --> 01:32:24.500
And that's the order that we want to 
put this information into the table.

01:32:24.500 --> 01:32:28.800
So, I need to put this student_id first, 
the name, and then the major.

01:32:28.800 --> 01:32:31.700
So, over here I’m just going to type in the student ID.

01:32:31.700 --> 01:32:35.800
So, we're going to be actually entering in the same 
information that we have over here.

01:32:35.800 --> 01:32:38.100
So, this is kind of like our little template.

01:32:38.100 --> 01:32:42.800
So, we have the first student, Jack, 
who’s a biology major and his student ID is 1.

01:32:42.800 --> 01:32:44.900
And you can see we just keep going from there.

01:32:44.900 --> 01:32:47.400
So, I’m going to insert, you know, 
roughly the same information.

01:32:47.400 --> 01:32:51.700
So, the first thing we want to do is put the student ID, 
so that’s going to be 1.

01:32:51.700 --> 01:32:55.400
And you'll notice that with an integer 
I can just type out the number 1.

01:32:55.400 --> 01:32:58.300
And then over here we're going 
to put in a VARCHAR(20).

01:32:58.300 --> 01:33:00.800
So, basically this is going to be a string.

01:33:00.800 --> 01:33:05.700
Anytime we're typing out strings in SQL, 
we're going to make these double quotation marks.

01:33:05.700 --> 01:33:08.000
And then in here we can type out the string.

01:33:08.000 --> 01:33:10.300
So, the student’s name is going to be Jack.

01:33:10.300 --> 01:33:15.600
And then another comma 
and we're going to put in his major which is biology.

01:33:15.600 --> 01:33:18.500
So, we have Jack with the student ID of 1.

01:33:18.500 --> 01:33:19.900
And his major is Biology.

01:33:19.900 --> 01:33:24.500
So, insert into student values 1, Jack, Biology, 

01:33:24.500 --> 01:33:29.000
this is going to go ahead 
and insert this value into the database table.

01:33:29.000 --> 01:33:30.700
So, now when I click Run – 

01:33:30.700 --> 01:33:34.400
and you'll notice all I have to do is 
just click on this query and then click Run.

01:33:34.400 --> 01:33:36.900
It says down here Success.

01:33:36.900 --> 01:33:38.300
Rows affected 1.

01:33:38.300 --> 01:33:41.600
So, we affected one row in the student table.

01:33:41.600 --> 01:33:46.600
So, now what we can do is we can actually see 
how this got inserted into the table.

01:33:46.600 --> 01:33:54.400
So, what I’m going to do is actually below here, 
I’m just going to type out select asterisk from student.

01:33:54.400 --> 01:33:57.900
And this is actually a very simple SQL command.

01:33:57.900 --> 01:34:01.000
What this is going to do is it’s going to 
grab all the information from the student table.

01:34:01.000 --> 01:34:05.500
And we're actually going to spend 
a whole tutorial just talking about using SELECT.

01:34:05.500 --> 01:34:08.900
But for now, just know 
you can type SELECT * FROM student.

01:34:08.900 --> 01:34:12.200
And this is going to go ahead and give us 
all the information from the student table.

01:34:12.200 --> 01:34:14.800
So, I’m just going to click on this query here 
and I’ll click Run.

01:34:14.800 --> 01:34:18.500
And you'll see down here we get this table.

01:34:18.500 --> 01:34:20.400
So, it says student_id 1.

01:34:20.400 --> 01:34:21.200
Name, Jack.

01:34:21.200 --> 01:34:22.200
Major, Biology.

01:34:22.200 --> 01:34:29.200
So, so far in the student table, we have 1 student 
which is Jack and he’s a biology major.

01:34:29.200 --> 01:34:31.100
So, why don’t we actually insert another student.

01:34:31.100 --> 01:34:35.200
So, I’m  going to keep this same line of code except 
now I’m going to insert a student with ID 2.

01:34:35.200 --> 01:34:38.300
And this student’s name is going to be Kate.

01:34:38.300 --> 01:34:42.800
And she is going to be a sociology major.

01:34:42.800 --> 01:34:45.900
So, we have Kate who’s ID is 2, sociology.

01:34:45.900 --> 01:34:48.800
So, if I click on this command here and I click Run, 

01:34:48.800 --> 01:34:52.300
this is going to go ahead and insert Kate 
into the database.

01:34:52.300 --> 01:34:57.500
So, now I’m going to click on Select All 
from students, or Select * from students.

01:34:57.500 --> 01:34:58.700
And we'll click Run.

01:34:58.700 --> 01:35:01.900
And now you can see we're getting 
all the information from the student table.

01:35:01.900 --> 01:35:04.400
So, we have Jack and then we also have Kate.

01:35:04.400 --> 01:35:09.300
So, we added in – so far, 
we've inserted in 2 people into there.

01:35:09.300 --> 01:35:13.000
So, what you can do is you can basically 
use this same format 

01:35:13.000 --> 01:35:15.500
in order to keep inserting students 
into the student table, right?

01:35:15.500 --> 01:35:20.900
As long as you have their ID and you have their name 
and their major, then everything is good.

01:35:20.900 --> 01:35:23.500
But I want to show you guys another thing we can do.

01:35:23.500 --> 01:35:27.200
So, let’s say there’s a situation where 
we had a student who didn’t have a major.

01:35:27.200 --> 01:35:31.800
Maybe they just had no major or we didn’t know 
what their major was 

01:35:31.800 --> 01:35:33.600
so we couldn't insert it into the database.

01:35:33.600 --> 01:35:37.600
Well, in a situation like that we could 
actually modify this statement a little bit.

01:35:37.600 --> 01:35:41.900
So, I could say INSERT INTO student 
and then after student here 

01:35:41.900 --> 01:35:44.200
I can make an open and closed parenthesis.

01:35:44.200 --> 01:35:48.900
And I can type out the names of the columns 
or the names of the attributes that I have 

01:35:48.900 --> 01:35:50.600
or that I want to insert.

01:35:50.600 --> 01:35:54.700
So, I can say like student_id and then name.

01:35:54.700 --> 01:35:57.200
So, you'll notice that I’m not including major in here.

01:35:57.200 --> 01:36:00.700
And what that means is that over here 
in this value section, 

01:36:00.700 --> 01:36:04.000
we can include the student ID and the name.

01:36:04.000 --> 01:36:06.000
But we don’t have to include the major.

01:36:06.000 --> 01:36:10.800
So, like if I don’t know what the major is, 
like I can’t obviously put anything there.

01:36:10.800 --> 01:36:16.500
So, by saying student open and closed parenthesis 
and then specifying the 2 attributes that I do have, 

01:36:16.500 --> 01:36:20.700
I can then just put them over here 
and I won’t get an error by not including it.

01:36:20.700 --> 01:36:22.700
So, now we can change these values.

01:36:22.700 --> 01:36:24.000
So, we'll change the primary key to three.

01:36:24.000 --> 01:36:27.300
And then let’s see who the next student 
in our database is.

01:36:27.300 --> 01:36:28.300
So, it looks like it’s Claire.

01:36:28.300 --> 01:36:31.000
So, we'll have Claire.

01:36:31.000 --> 01:36:33.700
And then we can go ahead and run this query.

01:36:33.700 --> 01:36:35.500
And you'll see rows affected, 1.

01:36:35.500 --> 01:36:37.200
So, we added in another row.

01:36:37.200 --> 01:36:41.500
But if I was to select all this data – so I’m going to click 
Select again and I’m just going to click Run.

01:36:41.500 --> 01:36:44.800
Now you'll see that Claire’s student ID is 3.

01:36:44.800 --> 01:36:47.000
Her name is Claire, but here major is NULL.

01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:50.300
So, we didn’t actually enter in a major for Claire.

01:36:50.300 --> 01:36:55.500
And therefore, inside the major field, 
she’s getting a value of NULL.

01:36:55.500 --> 01:36:57.100
And that’s actually pretty common.

01:36:57.100 --> 01:37:01.500
So, that’s basically going to be what happens 
when we don’t enter in something.

01:37:01.500 --> 01:37:07.000
And so, what you can do is you can specify what 
pieces of information you want to insert into the table 

01:37:07.000 --> 01:37:08.700
by specifying them over here.

01:37:08.700 --> 01:37:13.400
And then here in the value section, 
you just have to add those pieces of information.

01:37:13.400 --> 01:37:17.100
So, that’s two ways that you can insert something 
into a database table.

01:37:17.100 --> 01:37:21.100
And it’s important to notice 
that you can’t insert duplicate entries.

01:37:21.100 --> 01:37:25.800
So, if I was to try to insert this again, 
you'll see that the primary key is still 3 

01:37:25.800 --> 01:37:30.100
which means I’m not going to be able to 
enter this record in 

01:37:30.100 --> 01:37:33.600
because a record with that primary key 
already exists inside the table.

01:37:33.600 --> 01:37:37.300
So, if I was to click Run again, 
you'll see I'm getting this error.

01:37:37.300 --> 01:37:40.600
It says Duplicate Entry ‘3’ for key ‘PRIMARY’.

01:37:40.600 --> 01:37:45.400
Basically, what that means is we have a duplicate key 
and it’s not going to work.

01:37:45.400 --> 01:37:47.500
But if I was to change this to 4.

01:37:47.500 --> 01:37:50.700
So, instead of having an ID of 3, it’s 4.

01:37:50.700 --> 01:37:52.000
Now, this is going to work.

01:37:52.000 --> 01:37:56.500
And we'll be able to see we have 
two entries with Claire, 

01:37:56.500 --> 01:37:59.800
but they have different student ID’s 
so we're all good to go.

01:37:59.800 --> 01:38:02.900
So, that is the basics of inserting stuff into a table.

01:38:02.900 --> 01:38:05.800
And really, those two techniques are really useful.

01:38:05.800 --> 01:38:08.100
So, that’s kind of the basics of inserting into a table.

01:38:08.100 --> 01:38:10.600
Now, there’s a lot of more things 
we can get into with this.

01:38:10.600 --> 01:38:13.900
And in the next tutorial, I’m going to show 
you guys some other stuff that we can do 

01:38:13.900 --> 01:38:16.700
in order to make inserting into the tables a lot easier.

01:38:16.700 --> 01:38:20.500
[C]

01:38:20.500 --> 01:38:25.200
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys some 
more about inserting information into a database.

01:38:25.200 --> 01:38:29.100
So, in the last tutorial we kind of looked at 
the basics of inserting information.

01:38:29.100 --> 01:38:30.400
So, we have our table here.

01:38:30.400 --> 01:38:31.600
It’s the student table.

01:38:31.600 --> 01:38:35.300
And down here we kind of looked at 
how we can insert information.

01:38:35.300 --> 01:38:38.800
So, we can insert into the name of the table, 
and then values.

01:38:38.800 --> 01:38:40.300
And we can pass in the values.

01:38:40.300 --> 01:38:43.600
We can also say insert into the name of the table.

01:38:43.600 --> 01:38:47.800
And then over here we could specify 
what information we want to insert.

01:38:47.800 --> 01:38:51.300
And then here in the values 
we just insert that information.

01:38:51.300 --> 01:38:56.400
So, we've kind of been using this photo over here of 
this like, you know, student database.

01:38:56.400 --> 01:38:59.500
And I actually just went ahead 
and wrote out all the code 

01:38:59.500 --> 01:39:02.800
for inserting all of these different students 
into the database.

01:39:02.800 --> 01:39:06.100
So, you'll see we're inserting Jack 
who’s a biology major.

01:39:06.100 --> 01:39:07.600
Kate, sociology.

01:39:07.600 --> 01:39:09.300
Claire, who doesn’t have a major.

01:39:09.300 --> 01:39:12.600
There’s another one name Jack 
who’s also a biology major.

01:39:12.600 --> 01:39:16.500
But you'll notice that it has a 
different primary key than this guy up here.

01:39:16.500 --> 01:39:19.000
And then there’s Mike who’s computer science.

01:39:19.000 --> 01:39:25.400
So, these instructions will actually insert 
each of those students into our database.

01:39:25.400 --> 01:39:30.500
And you'll see over here 
when I run this Select * FROM command.

01:39:30.500 --> 01:39:33.600
So, with PopSQL, if you just click on the SQL statement 

01:39:33.600 --> 01:39:36.300
and then you click Run, it’ll go ahead 
and run it for you.

01:39:36.300 --> 01:39:38.400
So, you'll see down here we get all that information.

01:39:38.400 --> 01:39:39.800
So, there database is set up.

01:39:39.800 --> 01:39:42.100
And that’s sort of like the general use case, right?

01:39:42.100 --> 01:39:46.800
That’s like we set up our database table 
and we're just straight-up inserting information into it.

01:39:46.800 --> 01:39:48.500
But there’s some other stuff we can do.

01:39:48.500 --> 01:39:51.400
Namely, we can actually set up our database table 

01:39:51.400 --> 01:40:00.000
in order to make it easier for us to insert elements or 
to control the type of information or the type of rows 

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:02.500
that we can insert into the database table.

01:40:02.500 --> 01:40:06.200
So, what I’m actually going to do 
is I’m actually just going to drop this table.

01:40:06.200 --> 01:40:09.100
So, I’m just going to say DROP TABLE student.

01:40:09.100 --> 01:40:11.000
And we're actually just going to start over.

01:40:11.000 --> 01:40:15.300
So, again, with PopSQL all you have to do 
is click on the SQL command.

01:40:15.300 --> 01:40:17.800
Like I can click on DROP TABLE right here.

01:40:17.800 --> 01:40:20.900
And then when I click Run, it’ll go ahead and drop it.

01:40:20.900 --> 01:40:26.400
So, now if I tried to SELECT ALL from the student table, 
you'll see it says there’s no such table.

01:40:26.400 --> 01:40:31.200
So, let’s go ahead and I’m going to show you guys 
how we can create this student table 

01:40:31.200 --> 01:40:35.100
in order to make it a little bit easier 
for us to insert stuff.

01:40:35.100 --> 01:40:39.000
So, the first thing I’m going to show you guys 
is something called NOT NULL.

01:40:39.000 --> 01:40:44.400
And NOT NULL will basically allow us to define 
that a particular column in the table cannot be NULL.

01:40:44.400 --> 01:40:47.100
So, let’s say that when we're storing our students, 

01:40:47.100 --> 01:40:49.800
we don’t want the student 
to be able to have a NULL name.

01:40:49.800 --> 01:40:54.500
And NULL is basically just a value 
that represents no value.

01:40:54.500 --> 01:40:57.400
So, if something is NULL it means 
that it doesn’t have a value.

01:40:57.400 --> 01:41:00.700
So, let’s say that we always want the student 
to have a name no matter what.

01:41:00.700 --> 01:41:06.100
Well, after I same name VARCHAR(20) 
what I could do is I can say NOT NULL.

01:41:06.100 --> 01:41:08.300
And I'm just putting that right after here.

01:41:08.300 --> 01:41:10.400
What that means is this column can’t be null.

01:41:10.400 --> 01:41:14.700
So, if you insert a row into the table, 
you can’t insert NULL for a name.

01:41:14.700 --> 01:41:19.500
I could also do something else 
which would be UNIQUE.

01:41:19.500 --> 01:41:21.900
So, UNIQUE is another keyword which we can use.

01:41:21.900 --> 01:41:27.900
And basically, what this is going to mean is that the 
major field has to be unique for each row in this table.

01:41:27.900 --> 01:41:34.300
So, if somebody else or another row in the table 
has the same major as another entry, 

01:41:34.300 --> 01:41:36.400
then that will get rejected.

01:41:36.400 --> 01:41:39.300
So, NOT NULL and UNIQUE 
are actually pretty useful.

01:41:39.300 --> 01:41:41.900
So, now what we can do 
is we can actually create this table.

01:41:41.900 --> 01:41:42.700
So, I’m just going to click on this query.

01:41:42.700 --> 01:41:44.100
And I’m going to click Run.

01:41:44.100 --> 01:41:46.400
And you'll see that we get a success message.

01:41:46.400 --> 01:41:47.400
So, we were able to create the table.

01:41:47.400 --> 01:41:50.300
And so, now why don’t we start populating 
this table with information?

01:41:50.300 --> 01:41:54.400
So, I’ll come over here and I’ll click insert 
into student – Jack, biology.

01:41:54.400 --> 01:41:56.300
So, we're going to insert Jack.

01:41:56.300 --> 01:42:00.800
And then we'll select the table and you'll see 
that Jack got inserted just fine.

01:42:00.800 --> 01:42:02.500
Let’s click on this second one.

01:42:02.500 --> 01:42:03.500
Kate, sociology.

01:42:03.500 --> 01:42:04.600
So, we'll run this.

01:42:04.600 --> 01:42:07.200
And again, we'll just select all from the table.

01:42:07.200 --> 01:42:09.300
You'll see both of those got inserted.

01:42:09.300 --> 01:42:11.400
So, now we get down here into this next one.

01:42:11.400 --> 01:42:13.000
So, insert into student.

01:42:13.000 --> 01:42:16.300
So, for Claire, we're inserting in 
student_id and name.

01:42:16.300 --> 01:42:18.400
But let’s actually switch this up.

01:42:18.400 --> 01:42:23.900
So, why don’t we get rid of this and instead 
of saying this, we'll give her a major.

01:42:23.900 --> 01:42:26.200
So, we're going to say that Claire’s a chemistry major.

01:42:26.200 --> 01:42:27.900
But we're actually going to get rid of this name.

01:42:27.900 --> 01:42:31.200
So, instead of Claire, 
I’m just going to say  NULL here.

01:42:31.200 --> 01:42:35.100
And you'll notice up here, 
I said that the name cannot be NULL.

01:42:35.100 --> 01:42:40.400
So, when I created this table, 
I specified that the name field cannot be NULL.

01:42:40.400 --> 01:42:44.800
So, if I come down here and I try to 
insert a student with no name, 

01:42:44.800 --> 01:42:49.100
with a name that’s set to NULL and click Run, 
you'll see that we get this error.

01:42:49.100 --> 01:42:51.200
And it says, “You have an error in your SQL.”

01:42:51.200 --> 01:42:57.600
And basically, what that means 
is we can’t insert in  a value here that’s NULL 

01:42:57.600 --> 01:42:59.800
because we specified that it can’t be NULL up here.

01:42:59.800 --> 01:43:02.100
So, you’re actually not going to be able to do that.

01:43:02.100 --> 01:43:07.700
Now, we also, over here on this major field, 
we defined that it has to be unique.

01:43:07.700 --> 01:43:09.800
So, if I come down here 
and I try to execute this line, 

01:43:09.800 --> 01:43:13.500
so you’ll see, we're trying to insert 
this person’s name as Jack 

01:43:13.500 --> 01:43:15.000
and they're a biology major.

01:43:15.000 --> 01:43:19.500
But we already defined one person 
that was a biology major up here, right?

01:43:19.500 --> 01:43:20.700
It was this first one.

01:43:20.700 --> 01:43:25.100
So, if I try to enter in another biology major, 
it’s going to give me another error.

01:43:25.100 --> 01:43:26.500
So, I’m going to click run.

01:43:26.500 --> 01:43:31.700
And you'll see down here it says 
Duplicate entry ‘Biology’ for key ‘major’.

01:43:31.700 --> 01:43:36.600
So, it yelled at us because we entered 
in a duplicate entry.

01:43:36.600 --> 01:43:40.800
So, NOT NULL and UNIQUE are really 
good ways for you 

01:43:40.800 --> 01:43:44.300
to kind of control the data 
that gets stored on the table.

01:43:44.300 --> 01:43:47.400
And actually, funny enough, 
if you have a primary key – 

01:43:47.400 --> 01:43:54.400
a primary key is actually just an attribute or a column 
on the table that is both NOT NULL and UNIQUE.

01:43:54.400 --> 01:43:58.300
So, a primary key is basically just something 
that’s NOT NULL and UNIQUE.

01:43:58.300 --> 01:43:59.900
So, there’s a couple other ones.

01:43:59.900 --> 01:44:01.600
We're actually going to drop this table again.

01:44:01.600 --> 01:44:03.600
So, I’m just going to click DROP TABLE 
and we'll go ahead and drop the table.

01:44:03.600 --> 01:44:08.900
And over here, I’m going to create the table again, 
but we're going to give this some other stuff.

01:44:08.900 --> 01:44:10.300
So, these are actually what we would call constraints.

01:44:10.300 --> 01:44:15.100
So, I would say like a constraint would be 
that it’s NOT NULL or that it’s UNIQUE.

01:44:15.100 --> 01:44:18.300
But there’s a couple other constraints 
that we can add that I want to show you guys.

01:44:18.300 --> 01:44:21.800
So, let’s say that we wanted to set a default value.

01:44:21.800 --> 01:44:27.600
So, let’s say that if somebody didn’t enter in a major, 
we wanted to be able to give them a default major.

01:44:27.600 --> 01:44:31.100
Well, I can actually say that something has a default.

01:44:31.100 --> 01:44:34.600
So, I can come down here 
and I can say after major, DEFAULT.

01:44:34.600 --> 01:44:40.200
And then inside of single quotes I can just type in 
what I want the default to be.

01:44:40.200 --> 01:44:45.800
So, in our case, if somebody doesn’t provide us with a 
major, why don’t we just say that they’re ‘undecided’.

01:44:45.800 --> 01:44:50.900
So, basically if the major field is left blank, 
we'll say that the person is undecided.

01:44:50.900 --> 01:44:55.000
So, then down here, why don’t we go ahead 
and insert a student into the table 

01:44:55.000 --> 01:44:56.500
that doesn’t have a major.

01:44:56.500 --> 01:44:58.100
So, here we can just say INSERT INTO student 

01:44:58.100 --> 01:45:01.900
and we'll go ahead and specify 
that we're going to enter in the student_id.

01:45:01.900 --> 01:45:03.300
And also the name.

01:45:03.300 --> 01:45:06.000
But you'll notice that I’m not putting major in here.

01:45:06.000 --> 01:45:08.000
Which means that we don’t have to give this a major.

01:45:08.000 --> 01:45:09.500
So, now we'll get rid of Jack’s major.

01:45:09.500 --> 01:45:15.500
And when I go ahead and run this, 
you'll see that we got a success message.

01:45:15.500 --> 01:45:19.800
And if I was to select all the entries from the student, 
it says student_id is 1.

01:45:19.800 --> 01:45:22.300
His name is Jack and his major is undecided.

01:45:22.300 --> 01:45:24.500
So, because I didn’t insert a major, 

01:45:24.500 --> 01:45:28.500
it’s going ahead and populating that major 
for us with the value undecided 

01:45:28.500 --> 01:45:32.200
because that was the default value 
that I placed over here.

01:45:32.200 --> 01:45:35.700
So, that’s another really useful constraint 
that we can put on this.

01:45:35.700 --> 01:45:38.800
All right, so there’s one more of these 
little constraints that I want to show you guys.

01:45:38.800 --> 01:45:42.300
And it’s actually really useful for working 
with primary keys.

01:45:42.300 --> 01:45:50.900
So, you'll notice that the primary keys 
for all of these guys were like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, etc., right?

01:45:50.900 --> 01:45:54.400
I like manually went in and incremented 
all those primary keys.

01:45:54.400 --> 01:45:57.900
But what you can actually do 
you can have the database do that for you.

01:45:57.900 --> 01:46:04.500
So, I can come over here right here after students, 
so remember, student_id is the primary key, right?

01:46:04.500 --> 01:46:05.800
We defined that down here.

01:46:05.800 --> 01:46:10.500
I can come ever here and say AUTO_INCREMENT.

01:46:10.500 --> 01:46:15.100
And AUTO_INCREMENT is basically going to specify 
that the data that gets inserted into here 

01:46:15.100 --> 01:46:18.400
is going to automatically incremented 
every time we add one in.

01:46:18.400 --> 01:46:23.500
So, I could actually come down here and instead of 
inserting a student ID, I can just leave it out.

01:46:23.500 --> 01:46:27.700
So, I can just insert the name and the major.

01:46:27.700 --> 01:46:31.200
And then I can add that information in here.

01:46:31.200 --> 01:46:35.700
So, Jack is going to go ahead and study biology.

01:46:35.700 --> 01:46:37.800
And actually, let me get rid of these.

01:46:37.800 --> 01:46:39.200
So, we can kind of see how this works.

01:46:39.200 --> 01:46:43.300
So, I can copy this for another one.

01:46:43.300 --> 01:46:47.700
And we'll make this Kate and she’s studying sociology.

01:46:47.700 --> 01:46:51.400
So, notice that I’m not actually 
inserting the student_id, right?

01:46:51.400 --> 01:46:53.400
All I’m inserting is the name and the major.

01:46:53.400 --> 01:46:56.900
But because we said that this table 
was going to be auto incremented.

01:46:56.900 --> 01:47:00.400
In other words because we said that the student_id 
was going to be auto incremented 

01:47:00.400 --> 01:47:02.700
we don’t actually have to do that.

01:47:02.700 --> 01:47:04.200
So, I can come down here and I can run this.

01:47:04.200 --> 01:47:05.600
And actually, whoops.

01:47:05.600 --> 01:47:06.400
I have to create the table again.

01:47:06.400 --> 01:47:07.200
My bad.

01:47:07.200 --> 01:47:08.100
So, we're going to create the table.

01:47:08.100 --> 01:47:11.200
And I can insert in this first entry, Jack, 
who’s a biology major.

01:47:11.200 --> 01:47:13.200
And I’ll run this.

01:47:13.200 --> 01:47:16.300
And you'll see it says 1 row affected.

01:47:16.300 --> 01:47:19.800
And then I’m also going to insert this next one 
which is Kate who is studying sociology, 

01:47:19.800 --> 01:47:21.100
so I’m just going to click Run.

01:47:21.100 --> 01:47:23.500
And so, now I’ve inserted 
both of these students into the table.

01:47:23.500 --> 01:47:28.100
And if I select all from the student table, you'll see 
down here that we get both of these entries.

01:47:28.100 --> 01:47:33.400
But Jack has an ID of 1 and Kate as an ID of 2 even 
though I didn’t rigorously specify that, right?

01:47:33.400 --> 01:47:37.400
So, I didn’t actually add in the ID’s 
for either of these guys, 

01:47:37.400 --> 01:47:42.700
and yet they still showed up down here in the table 
which was pretty useful.

01:47:42.700 --> 01:47:47.400
So, that’s a great way for you to just like automatically 
increment like something like a primary key 

01:47:47.400 --> 01:47:50.100
or really any other row in the table 
by using AUTO_INCREMENT.

01:47:50.100 --> 01:47:54.800
So, those are just a couple little things 
that you can do when you’re creating the table.

01:47:54.800 --> 01:47:56.700
We would call those constraints.

01:47:56.700 --> 01:48:02.000
So, you can add constraints onto the specific columns 
of the table and that can control 

01:48:02.000 --> 01:48:04.500
like what types of information 
can be put inside of there.

01:48:04.500 --> 01:48:07.800
So, that’s – there’s a little bit more 
of an advanced way 

01:48:07.800 --> 01:48:10.500
for you guys to insert information into the database.

01:48:10.500 --> 01:48:14.600
[Update &amp; Delete]

