that are stored inside of our database.

01:48:48.200 --> 01:48:51.500
So, these are the database entries 
that we can work with.

01:48:51.500 --> 01:48:54.600
The first thing I’ll show you guys show to do 
is update some of these entries.

01:48:54.600 --> 01:48:58.500
And so, you know, let’s say that we wanted, 
you know, update something.

01:48:58.500 --> 01:49:02.500
So, let’s say that we wanted to change the name of 
one of the majors in our school, right?

01:49:02.500 --> 01:49:06.600
So, let’s say I am the database 
administrator for my school.

01:49:06.600 --> 01:49:12.100
And we decided that instead of calling the major 
biology, we wanted to call it bio.

01:49:12.100 --> 01:49:15.700
So, the official name for the major 
is no longer biology.

01:49:15.700 --> 01:49:17.100
It’s just going to be bio.

01:49:17.100 --> 01:49:20.400
Well, we could actually make that change 
inside the database.

01:49:20.400 --> 01:49:22.800
So, I can use the special keyword called UPDATE.

01:49:22.800 --> 01:49:26.300
And I can just type out the name of the table 
where I want to do the update.

01:49:26.300 --> 01:49:27.600
So, in our case, it’s student.

01:49:27.600 --> 01:49:33.266
And over here I can say UPDATE student 
I want to set major equal to ‘Bio’, right?

01:49:27.600 --> 01:49:37.900
But here’s the thing.

01:49:37.900 --> 01:49:42.000
I don’t want to set the major equal to bio 
for all the students.

01:49:42.000 --> 01:49:46.800
I only want to set the major equal to bio for the 
students who have their major as biology.

01:49:46.800 --> 01:49:48.600
So, over here we can specify that.

01:49:48.600 --> 01:49:50.100
I can say WHERE.

01:49:50.100 --> 01:49:51.700
And here I can put a condition.

01:49:51.700 --> 01:49:54.400
So, I can say major is equal to ‘Biology’.

01:49:54.400 --> 01:49:56.000
And I’m going to be semicolon.

01:49:56.000 --> 01:50:00.400
So, this whole thing now is 
one SQL statement or query.

01:50:00.400 --> 01:50:03.700
And so, what I'm doing is 
I’m updating the student table 

01:50:03.700 --> 01:50:10.500
and I’m setting the major column equal to ‘Bio’ 
where the major column is already equal to ‘Biology’.

01:50:10.500 --> 01:50:14.800
So, now if I was to go ahead and run this, 
you'll see it says Rows affected:  2.

01:50:14.800 --> 01:50:17.700
So, we updated to of the rows in this database.

01:50:17.700 --> 01:50:20.600
And if I was to select all of them 
and I’m going to click Run, 

01:50:20.600 --> 01:50:23.900
you'll see down here now for the two students 
who are biology majors, 

01:50:23.900 --> 01:50:27.200
it’s now saying that they’re bio majors, right?

01:50:27.200 --> 01:50:33.800
So, it updated the database only in those places 
where the student’s major was equal to ‘Biology’.

01:50:33.800 --> 01:50:35.200
And that’s what we can do here.

01:50:35.200 --> 01:50:40.400
So, this is just a very basic update statement, 
but we can use this in order to all sorts of stuff.

01:50:40.400 --> 01:50:41.800
So, let’s do another one.

01:50:41.800 --> 01:50:44.300
We can do the same for computer science.

01:50:44.300 --> 01:50:45.700
So, I could say UPDATE student.

01:50:45.700 --> 01:50:52.800
SET major equal to ‘Comp sci’ 
WHERE major = ‘Computer Science’.

01:50:52.800 --> 01:50:54.800
So, I’m basically abbreviating this major.

01:50:54.800 --> 01:50:56.300
So, let’s go ahead and run this.

01:50:56.300 --> 01:50:58.900
And again, I’m just going to 
SELECT ALL from the table.

01:50:58.900 --> 01:51:04.500
You'll see down here, Mike, who used to be a 
‘Computer Science’ major is now a ‘Comp Sci’ major.

01:51:04.500 --> 01:51:06.200
So, I was able to update that.

01:51:06.200 --> 01:51:08.600
Another thing you can do 
is you can update specific things.

01:51:08.600 --> 01:51:10.300
So, I can say like UPDATE student.

01:51:10.300 --> 01:51:13.000
SET major equal to ‘Comp Sci’, WHERE.

01:51:13.000 --> 01:51:15.200
And then over here I can do a different condition.

01:51:15.200 --> 01:51:19.800
So, I can say like student_id is equal to 4.

01:51:19.800 --> 01:51:24.500
So, now I’m updating the student’s major 
who has an ID of 4.

01:51:24.500 --> 01:51:26.400
So, this is like a different type of query.

01:51:26.400 --> 01:51:28.400
So, down here I’m running that.

01:51:28.400 --> 01:51:30.100
And then I’m going to SELECT ALL.

01:51:30.100 --> 01:51:34.600
And so, now you can see that Jack 
who used to be a biology major 

01:51:34.600 --> 01:51:37.700
is now a comp sci major 
because his student ID was 4.

01:51:37.700 --> 01:51:41.400
So, you can get kind of creative, you know, 
playing around with setting different things 

01:51:41.400 --> 01:51:43.300
and then checking different conditions.

01:51:43.300 --> 01:51:47.000
You can also use more complex conditions down here.

01:51:47.000 --> 01:51:50.100
So, down here we have bio majors 
and we have chemistry majors, right?

01:51:50.100 --> 01:51:54.600
Well, let’s say that any student 
who is either a biology or a chemistry major 

01:51:54.600 --> 01:51:56.900
is now going to be a biochemistry major.

01:51:56.900 --> 01:51:59.800
So, we're combining those two majors together.

01:51:59.800 --> 01:52:01.600
Well, I could update that here.

01:52:01.600 --> 01:52:03.900
So, I could say UPDATE student.

01:52:03.900 --> 01:52:06.200
SET major equal to ‘Biochemistry’.

01:52:06.200 --> 01:52:11.500
WHERE major is equal to ‘Bio’.

01:52:11.500 --> 01:52:14.000
And over here we could say OR.

01:52:14.000 --> 01:52:18.100
And then we could also say major 
is equal to ‘Chemistry’.

01:52:18.100 --> 01:52:20.000
And this is going to give us some OR logic.

01:52:20.000 --> 01:52:24.700
So, now if the student has a major of bio 
or if they have a major of chemistry, 

01:52:24.700 --> 01:52:26.600
we're going to set it equal to biochemistry.

01:52:26.600 --> 01:52:29.500
So, we're basically combining those two majors together.

01:52:29.500 --> 01:52:33.200
So, now I’ll click Run 
and you can see rows affected is 2.

01:52:33.200 --> 01:52:35.300
And I’ll SELECT ALL from students.

01:52:35.300 --> 01:52:39.600
And you can see now Jack and Claire – 
Jack used to be a bio major.

01:52:39.600 --> 01:52:41.200
Claire used to be a chem major.

01:52:41.200 --> 01:52:43.300
Are both now biochemistry majors.

01:52:43.300 --> 01:52:47.400
So, you can use OR in order to 
check multiple things like that.

01:52:47.400 --> 01:52:49.900
You can also set multiple things.

01:52:49.900 --> 01:52:53.000
So, over here I’m actually going to 
get rid of this condition.

01:52:53.000 --> 01:52:57.700
And I can say SET name equal to ‘Tom’.

01:52:57.700 --> 01:53:06.000
And major equal to ‘undecided’.

01:53:06.000 --> 01:53:11.800
WHERE student_id is equal to 1.

01:53:11.800 --> 01:53:16.300
So, whoever has the student equal to 1 
is going to have their name set to Tom 

01:53:16.300 --> 01:53:18.800
and their major set to undecided.

01:53:18.800 --> 01:53:20.000
So, let’s go ahead and run this.

01:53:20.000 --> 01:53:22.500
And you'll see it says 1 row affected.

01:53:22.500 --> 01:53:24.600
So, let’s SELECT ALL from the student’s table.

01:53:24.600 --> 01:53:29.700
And you'll see down here, the student with the ID of 1 
is now named Tom and their major is undecided.

01:53:29.700 --> 01:53:34.500
So, you can change multiple columns 
within the same query like I did up here.

01:53:34.500 --> 01:53:39.200
All right, so there’s one more thing we can do, 
which we can actually get rid of this WHERE.

01:53:39.200 --> 01:53:40.400
So, this is actually optional.

01:53:40.400 --> 01:53:45.600
So, if I got rid of WHERE, I can then set something.

01:53:45.600 --> 01:53:47.000
So, why don’t we set major equal to undecided.

01:53:47.000 --> 01:53:51.200
So, if I get rid of that WHERE statement, this is just 
going to apply to every single row in the table.

01:53:51.200 --> 01:53:53.800
So, now when we run this, 
you'll see rows affected was 5.

01:53:53.800 --> 01:53:58.800
And now when we SELECT ALL from the students 
table, all of their majors are now undecided.

01:53:58.800 --> 01:54:03.100
So, you can either select specific rows 
or groups of rows with that WHERE 

01:54:03.100 --> 01:54:06.800
or you can just do it to all of them 
by dropping the WHERE.

01:54:06.800 --> 01:54:10.100
So, now I’m going to show you guys 
how we can delete rows from the table.

01:54:10.100 --> 01:54:12.400
And it’s actually pretty similar 
to how we would update rows.

01:54:12.400 --> 01:54:15.100
So, instead of updating a specific row 
or a group of rows, 

01:54:15.100 --> 01:54:18.400
I can actually delete a specific row 
or a group of rows.

01:54:18.400 --> 01:54:21.700
So, it’s like I said, pretty similar to UPDATE.

01:54:21.700 --> 01:54:25.200
I’m just going to type in DELETE FROM 
and then the name of the table.

01:54:25.200 --> 01:54:27.300
So in our case it’s going to be student.

01:54:27.300 --> 01:54:30.600
And you can actually just end this off right here.

01:54:30.600 --> 01:54:31.800
I can put a semicolon here.

01:54:31.800 --> 01:54:35.500
And this will go ahead and delete 
all of the rows inside of the table.

01:54:35.500 --> 01:54:37.600
But why don’t we try to get more specific?

01:54:37.600 --> 01:54:39.600
So, I’m going to try to delete a specific row.

01:54:39.600 --> 01:54:41.300
I can say DELETE FROM student.

01:54:41.300 --> 01:54:43.500
WHERE – and now I can specify a condition.

01:54:43.500 --> 01:54:48.800
So, I can say like WHERE student_id is equal to 5.

01:54:48.800 --> 01:54:55.200
So, what this will do is it’ll delete any students or any 
rows from the table that have a student ID of 5.

01:54:55.200 --> 01:54:58.100
So, down here we just have one like that.

01:54:58.100 --> 01:55:01.600
So, Mike, who is an undecided major has an ID of 5.

01:55:01.600 --> 01:55:03.200
So, if I was to run this.

01:55:03.200 --> 01:55:06.300
Now you'll see it says rows affected, 1.

01:55:06.300 --> 01:55:10.800
And we'll select all from students 
and Mike is no longer inside the table.

01:55:10.800 --> 01:55:13.100
So, we actually deleted a specific row.

01:55:13.100 --> 01:55:16.000
And just like update, 
you can get more fancy with these queries.

01:55:16.000 --> 01:55:21.300
So, I could say like WHERE name is equal to Tom.

01:55:21.300 --> 01:55:23.800
And you can also do an AND.

01:55:23.800 --> 01:55:26.300
So, instead doing OR, we're doing an AND here.

01:55:26.300 --> 01:55:30.800
And I can say like major is equal to ‘undecided’.

01:55:30.800 --> 01:55:34.700
So, this will delete any students from the table 
who have the name of Tom 

01:55:34.700 --> 01:55:37.400
and who are an undecided major.

01:55:37.400 --> 01:55:41.100
So, now when I run this query, you'll see – 
and I select all the students again – 

01:55:41.100 --> 01:55:44.100
that Tom got deleted from the table.

01:55:44.100 --> 01:55:45.800
So, he’s no longer there.

01:55:45.800 --> 01:55:48.700
So, just like we used WHERE 
in the UPDATE statements, 

01:55:48.700 --> 01:55:51.500
we can use WHERE with the DELETE statements 
to do all of that.

01:55:51.500 --> 01:55:54.000
But if you wanted, 
you can just delete everything from the table.

01:55:54.000 --> 01:55:56.100
So, now I’m just going to run this command.

01:55:56.100 --> 01:56:00.200
And if I query the table, 
you'll see that nothing is there.

01:56:00.200 --> 01:56:01.700
So, we deleted everything.

01:56:01.700 --> 01:56:03.700
So, updating and deleting is extremely useful.

01:56:03.700 --> 01:56:09.000
And you can use that WHERE statement in order to 
specify what specific row you want to update 

01:56:09.000 --> 01:56:10.600
and what specific row you want to delete.

01:56:10.600 --> 01:56:14.400
[Basic Queries]

01:56:14.400 --> 01:56:18.500
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys about 
getting information from the database.

01:56:18.500 --> 01:56:21.600
More specifically we're going to 
look at the SELECT keyword 

01:56:21.600 --> 01:56:26.300
and we're going to look at the different ways 
that we can ask the database management system 

01:56:26.300 --> 01:56:28.200
to give us some information back.

01:56:28.200 --> 01:56:31.500
So, one of the core tenets of interacting 
with the database management system 

01:56:31.500 --> 01:56:34.600
and using SQL is writing these little queries.

01:56:34.600 --> 01:56:41.600
And a query is essentially just a block of SQL that’s 
designed to ask the database management system 

01:56:41.600 --> 01:56:43.600
for a particular piece of information.

01:56:43.600 --> 01:56:48.800
And so, one of the things that we need to be aware of 
is that when you’re keeping track of, you know, 

01:56:48.800 --> 01:56:50.500
huge amounts of information in a database, 

01:56:50.500 --> 01:56:53.500
you want to be able to grab 
specific information easily.

01:56:53.500 --> 01:56:59.700
So, let’s say that I’m trying to, you know, grab a bunch 
of students from our little student table right here.

01:56:59.700 --> 01:57:04.500
Well, imagine that we had like a million students 
stored inside of that table, right?

01:57:04.500 --> 01:57:07.400
I might not want to just grab every single student.

01:57:07.400 --> 01:57:11.100
I might want to just grab students 
who meet a certain condition 

01:57:11.100 --> 01:57:14.800
or students who have a certain major 
or students with a certain name.

01:57:14.800 --> 01:57:18.900
And we can use SQL queries in order to 
specify those things.

01:57:18.900 --> 01:57:24.500
So, instead of the relational database management 
system giving us back all the entries in a specific table,

01:57:24.500 --> 01:57:28.600
 instead it can just give us back very specific entries 
which meet a certain condition.

01:57:28.600 --> 01:57:32.300
So, we're going to talk a little bit 
about the basics of doing that stuff.

01:57:32.300 --> 01:57:34.000
Now, this is actually a huge topic 

01:57:34.000 --> 01:57:36.900
and it’s the topic that we're going to be talking about 
for most of the rest of course.

01:57:36.900 --> 01:57:41.800
So, this is going to kind of give you guys an 
introduction into writing all of these little queries.

01:57:41.800 --> 01:57:44.500
So, over here, you’ll see that I have this query here.

01:57:44.500 --> 01:57:48.400
And it’s just SELECT * FROM student.

01:57:48.400 --> 01:57:50.600
And actually, if you want, 
you can put this on two different lines.

01:57:50.600 --> 01:57:51.900
A lot of people will do that.

01:57:51.900 --> 01:57:56.000
So, this SELECT keyword is a very special word.

01:57:56.000 --> 01:58:00.300
And this SELECT keyword is basically going to tell 
the relational database management system 

01:58:00.300 --> 01:58:02.800
that we want to get some information from it.

01:58:02.800 --> 01:58:04.200
So, I can say SELECT.

01:58:04.200 --> 01:58:07.500
And then right next to it I can specify 
what information I want to get.

01:58:07.500 --> 01:58:12.100
And so far in this course we've 
been using this star or this asterisk.

01:58:12.100 --> 01:58:15.900
And basically, the asterisk means 
that we want to grab all of the information.

01:58:15.900 --> 01:58:19.700
But if we wanted, we could specify a specific column.

01:58:19.700 --> 01:58:23.900
So, we can select specific columns of information 

01:58:23.900 --> 01:58:26.700
that we want to get back 
from the database management system 

01:58:26.700 --> 01:58:29.100
and then we can say FROM whichever table.

01:58:29.100 --> 01:58:36.600
So, SELECT * FROM table could also be read 
as select every column from the student table.

01:58:36.600 --> 01:58:40.900
So, over here you'll see when I run this, 
we get this information down here.

01:58:40.900 --> 01:58:43.000
So, we're getting all of the students in the table.

01:58:43.000 --> 01:58:47.500
We're getting their student ID’s 
and their names and their majors.

01:58:47.500 --> 01:58:49.600
If I wanted, I could change the columns up here.

01:58:49.600 --> 01:58:52.000
So, I could say like name.

01:58:52.000 --> 01:58:56.200
And now what this will do is 
it’ll select all of the names from the student table.

01:58:56.200 --> 01:59:00.100
So, if I was to run this query, 
you'll see down here we get just the names.

01:59:00.100 --> 01:59:04.600
So, we have the name 
and then it’s Jake, Kate, Claire, Jack, and Mike.

01:59:04.600 --> 01:59:07.900
So, these are all of the names 
that were stored inside of the database.

01:59:07.900 --> 01:59:10.400
I could also include something else.

01:59:10.400 --> 01:59:13.500
So, I could say name, major FROM student.

01:59:13.500 --> 01:59:15.500
And then down here when I run this query, 

01:59:15.500 --> 01:59:18.400
you'll see we're getting the 
students, names, and the majors.

01:59:18.400 --> 01:59:20.700
But we're not getting the student’s ID.

01:59:20.700 --> 01:59:24.200
So, we're able to specify 
which specific columns we want to get back.

01:59:24.200 --> 01:59:27.300
You can also pre-pen these 
with the name of the table.

01:59:27.300 --> 01:59:30.000
So, I could say like student.name.

01:59:30.000 --> 01:59:31.600
And student.major.

01:59:31.600 --> 01:59:34.100
And sometimes people will do this 
just because student.name, 

01:59:34.100 --> 01:59:37.400
it’s clear which table the name is coming from.

01:59:37.400 --> 01:59:40.700
And as we write more and more complex queries, 
that can come in handy more.

01:59:40.700 --> 01:59:46.500
So, for the most part, I’m probably going to be writing 
them both ways, just depending on the situation.

01:59:46.500 --> 01:59:48.300
But you could do something like this.

01:59:48.300 --> 01:59:50.800
We can also order the information that we get back.

01:59:50.800 --> 01:59:55.900
So, here I’m getting the student’s name 
and the student’s major from student.

01:59:55.900 --> 01:59:58.600
And if I wanted, I could order that information.

01:59:58.600 --> 02:00:02.100
So, I could use another command which is ORDER BY.

02:00:02.100 --> 02:00:07.000
And then over here I can just put the name 
of the column that I want to order these by.

02:00:07.000 --> 02:00:08.900
So, I can say ORDER BY name.

02:00:08.900 --> 02:00:12.200
And now when I run this, 
you'll see we get the same results, 

02:00:12.200 --> 02:00:15.100
but they're in alphabetical order based off the name.

02:00:15.100 --> 02:00:17.700
So, we get Claire, Jack, Jack, Kate, and Mike.

02:00:17.700 --> 02:00:20.000
So, these are now ordered in alphabetical order.

02:00:20.000 --> 02:00:23.300
And by default, these are going to be 
in ascending order.

02:00:23.300 --> 02:00:26.000
But if you wanted, you could put them 
in descending order.

02:00:26.000 --> 02:00:29.500
So, you could say DESC and this stands for descending.

02:00:29.500 --> 02:00:33.800
So, now if I run this, you'll see that all the names 
get returned in the opposite order.

02:00:33.800 --> 02:00:36.300
So, Claire is all the way at the bottom.

02:00:36.300 --> 02:00:39.000
And then we go all the way back up to Mike.

02:00:39.000 --> 02:00:40.600
So, you can order by anything.

02:00:40.600 --> 02:00:43.100
So, I can even order by like student_id.

02:00:43.100 --> 02:00:46.000
So, I’m not returning the student_id.

02:00:46.000 --> 02:00:49.900
In other words, I’m not getting the student ID up here, 
but I can still order by it.

02:00:49.900 --> 02:00:53.600
And so, now these are going to be in descending 
order of student ID’s.

02:00:53.600 --> 02:00:56.500
So, actually why don’t we just get all of these now.

02:00:56.500 --> 02:00:58.600
So, I’ll just say SELECT ALL from student.

02:00:58.600 --> 02:01:01.700
And you'll see now it’s ordered in descending order 
by student ID.

02:01:01.700 --> 02:01:03.700
It’s a little bit clearer, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

02:01:03.700 --> 02:01:08.100
But I can also get rid of it or I can just say ASC 
which stands for ascending.

02:01:08.100 --> 02:01:10.800
And now it will order them in ascending order.

02:01:10.800 --> 02:01:13.800
So, you can order by a bunch of different stuff.

02:01:13.800 --> 02:01:15.900
You can also order by different sub columns.

02:01:15.900 --> 02:01:20.200
So, I could say like ORDER BY – 
and we'll start with major.

02:01:20.200 --> 02:01:23.500
And then after that we'll do student_id.

02:01:23.500 --> 02:01:26.600
So, SELECT ALL from student.

02:01:26.600 --> 02:01:27.900
ORDER BY major.

02:01:27.900 --> 02:01:29.100
And then student_id.

02:01:29.100 --> 02:01:31.000
So, it’s going to order them by major first.

02:01:31.000 --> 02:01:33.400
And then if there’s any of them 
that have the same major, 

02:01:33.400 --> 02:01:35.500
it’ll order them by student ID further.

02:01:35.500 --> 02:01:38.200
So, I’m going to run this.

02:01:38.200 --> 02:01:40.200
And you'll see down here, 
it’s ordering everybody by major.

02:01:40.200 --> 02:01:43.700
So, we're getting biology, biology, chemistry, 
computer science, sociology.

02:01:43.700 --> 02:01:46.600
In this case, these two students 
have the same major.

02:01:46.600 --> 02:01:48.500
They also have the same name too.

02:01:48.500 --> 02:01:50.500
But their student ID’s are different.

02:01:50.500 --> 02:01:53.600
So, the student ID’s are now order 
in ascending order.

02:01:53.600 --> 02:01:57.900
But I said descending right here.

02:01:57.900 --> 02:01:59.200
And I run this query.

02:01:59.200 --> 02:02:04.500
Now you'll notice that the biology major 
with student ID 4 came first and then 1.

02:02:04.500 --> 02:02:10.300
So, it ordered it first by major and then within that, 
if they have the same major, 

02:02:10.300 --> 02:02:11.800
it ordered them by student ID.

02:02:11.800 --> 02:02:13.800
And you can specify as many of those as you want.

02:02:13.800 --> 02:02:16.500
You can also limit the amount 
and results you're getting.

02:02:16.500 --> 02:02:19.400
So, I can say like SELECT ALL from student.

02:02:19.400 --> 02:02:20.600
And then I can say LIMIT.

02:02:20.600 --> 02:02:21.900
And here I can put like 2.

02:02:21.900 --> 02:02:25.000
And now what this will do, 
is it’ll limit the results I get back to 2.

02:02:25.000 --> 02:02:28.100
So, now instead of getting all the students back, 
we only got 2.

02:02:28.100 --> 02:02:33.800
So, if you only want like a specify number of rows 
back from the table, then you’ll only get that.

02:02:33.800 --> 02:02:35.500
And you can also combine these.

02:02:35.500 --> 02:02:36.900
So, I could also like order them.

02:02:36.900 --> 02:02:41.700
So, I could say ORDER BY student_id, descending.

02:02:41.700 --> 02:02:44.600
So, now this is going to select all the students.

02:02:44.600 --> 02:02:47.800
Order them by student ID 
only give us 2 of them back.

02:02:47.800 --> 02:02:50.200
So, now when I run this 
you'll see we're getting 2 back.

02:02:50.200 --> 02:02:53.700
And it’s ordering them in 
descending order by student ID.

02:02:53.700 --> 02:02:55.600
So, that can be pretty useful.

02:02:55.600 --> 02:02:58.300
And that’s just another way 
that you can make these more complex.

02:02:58.300 --> 02:03:01.800
The final thing that we can do that I’m going to show 
you guys in this tutorial is filtering.

02:03:01.800 --> 02:03:04.100
So, I can say WHERE.

02:03:04.100 --> 02:03:07.600
So, if you remember in a previous tutorial 
we were updating and deleting students.

02:03:07.600 --> 02:03:12.700
And we wanted to only update or delete specific 
students where certain conditions were true.

02:03:12.700 --> 02:03:14.600
And we used this WHERE condition.

02:03:14.600 --> 02:03:16.000
You can do the same thing for SELECT.

02:03:16.000 --> 02:03:22.800
So, I could say like SELECT ALL FROM students 
WHERE major is equal to ‘Biology’.

02:03:22.800 --> 02:03:26.300
And so, now this is only going to give us 
the students who are biology majors.

02:03:26.300 --> 02:03:31.100
And you can see down here we get back the 
two Jacks who are both biology majors.

02:03:31.100 --> 02:03:34.200
You can do the same thing for chemistry.

02:03:34.200 --> 02:03:36.000
So, let’s see if we have any chemistry majors.

02:03:36.000 --> 02:03:37.300
We do.

02:03:37.300 --> 02:03:38.500
Looks like Claire is a chemistry major.

02:03:38.500 --> 02:03:42.800
And if you wanted, we can only return 
specific columns, like I said.

02:03:42.800 --> 02:03:46.800
So, we can say SELECT, you know, 
the name and major from student.

02:03:46.800 --> 02:03:48.200
WHERE major is ‘Chemistry’.

02:03:48.200 --> 02:03:51.700
And now we're only getting the name 
and the major back.

02:03:51.700 --> 02:03:53.800
And you can make these more complex.

02:03:53.800 --> 02:03:59.500
So, I could say like WHERE major is equal to 
‘Chemistry’ OR major is equal to ‘Biology’.

02:03:59.500 --> 02:04:03.500
And so, now this will give us all of the chemistry 
and the biology majors 

02:04:03.500 --> 02:04:05.700
so we get the two Jacks and Claire.

02:04:05.700 --> 02:04:08.600
And we can also do different things.

02:04:08.600 --> 02:04:13.600
So, like I said, like WHERE major is equal to 
‘Chemistry’ OR name is equal to ‘Kate’.

02:04:13.600 --> 02:04:16.300
And so, now we'll get back any of those students.

02:04:16.300 --> 02:04:20.000
So, we get back Kate and Claire 
and they have different majors.

02:04:20.000 --> 02:04:22.800
So, you can play around 
with those WHERE statements 

02:04:22.800 --> 02:04:26.500
to get specific entries from the individual table.

02:04:26.500 --> 02:04:31.100
So, I want to talk to you guys about how we can make 
these where’s a little bit more complex.

02:04:31.100 --> 02:04:35.600
So, obviously over here we're using equals, 
but there’s a bunch of other stuff you can use too.

02:04:35.600 --> 02:04:38.100
So, this is actually comment in SQL.

02:04:38.100 --> 02:04:42.800
So, if you put two dashes, then anything 
after it is going to be a comment.

02:04:42.800 --> 02:04:46.100
But here I have all the different comparison operators.

02:04:46.100 --> 02:04:47.100
So, we have equals.

02:04:47.100 --> 02:04:53.700
We also have less than, greater than, less than or 
equal to, greater than equal to, equal to, not equal to

02:04:53.700 --> 02:04:56.000
– which is these less than/greater than signs.

02:04:56.000 --> 02:04:57.000
And then AND and OR.

02:04:57.000 --> 02:04:59.500
So, you guys have seen some of these, 
but we can use these.

02:04:59.500 --> 02:05:02.400
So, I can say like major not equal to chemistry.

02:05:02.400 --> 02:05:06.300
So, this is going to select all the students 
where the major is not equal to chemistry.

02:05:06.300 --> 02:05:11.600
So, if I run this, now we get all the students except 
Claire because Claire is a chemistry major.

02:05:11.600 --> 02:05:13.700
Or we can do the same thing for numbers.

02:05:13.700 --> 02:05:16.000
So, I can say like student_id.

02:05:16.000 --> 02:05:19.500
So SELECT ALL the students where student_id 
is less than 3.

02:05:19.500 --> 02:05:21.700
And we need to get rid of this.

02:05:21.700 --> 02:05:26.100
And so, now we'll get all the students 
who have student ID’s.

02:05:26.100 --> 02:05:28.500
Let me get all of these.

02:05:28.500 --> 02:05:32.500
So, we're only getting students 1 and 2.

02:05:32.500 --> 02:05:35.700
We're not getting anything – 
or we can do less than equal to 3.

02:05:35.700 --> 02:05:39.000
And now this will also give us that student ID 
equal to 3.

02:05:39.000 --> 02:05:40.500
And you can combine these.

02:05:40.500 --> 02:05:46.700
So, like where student_id is less than 3 
and name is not equal to Jack.

02:05:46.700 --> 02:05:50.400
So, if we run this now, you'll see we get Kate 
and Claire, 

02:05:50.400 --> 02:05:54.100
but we don’t get Jack who is student ID number 1 
because it didn’t fit the condition.

02:05:54.100 --> 02:05:58.800
So, you can these all these comparison operators 
to compare, you know, different things.

02:05:58.800 --> 02:06:01.700
And by using them inside of this 
WHERE statement over here 

02:06:01.700 --> 02:06:06.500
you can seriously filter the results down to 
only like the few that you need.

02:06:06.500 --> 02:06:10.800
All right, so I want to show you guys one more cool 
thing we can do which is using the IN keyword.

02:06:10.800 --> 02:06:16.400
So, instead of just like putting a little condition 
like this, we could say WHERE.

02:06:16.400 --> 02:06:19.100
And then we could say like 
the name of one of the columns.

02:06:19.100 --> 02:06:20.100
So, like name.

02:06:20.100 --> 02:06:21.600
And then I can say IN.

02:06:21.600 --> 02:06:24.900
And then over here I can put some parenthesis 
and I can put some values in here.

02:06:24.900 --> 02:06:31.500
So, if the name is Claire, Kate, or Mike.

02:06:31.500 --> 02:06:38.100
So, basically what this is saying is SELECT ALL 
from student WHERE the name is IN these 

02:06:38.100 --> 02:06:39.300
like this group of values, right?

02:06:39.300 --> 02:06:42.600
So, if the name is Claire, Kate, or Mike, 
then it’s going to select that.

02:06:42.600 --> 02:06:48.400
So, now I can click Run and you'll see down here, 
we get all the entries, Kate, Claire, and Mike.

02:06:48.400 --> 02:06:52.700
So, this is a really easy way to compare like 
one column to a bunch of different values.

02:06:52.700 --> 02:06:55.400
So, like we could check to see if the name is in there.

02:06:55.400 --> 02:06:57.500
We could also do the same thing for like major.

02:06:57.500 --> 02:07:03.000
So, like WHERE major IN ‘Biology’ or ‘Chemistry’.

02:07:03.000 --> 02:07:09.400
And now if we run this, you'll see that we get Jack 
and Claire because they’re both biology.

02:07:09.400 --> 02:07:11.300
So, we can use IN.

02:07:11.300 --> 02:07:13.800
And you can also combine all these things together.

02:07:13.800 --> 02:07:25.300
So, you could say like WHERE major IN ‘Biology’ and 
‘Chemistry’ AND student_id is greater than 2, right?

02:07:25.300 --> 02:07:30.100
And now I'll click Run and you'll see 
that we get these two students, right?

02:07:30.100 --> 02:07:34.600
So, you can combine the SELECTs 
and the WHEREs and even like the LIMITs 

02:07:34.600 --> 02:07:38.100
and all that other stuff to make these 
kind of complex queries.

02:07:38.100 --> 02:07:41.200
But that’s sort of the basics of doing these queries.

02:07:41.200 --> 02:07:43.700
I mean obviously these are very simple queries.

02:07:43.700 --> 02:07:47.200
And as the database schemas get more complex, 

02:07:47.200 --> 02:07:52.300
the queries that you need to select specific pieces 
of information are also going to get more complex.

02:07:52.300 --> 02:07:54.000
And we're just using one table here, right?

02:07:54.000 --> 02:07:56.900
We're just getting information from the student table.

02:07:56.900 --> 02:07:59.100
But if we had multiple different tables, 

02:07:59.100 --> 02:08:02.900
you know, maybe certain tables 
have like foreign keys to other tables, 

02:08:02.900 --> 02:08:05.300
like getting information can get pretty complex.

02:08:05.300 --> 02:08:10.500
And as we go forward in the course, we're actually 
going to design a more complex database schema.

02:08:10.500 --> 02:08:16.600
And using that database schema, we're going to learn 
more about using these select commands.

02:08:16.600 --> 02:08:19.500
But for now, that kind of shows you guys the basics.

02:08:19.500 --> 02:08:21.800
And so, what you want to do is 
just kind of play around with these, right?

02:08:21.800 --> 02:08:22.700
I mean we have our student table.

02:08:22.700 --> 02:08:24.400
It’s very simple.

02:08:24.400 --> 02:08:25.600
It has three columns.

02:08:25.600 --> 02:08:28.700
And you know, just play around with getting 
specific pieces of information 

02:08:28.700 --> 02:08:32.800
using these WHEREs and INs 
and all of these different keywords 

02:08:32.800 --> 02:08:36.100
in order to select the information that you want.

