02:10:35.300 --> 02:10:37.700
So, this is a more complex database schema.

02:10:37.700 --> 02:10:44.500
But because it’s complex, it’s going to give us some 
awesome opportunities to query it, right?

02:10:44.500 --> 02:10:47.100
In other words, the more complex 
the database schema, 

02:10:47.100 --> 02:10:50.800
the more complex the queries we're going to be able 
to write and play around with.

02:10:50.800 --> 02:10:55.700
Because there’s only so many types of queries 
that we can write for that student table.

02:10:55.700 --> 02:11:01.500
But this is going to be a better example for us to learn 
about different types of queries and stuff like that.

02:11:01.500 --> 02:11:04.900
So, I’m going to go ahead and walk you guys through 
this schema in this tutorial.

02:11:04.900 --> 02:11:07.000
That way you guys understand it.

02:11:07.000 --> 02:11:09.000
Because if you don’t – 
if you can’t wrap your head around it, 

02:11:09.000 --> 02:11:12.500
then you’re not going to be able to follow along 
with the rest of the course.

02:11:12.500 --> 02:11:15.000
So, I’m just going to give you guys 
an explanation of this.

02:11:15.000 --> 02:11:17.400
And that way we have it going forward.

02:11:17.400 --> 02:11:22.500
So, this database schema maps out 
the database for a company, right?

02:11:22.500 --> 02:11:26.300
So, this would be like the information 
that we might want to store about a company.

02:11:26.300 --> 02:11:29.200
So, up here we have our first table 
which is the Employee table.

02:11:29.200 --> 02:11:32.200
And so, this is just going to store 
information about employees.

02:11:32.200 --> 02:11:36.500
So, we're storing like the employee ID, 
the first name, the last name, birth date.

02:11:36.500 --> 02:11:38.600
So, you'll see here, this is actually a date.

02:11:38.600 --> 02:11:40.400
Sex which is going to be male or female.

02:11:40.400 --> 02:11:43.100
Salary, which will be like how much they make.

02:11:43.100 --> 02:11:45.600
And then over here we have two foreign keys.

02:11:45.600 --> 02:11:51.100
So, the primary key of this table is the employee ID 
over here which we have in red.

02:11:51.100 --> 02:11:55.600
The foreign keys are going to be 
these keys over here in green.

02:11:55.600 --> 02:12:03.600
And basically, a foreign key is just – it’s going to store 
the primary key of an entry in a different table, right?

02:12:03.600 --> 02:12:05.000
So, we have two foreign keys.

02:12:05.000 --> 02:12:06.400
The first here is super_id.

02:12:06.400 --> 02:12:08.700
That stands for supervisor ID.

02:12:08.700 --> 02:12:13.600
So, an employee in our company 
is going to be able to have a supervisor.

02:12:13.600 --> 02:12:16.500
And a supervisor is actually going 
to be another employee.

02:12:16.500 --> 02:12:21.800
So, super_id is going to point to 
another employee in the employee table.

02:12:21.800 --> 02:12:23.600
We also have branch_id.

02:12:23.600 --> 02:12:28.500
So, different employees in the company are going to 
be able to work for different branches.

02:12:28.500 --> 02:12:31.600
And you'll see down here we have this branch table.

02:12:31.600 --> 02:12:35.900
And it just has a branch ID, a name, and then also 
some other stuff which we'll talk about.

02:12:35.900 --> 02:12:39.200
So, an employee can work at a branch, right?

02:12:39.200 --> 02:12:41.800
And that’s kind of what we're storing over here.

02:12:41.800 --> 02:12:43.100
So, let’s take a look at this.

02:12:43.100 --> 02:12:47.800
We would say that the employee Michael Scott, 
his super_id is 100.

02:12:47.800 --> 02:12:52.500
That means Michael Scott’s supervisor 
has an ID of 100.

02:12:52.500 --> 02:12:56.600
So, Michael Scott’s supervisor 
is going to be David Wallace, right?

02:12:56.600 --> 02:12:59.000
Because David Wallace has an employee ID of 100.

02:12:59.000 --> 02:13:01.666
Kelly Kapoor has a supervisor ID of 102.

02:12:59.000 --> 02:13:08.000
That means Kelly Kapoor’s supervisor 
is going to be employee 102.

02:13:08.000 --> 02:13:09.700
So, it’s going to be Michael Scott, right?

02:13:09.700 --> 02:13:11.100
So, hopefully that makes sense, right?

02:13:11.100 --> 02:13:13.000
An employee can have a supervisor.

02:13:13.000 --> 02:13:18.900
And super_id is a foreign key which points 
to the employee ID of another employee.

02:13:18.900 --> 02:13:21.700
And then we also have branch_id over here again.

02:13:21.700 --> 02:13:24.400
And this will point to the branch.

02:13:24.400 --> 02:13:25.600
So, branch_id.

02:13:25.600 --> 02:13:28.300
Angela Martin has a branch ID of 2.

02:13:28.300 --> 02:13:32.600
That means Angela Martin works 
at the Scranton branch.

02:13:32.600 --> 02:13:36.300
Andy Bernard has a branch ID of 3.

02:13:36.300 --> 02:13:38.900
That means Andy Bernard works 
at the Stamford branch.

02:13:38.900 --> 02:13:41.000
So, hopefully that kind of makes sense.

02:13:41.000 --> 02:13:43.700
And then down here, like I said, 
we have the Branch table.

02:13:43.700 --> 02:13:46.300
And the Branch table just has an ID, a name.

02:13:46.300 --> 02:13:47.900
And also has a manager ID.

02:13:47.900 --> 02:13:54.500
So, on the Branch table we're actually storing 
the ID of an employee who is the manager.

02:13:54.500 --> 02:13:56.700
So, this is actually a foreign key.

02:13:56.700 --> 02:14:01.100
So, manager ID is going to point to 
one of the employees up here.

02:14:01.100 --> 02:14:07.200
So, we would say that the manager 
of the Scranton branch has an ID of 102.

02:14:07.200 --> 02:14:11.900
So, the manager of the Scranton branch 
is going to be Michael Scott because he has a 102.

02:14:11.900 --> 02:14:14.600
The manager of the Stamford branch has an ID of 106.

02:14:14.600 --> 02:14:19.900
So, the manager of the Stamford branch 
is Josh Porter because he has an ID of 106.

02:14:19.900 --> 02:14:20.900
Hopefully that makes sense.

02:14:20.900 --> 02:14:24.200
And then we're storing the date 
that the manager started being the manager.

02:14:24.200 --> 02:14:26.200
So, now we'll check out the client table.

02:14:26.200 --> 02:14:32.400
So, the client table has a client ID, client name, 
and also has a foreign key branch ID.

02:14:32.400 --> 02:14:39.100
So, we would say that the client Dunmore High 
School, you know, works with branch number 2.

02:14:39.100 --> 02:14:43.000
Or the client Times Newspaper works 
with branch number 3.

02:14:43.000 --> 02:14:47.300
So, Dunmore Highschool 
would be a client of branch number 2 

02:14:47.300 --> 02:14:51.800
which is the Scranton branch over here, right?

02:14:51.800 --> 02:14:53.900
Hopefully that makes sense.

02:14:53.900 --> 02:14:59.200
The Times Newspaper is a client of branch number 3 
which is the Stamford branch.

02:14:59.200 --> 02:15:01.200
So, that’s kind of how those are connected.

02:15:01.200 --> 02:15:05.100
And then over here we have another one 
which is branch supplier.

02:15:05.100 --> 02:15:10.000
So, this has a compound primary key, 
or a composite key.

02:15:10.000 --> 02:15:13.100
And the first part of it is the branch_id.

02:15:13.100 --> 02:15:16.400
So, a Branch Supplier is obviously going to 
store the branch_id.

02:15:16.400 --> 02:15:19.400
And it’s also going to store a supplier name.

02:15:19.400 --> 02:15:23.100
So, it’s important to notice 
that we need a composite key here 

02:15:23.100 --> 02:15:27.800
because the branch_id doesn’t 
uniquely identify each row.

02:15:27.800 --> 02:15:30.300
And the supplier_name doesn’t 
uniquely identify each row.

02:15:30.300 --> 02:15:33.800
Only together can they uniquely identify each row.

02:15:33.800 --> 02:15:37.000
And then the last table down here 
is the Works_With table.

02:15:37.000 --> 02:15:41.800
So, this basically defines the relationship 
between employees and clients.

