02:30:26.500 --> 02:30:30.200
[More Basic Queries]

02:30:30.200 --> 02:30:33.700
In this tutorial I’m going to show you guys 
some awesome SELECT statements 

02:30:33.700 --> 02:30:36.900
which will allow us to query the company database 

02:30:36.900 --> 02:30:39.800
that we've been setting up 
for the last couple tutorials.

02:30:39.800 --> 02:30:44.500
So, basically, I’m going to give us some little prompts 
and we'll see if we can figure out how to get the data.

02:30:44.500 --> 02:30:48.800
So, first thing we're going to do 
is try to find all employees.

02:30:48.800 --> 02:30:54.500
So, our first task is to get 
all the employees from the employee table.

02:30:54.500 --> 02:30:59.000
And you'll see over here, this is essentially 
all of the data that we have stored in our database.

02:30:59.000 --> 02:31:01.300
So, let’s see if we can find all employees.

02:31:01.300 --> 02:31:05.800
I’m going to say SELECT and I’m just going to say *.

02:31:05.800 --> 02:31:10.900
So, SELECT * would mean SELECT ALL COLUMNS 
from the Employee table.

02:31:10.900 --> 02:31:13.400
And this should actually do it.

02:31:13.400 --> 02:31:16.200
So, just by saying this, I’m going to run this 
and we'll see what we get.

02:31:16.200 --> 02:31:19.900
So, down here in the results 
we have returned all the employees.

02:31:19.900 --> 02:31:20.500
Cool.

02:31:20.500 --> 02:31:22.800
So, we were able to get all of the employees.

02:31:22.800 --> 02:31:23.800
Let’s try another one.

02:31:23.800 --> 02:31:26.300
How about we'll try to find all clients.

02:31:26.300 --> 02:31:30.200
So, now instead of finding all employees, 
we'll see if we can find all the clients.

02:31:30.200 --> 02:31:32.200
So, basically it’s the same thing.

02:31:32.200 --> 02:31:34.900
But all I have to do is change employee to client.

02:31:34.900 --> 02:31:37.500
So, now we're grabbing information 
from the Client table.

02:31:37.500 --> 02:31:41.000
So, I’m going to run this and you'll see down here 
we’re getting all of the clients.

02:31:41.000 --> 02:31:41.600
Cool.

02:31:41.600 --> 02:31:45.700
So, that’s, you know, how we can 
just get all the stuff from a single table.

02:31:45.700 --> 02:31:46.700
Let’s try another one.

02:31:46.700 --> 02:31:49.200
Find all employees ordered by salary.

02:31:49.200 --> 02:31:51.700
So, this one is a little bit different.

02:31:51.700 --> 02:31:56.000
Not only do we want to find all the employees, 
but we want to order them by how much they make.

02:31:56.000 --> 02:31:59.800
So, we'll say SELECT ALL from employee.

02:31:59.800 --> 02:32:01.300
And now we want to order.

02:32:01.300 --> 02:32:03.700
So, we can just say ORDER BY.

02:32:03.700 --> 02:32:06.800
And we want to specify what column 
we want to order these by.

02:32:06.800 --> 02:32:08.200
So, we could say salary.

02:32:08.200 --> 02:32:09.500
So, now I’m going to run this.

02:32:09.500 --> 02:32:12.500
And you'll see down here, 
now we get all the employees 

02:32:12.500 --> 02:32:14.800
but they’re ordered by how much they make.

02:32:14.800 --> 02:32:17.000
So, down here, this employee makes 250k a year.

02:32:17.000 --> 02:32:19.700
And it goes all the way down to 55k a year.

02:32:19.700 --> 02:32:21.100
Let’s see if we can do this though.

02:32:21.100 --> 02:32:25.800
So, the richest employee, 
or the employee that makes the most starts.

02:32:25.800 --> 02:32:28.300
So, we can say DESC for descending.

02:32:28.300 --> 02:32:30.700
And now this will list them all out in descending order.

02:32:30.700 --> 02:32:33.600
So, the 250,000 guy starts up at the top.

02:32:33.600 --> 02:32:35.500
And then we go down to 55,000.

02:32:35.500 --> 02:32:36.100
All right.

02:32:36.100 --> 02:32:37.100
So, that’s pretty cool.

02:32:37.100 --> 02:32:39.600
And that shows how we could execute that query.

02:32:39.600 --> 02:32:40.600
So, let’s try another.

02:32:40.600 --> 02:32:44.500
It says Find all employees ordered by sex then name.

02:32:44.500 --> 02:32:48.100
So, what we want to do is order 
all the employees by what sex they are.

02:32:48.100 --> 02:32:52.200
And then within those orderings, we want to order 
them by their name, alphabetically.

02:32:52.200 --> 02:32:53.600
So, I can say SELECT ALL FROM employee.

02:32:53.600 --> 02:32:54.600
It’s the same.

02:32:54.600 --> 02:32:57.100
ORDER BY – this time, we're going to do sex.

02:32:57.100 --> 02:33:00.200
So, why don’t we do first name.

02:33:00.200 --> 02:33:03.200
And then we'll do last name.

02:33:03.200 --> 02:33:07.800
So, now when we run this, you'll see here 
all of these ordered first by sex.

02:33:07.800 --> 02:33:10.400
So, we get female and then here’s all the males.

02:33:10.400 --> 02:33:13.100
And then within that, 
they’re actually ordered alphabetically.

02:33:13.100 --> 02:33:14.900
So, we'll start with the first name.

02:33:14.900 --> 02:33:17.400
So, Angela is first, then Jan, Kelly.

02:33:17.400 --> 02:33:19.400
And then these are all the girls.

02:33:19.400 --> 02:33:21.300
And then down here we have the guys.

02:33:21.300 --> 02:33:23.100
So, Andy, David, Jim, Josh, etc.

02:33:23.100 --> 02:33:26.400
And then if there were any duplicates 
with the first names 

02:33:26.400 --> 02:33:29.000
or there were, you know, 
two that were the same, 

02:33:29.000 --> 02:33:31.900
then we would defer to the last name 
as we specified up there.

02:33:31.900 --> 02:33:34.200
All right, let’s try another query prompt.

02:33:34.200 --> 02:33:36.800
So, we'll ask for a different piece of information.

02:33:36.800 --> 02:33:41.300
So, it says Find the first 5 employees in the table.

02:33:41.300 --> 02:33:43.600
So, this is a situation we could say FROM employee.

02:33:43.600 --> 02:33:45.200
SELECT ALL FROM employee.

02:33:45.200 --> 02:33:48.000
And here we can limit it to 5 employees.

02:33:48.000 --> 02:33:49.900
So, SELECT ALL FROM employee.
LIMIT 5.

02:33:49.900 --> 02:33:52.100
That will give us the first five employees.

02:33:52.100 --> 02:33:55.400
So, you'll see down here, 
we just get the first five like that.

02:33:55.400 --> 02:33:59.900
All right, let’s do a few more 
and we'll see if we can mix it up a little bit.

02:33:59.900 --> 02:34:04.400
So, let’s try to find the first 
and last names of all employees.

02:34:04.400 --> 02:34:07.900
So, this time we're actually just trying 
to get the first and the last names.

02:34:07.900 --> 02:34:15.400
So, up here we could say – instead of SELECT * 
we can just SELECT first_name and last_name.

02:34:15.400 --> 02:34:16.200
Just like that.

02:34:16.200 --> 02:34:17.900
So, over here I’m going to click Run.

02:34:17.900 --> 02:34:21.600
And you'll see now instead of getting all that stuff, 
we're just getting first and last name.

02:34:21.600 --> 02:34:23.800
So, I’ll actually show you guys 
another thing we can do.

02:34:23.800 --> 02:34:24.800
So, here’s the prompt.

02:34:24.800 --> 02:34:28.700
It says find the forename 
and the surnames of all employees.

02:34:28.700 --> 02:34:33.200
So, this is actually similar, but you'll see down here 
when we return to the first and last names, 

02:34:33.200 --> 02:34:36.300
the columns were named first_name and last_name.

02:34:36.300 --> 02:34:40.400
But there’s another keyword 
that we can use in SQL which is called AS.

02:34:40.400 --> 02:34:41.800
So, I can say SELECT first_name.

02:34:41.800 --> 02:34:44.900
And I can say AS forename.

02:34:44.900 --> 02:34:50.000
And we can say last_name AS surname.

02:34:50.000 --> 02:34:53.300
And what this is going to is it’s going to 
return the first names and last names, 

02:34:53.300 --> 02:34:56.200
but instead of naming 
the columns first_name and last_name,

02:34:56.200 --> 02:34:58.600
it’s going to name them forename and surname.

02:34:58.600 --> 02:35:00.000
So we're going to go ahead and run this.

02:35:00.000 --> 02:35:04.300
And you'll see we get exactly the same thing except 
now it’s calling the columns forename and surname.

02:35:04.300 --> 02:35:06.200
So, that’s kind of a useful thing you can do.

02:35:06.200 --> 02:35:10.900
And there’ll be certain circumstances 
where you want to name the columns differently 

02:35:10.900 --> 02:35:12.300
depending on what you need.

02:35:12.300 --> 02:35:14.600
All right, so there’s one more I want to show you guys, 

02:35:14.600 --> 02:35:16.800
and I’m actually going to introduce you 
to a new SQL keyword.

02:35:16.800 --> 02:35:20.500
So, the prompt is find out all the different genders.

02:35:20.500 --> 02:35:24.600
So, what we want to do is figure out 
what are all the different genders 

02:35:24.600 --> 02:35:27.900
that employees are stored 
as inside of the table.

02:35:27.900 --> 02:35:31.100
So, what we can do is we can use 
this special keyword called DISTINCT.

02:35:31.100 --> 02:35:33.400
So, I can say SELECT DISTINCT.

02:35:33.400 --> 02:35:36.700
And then the name of the column 
that I want to select DISTINCT.

02:35:36.700 --> 02:35:40.900
And what this is going to do is when I run this, 
you'll see down here we're getting M, F.

02:35:40.900 --> 02:35:43.000
So, we're getting male and female.

02:35:43.000 --> 02:35:48.100
So, those are all of the different sexes 
that are stored inside of the table.

02:35:48.100 --> 02:35:50.600
I could do the same thing for something else.

02:35:50.600 --> 02:35:53.300
So, we could do like SELECT DISTINCT branch_id.

02:35:53.300 --> 02:35:56.800
And this will tell me all the different branch ID’s 
that are stored on the employee.

02:35:56.800 --> 02:36:00.300
So, you'll see down here 
we have different branch ID’s 1, 2, and 3.

02:36:00.300 --> 02:36:03.600
So, those are all of the distinct branch ID’s 
that employees have.

02:36:03.600 --> 02:36:08.000
And so, that’s this DISTINCT keyword is pretty useful 
if you want to find out like, you know, 

02:36:08.000 --> 02:36:11.900
what are the different values that are stored 
in a particular column.

02:36:11.900 --> 02:36:14.800
All right, so that should kind of show you guys.

02:36:14.800 --> 02:36:17.300
There’s a little bit more about 
how we can use these select queries.

02:36:17.300 --> 02:36:19.900
And it kind of gave you guys a chance 
to see how we might query data 

02:36:19.900 --> 02:36:24.400
from the company database schema 
that we set up.

02:36:24.400 --> 02:36:28.300
[Functions]

02:36:28.300 --> 02:36:32.000
In this tutorial I’m going to show you guys 
some SQL functions.

02:36:32.000 --> 02:36:37.500
Now, an SQL function is basically just a special little, 
kind of like block of code that we can call, 

02:36:37.500 --> 02:36:39.600
which will do something for us.

02:36:39.600 --> 02:36:43.900
So, these will like count things or they'll give us 
averages or they'll add things together.

02:36:43.900 --> 02:36:48.000
And they can be really useful for getting information 
about the data in our tables.

02:36:48.000 --> 02:36:50.600
So, I’m going to give you guys 
a couple different prompts 

02:36:50.600 --> 02:36:53.300
and then we'll figure out how we could solve them.

02:36:53.300 --> 02:36:56.800
So, over here, I have a prompt 
that says Find the number of employees.

02:36:56.800 --> 02:37:00.400
So, this is kind of a, you know, 
maybe something that you’d want to do.

02:37:00.400 --> 02:37:03.300
We want to figure out how many employees 
are in the database.

02:37:03.300 --> 02:37:05.600
So, what I could do is I could say SELECT.

02:37:05.600 --> 02:37:09.000
And I can use a special SQL function called COUNT.

02:37:09.000 --> 02:37:11.100
So, I can just type COUNT like that.

02:37:11.100 --> 02:37:12.900
And then open and close parenthesis.

02:37:12.900 --> 02:37:16.000
And in here I want to put whatever I want to count.

02:37:16.000 --> 02:37:18.900
So, basically we could just put in like emp_id.

02:37:18.900 --> 02:37:22.600
And this will basically tell us how many 
employee ID’s are inside of the table.

02:37:22.600 --> 02:37:27.000
And since the employee ID is the primary key, 
that’ll tell us how many employees we have.

02:37:27.000 --> 02:37:29.600
And then I can just say FROM employee.

02:37:29.600 --> 02:37:31.700
So, basically what I’m doing here 

02:37:31.700 --> 02:37:37.600
is I’m asking SQL to select how many employees 
are inside of the Employee table.

02:37:37.600 --> 02:37:44.400
So, when I run this, you’ll see down here we get 9 
because there’s 9 employees inside of the table.

02:37:44.400 --> 02:37:47.900
And you can see over here 
we have 100 through 108 which is 9.

02:37:47.900 --> 02:37:51.600
Another thing we could do would be to count 
how many employees have supervisors.

02:37:51.600 --> 02:37:53.600
So, this is going to be a different number.

02:37:53.600 --> 02:37:58.500
You’ll see over here that 1 employee, 
David Wallace doesn’t actually have a supervisor.

02:37:58.500 --> 02:38:00.200
So, we could check that out.

02:38:00.200 --> 02:38:02.000
We could say SELECT.

02:38:02.000 --> 02:38:04.100
And I can say super_id.

02:38:04.100 --> 02:38:08.400
And now when I run this, we should get 8 instead of 9 
which we do down there.

02:38:08.400 --> 02:38:13.900
So, this will count how many entries 
in the database table actually have values.

02:38:13.900 --> 02:38:17.000
Another thing we can do – 
and here’s another prompt, 

02:38:17.000 --> 02:38:19.400
which should be kind of interesting to see 
if we can figure it out.

02:38:19.400 --> 02:38:23.500
It says Find the number 
of female employees born after 1970.

02:38:23.500 --> 02:38:27.600
So, this is a little bit more specific, 
but nonetheless it’s kind of the same.

02:38:27.600 --> 02:38:31.500
So, first thing we want to do is count 
how many employees we have.

02:38:31.500 --> 02:38:35.200
So, I’m going to select COUNT 
employee ID FROM employee.

02:38:35.200 --> 02:38:38.000
But I want to limit the results that we get.

02:38:38.000 --> 02:38:39.900
So, I’m going to say WHERE.

02:38:39.900 --> 02:38:41.500
And here, I’m going to check these conditions.

02:38:41.500 --> 02:38:44.400
So, I’m going to say sex is equal to female.

02:38:44.400 --> 02:38:49.700
And birth_date is greater than.

02:38:49.700 --> 02:38:52.000
And over here, I’m actually going to put in a date.

02:38:52.000 --> 02:38:55.800
And so, when I put in I date, 
I’m just going to put the 4-digit year.

02:38:55.800 --> 02:38:58.500
So, I can say, 1970.

02:38:58.500 --> 02:39:00.300
And then a hyphen.

02:39:00.300 --> 02:39:02.000
And then put in the month.

02:39:02.000 --> 02:39:04.000
So, we'll just say 01 and then 01.

02:39:04.000 --> 02:39:07.700
So, this would be like January 1st, 1970.

02:39:07.700 --> 02:39:11.000
So, this will tell us the number 
of female employees born after 1970.

02:39:11.000 --> 02:39:13.500
Or I guess we would put this as 1971.

02:39:13.500 --> 02:39:14.700
So, let’s run this.

02:39:14.700 --> 02:39:17.700
And now you'll see that we get a count of 2.

02:39:17.700 --> 02:39:22.300
So, there are 2 female employees in 
the database table that were born after 1970.

02:39:22.300 --> 02:39:28.000
And you can see over here, 
looks like we have one born in 1971.

02:39:28.000 --> 02:39:29.000
Angela Martin.

02:39:29.000 --> 02:39:31.100
And then one born in 1980.

02:39:31.100 --> 02:39:32.700
So, that actually makes sense.

02:39:32.700 --> 02:39:34.700
All right, lets try another one.

02:39:34.700 --> 02:39:36.000
Here’s another prompt.

02:39:36.000 --> 02:39:39.400
It says Find the average of all employee’s salaries.

02:39:39.400 --> 02:39:41.300
So, this is a little bit different.

02:39:41.300 --> 02:39:44.400
In this case, we want to find out 
the average of all of the employee’s salaries.

02:39:44.400 --> 02:39:46.200
So, instead of COUNT we can say AVG.

02:39:46.200 --> 02:39:49.800
And then inside of parenthesis 
we can just put again the column.

02:39:49.800 --> 02:39:52.000
So, I can say salary.

02:39:52.000 --> 02:39:55.900
And then over here we can say FROM employee.

02:39:55.900 --> 02:39:58.800
And what this will do is it’ll tell us 
the average of all the employee salaries.

02:39:58.800 --> 02:40:00.200
So, I’m going to go ahead and run this.

02:40:00.200 --> 02:40:05.700
And you'll see down here the average 
looks like $92,888.

02:40:05.700 --> 02:40:08.600
And so, if we wanted, we could filter this further.

02:40:08.600 --> 02:40:12.900
So, let’s say I wanted to find the average 
from all the employees who are male.

02:40:12.900 --> 02:40:17.400
So, I could say WHERE sex 
is equal to male, just like that.

02:40:17.400 --> 02:40:20.200
And now this will give us 
the average of all the male salaries.

02:40:20.200 --> 02:40:24.300
So, here it looks like it’s a little higher, 101,333.

02:40:24.300 --> 02:40:28.600
That’s probably because David Wallace 
makes 250 grand a year.

02:40:28.600 --> 02:40:30.700
All right, so that’s kind of interesting.

02:40:30.700 --> 02:40:32.100
Let’s try another one.

02:40:32.100 --> 02:40:33.300
So, how about this.

02:40:33.300 --> 02:40:37.600
Why don’t we try to find the sum 
of all employee salaries?

02:40:37.600 --> 02:40:40.900
So, instead of finding the average, 
we're going to find the sum.

02:40:40.900 --> 02:40:43.600
So, over here, instead of AVG I can say SUM.

02:40:43.600 --> 02:40:44.600
And that stands for SUM.

02:40:44.600 --> 02:40:48.300
And what this will do is it’ll add 
up all the entries for the salaries.

02:40:48.300 --> 02:40:53.400
So, this would basically tell us like how much the 
company is spending on payroll to pay the employees.

02:40:53.400 --> 02:40:54.800
So, over here I’m going to click Run.

02:40:54.800 --> 02:41:01.000
