02:45:12.700 --> 02:45:16.600
[Wildcards]

02:45:16.600 --> 02:45:21.300
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys about 
wildcards and the LIKE keyword in SQL.

02:45:21.300 --> 02:45:26.200
Now wildcards are basically a way 
of defining different patterns 

02:45:26.200 --> 02:45:29.300
that we want to match specific pieces of data to.

02:45:29.300 --> 02:45:35.000
So, this would be a way to kind of like grab data 
that matches a specific pattern.

02:45:35.000 --> 02:45:37.700
And I’m going to show you guys 
exactly how this works.

02:45:37.700 --> 02:45:39.300
So, I have over here a prompt.

02:45:39.300 --> 02:45:43.600
And it just says Find any clients who are an LLC.

02:45:43.600 --> 02:45:45.700
So, this is kind of an interesting prompt.

02:45:45.700 --> 02:45:52.000
Let’s say that we want to query our database 
and find any clients who, you know, were like an LLC.

02:45:52.000 --> 02:45:55.700
And you'll see over here in the Client’s table, 
we actually have one, John Daly Law, LLC, right?

02:45:55.700 --> 02:45:57.300
So, this is a limited liability company.

02:45:57.300 --> 02:46:00.800
And this is kind of what we're looking to find 
with our search.

02:46:00.800 --> 02:46:05.300
So, I’m going to show you guys how we can use 
wildcards in order to find something like this.

02:46:05.300 --> 02:46:11.600
So, I could say SELECT ALL FROM client 
because I want to grab a client.

02:46:11.600 --> 02:46:14.100
And now I can use the WHERE keyword.

02:46:14.100 --> 02:46:15.700
So, I want to filter the results, right?

02:46:15.700 --> 02:46:17.900
WHERE – I’m going to say client_name.

02:46:17.900 --> 02:46:20.800
And I’m going to use this keyword called LIKE.

02:46:20.800 --> 02:46:25.500
And LIKE is a special SQL keyword 
which we're going to use with wildcards.

02:46:25.500 --> 02:46:27.600
Now, we can say LIKE.

02:46:27.600 --> 02:46:32.100
And then over here we want to write out 
a little expression or a little statement.

02:46:32.100 --> 02:46:34.500
So, I’m going to make an open 
and closed quotation mark.

02:46:34.500 --> 02:46:36.400
And we'll put a semicolon over here.

02:46:36.400 --> 02:46:40.500
And now, inside of here, I can uses a couple of 
different special characters.

02:46:40.500 --> 02:46:43.100
So, basically what I can do here 
is define a pattern, okay?

02:46:43.100 --> 02:46:50.500
And if the client’s name – if the specific client’s name 
matches the pattern that I define over here, 

02:46:50.500 --> 02:46:57.500
then this condition or this statement here will be true 
and we'll end up returning that client.

02:46:57.500 --> 02:47:02.600
And so, we can basically use wildcards 
to do a bunch of different stuff.

02:47:02.600 --> 02:47:07.100
So, inside of these quotation marks 
we can use two special characters.

02:47:07.100 --> 02:47:08.500
There is this percent sign.

02:47:08.500 --> 02:47:11.200
And this stands for any number of characters.

02:47:11.200 --> 02:47:14.600
And then there’s this underscore 
which stands for one character.

02:47:14.600 --> 02:47:18.700
And I can use these in order 
to define certain patterns 

02:47:18.700 --> 02:47:24.800
that can be used by the database 
in order to find what we need.

02:47:24.800 --> 02:47:26.700
So, over here I could say LIKE.

02:47:26.700 --> 02:47:28.500
And I could say %LLC.

02:47:28.500 --> 02:47:36.100
Basically, what this pattern is saying is if the client’s 
name is LIKE this pattern, then we want to return it.

02:47:36.100 --> 02:47:41.000
So, in other words, if it’s any number of characters 
and then an LLC at the end, then we want to return it.

02:47:41.000 --> 02:47:44.900
So, this percent sign is going to stand 
for any number of characters.

02:47:44.900 --> 02:47:46.500
So, that means any characters.

02:47:46.500 --> 02:47:48.400
Anything can come before that.

02:47:48.400 --> 02:47:51.900
But that would mean that the name 
has to end in LLC.

02:47:51.900 --> 02:47:57.200
So, you'll see over here John Daly Law, LLC 
has a bunch of characters here.

02:47:57.200 --> 02:48:00.500
And then the last three characters are LLC.

02:48:00.500 --> 02:48:01.900
And that’s kind of standard.

02:48:01.900 --> 02:48:05.000
Most limited liability companies 
will be set up like that.

02:48:05.000 --> 02:48:08.200
It’ll be like company name, LLC or John Daly Law, LLC.

02:48:08.200 --> 02:48:13.500
So, this wildcard, if there’s any company names 
like that that end with LLC 

02:48:13.500 --> 02:48:17.400
is going to catch them 
because it’s using this percent sign 

02:48:17.400 --> 02:48:20.900
because basically means 
any number of characters can come before this.

02:48:20.900 --> 02:48:23.200
And then LLC.

02:48:23.200 --> 02:48:27.300
If you’re familiar with regular expressions, 
this is very similar to regular expressions.

02:48:27.300 --> 02:48:28.900
It’s just a more simplified version.

02:48:28.900 --> 02:48:30.100
So, I’m just going to click Run.

02:48:30.100 --> 02:48:34.000
And you'll see down here 
we returned John Daly, LLC, just like that.

02:48:34.000 --> 02:48:39.500
So, that’s how we could use these wildcards and this 
LIKE keyword in order to find something like that.

02:48:39.500 --> 02:48:41.400
So, why don’t we try another one?

02:48:41.400 --> 02:48:45.100
So, I have another little prompt here 
that we can see if we can figure it out.

