Well, over here we're going to change this.

02:48:59.200 --> 02:49:04.500
So, instead of looking in client 
we're going to look in branch_supplier.

02:49:04.500 --> 02:49:07.000
And then down here 
we're going to do the same thing.

02:49:07.000 --> 02:49:11.100
Except here, we're just going 
to say supplier_name.

02:49:11.100 --> 02:49:14.300
So, over here in the Branch Supplier table we have 
a bunch of different branch suppliers, right?

02:49:14.300 --> 02:49:19.800
These would be like companies 
that supply products to our company.

02:49:19.800 --> 02:49:24.700
And you'll see there’s actually a couple of these 
that have the word Label in them.

02:49:24.700 --> 02:49:29.500
And so, if the word Label is in the company’s name, 
then we know they probably sell labels.

02:49:29.500 --> 02:49:32.500
And so, what I could do is I could say use a wildcard 

02:49:32.500 --> 02:49:37.500
to see if the word label shows up 
anywhere inside of their names.

02:49:37.500 --> 02:49:42.300
So, what I could do is I’m actually going to 
come over here and I’m going to change this up.

02:49:42.300 --> 02:49:45.100
So, basically we're going to have 
any number of characters.

02:49:45.100 --> 02:49:47.200
We're going to use this %.

02:49:47.200 --> 02:49:49.600
And then I'll just say Label.

02:49:49.600 --> 02:49:53.100
And then we'll make another one with another %.

02:49:53.100 --> 02:49:57.000
So, actually, why don’t we check to see 
if the word Label is actually in there.

02:49:57.000 --> 02:50:00.300
So, it’s going to be % and then Label, just like that.

02:50:00.300 --> 02:50:01.500
So, this is going to be our wildcard.

02:50:01.500 --> 02:50:07.400
So, this will basically match if the supplier name 
has the word Label in it somewhere.

02:50:07.400 --> 02:50:08.900
So, now I’m going to run this.

02:50:08.900 --> 02:50:12.300
