02:51:07.400 --> 02:51:10.300
So, over here we can change this to employee.

02:51:10.300 --> 02:51:11.500
So FROM employee.

02:51:11.500 --> 02:51:18.200
And we want to check to see 
if the birthdate is LIKE the wildcard.

02:51:18.200 --> 02:51:21.900
So, what we can do is we can 
actually use this character right here.

02:51:21.900 --> 02:51:23.500
It’s this underscore.

02:51:23.500 --> 02:51:26.800
So, the underscore represents any single character.

02:51:26.800 --> 02:51:32.100
So, the percent sign represented 
just like any number of random characters.

02:51:32.100 --> 02:51:35.800
Any number of characters would match it.

02:51:35.800 --> 02:51:38.000
And the underscore represents one character.

02:51:38.000 --> 02:51:39.900
And we can use that to our advantage.

02:51:39.900 --> 02:51:42.500
So, we know the way 
that these dates are formatted, right?

02:51:42.500 --> 02:51:45.600
It’s a 4-digit date, a hyphen.

02:51:45.600 --> 02:51:49.300
Or it’s a 4-digit year, a hyphen, 
