search the database for you.

02:53:52.600 --> 02:53:55.600
[Union]

02:53:55.600 --> 02:53:59.300
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys 
about unions in SQL.

02:53:59.300 --> 02:54:03.200
Now, a union is basically a special SQL operator 

02:54:03.200 --> 02:54:08.500
which we can use to combine the results 
of multiple select statements into one.

02:54:08.500 --> 02:54:13.200
So, I might have, you know, two or three 
different select statements that I’m using.

02:54:13.200 --> 02:54:17.700
And if I want it, I can combine all of them 
into the same result 

02:54:17.700 --> 02:54:22.600
and get just a big list or a big table back 
from the database.

02:54:22.600 --> 02:54:24.000
So, I’m going to show you guys how we can do this.

02:54:24.000 --> 02:54:27.300
And I’m actually going to give us some prompts 
so we can kind of practice.

02:54:27.300 --> 02:54:32.600
So, over here, I have prompt that says 
Find a list of employee and branch names.

02:54:32.600 --> 02:54:34.200
So, this is kind of interesting.

02:54:34.200 --> 02:54:38.300
We can actually use the UNION operator 
in order to do this.

02:54:38.300 --> 02:54:44.900
So, first thing, why don we see how we could just grab 
just the employee names and just the branch names.

02:54:44.900 --> 02:54:49.200
So, if I wanted to just grab the employee names 
I could just say SELECT first_name.

02:54:49.200 --> 02:54:53.300
And why don’t we just grab the first name 
FROM employee, right?

02:54:53.300 --> 02:54:54.300
So, that’s pretty easy.

02:54:54.300 --> 02:54:55.700
I mean that’s as easy as it gets.

02:54:55.700 --> 02:54:57.800
We're just getting all the first names 
from the employees.

02:54:57.800 --> 02:54:59.600
Let’s do the same thing for branch name.

02:54:59.600 --> 02:55:05.100
So, I could say SELECT branch_name 
FROM branch, right?

02:55:05.100 --> 02:55:07.000
Again, pretty simple.

02:55:07.000 --> 02:55:10.500
So, this over here will give us all the names 
of the employees.

02:55:10.500 --> 02:55:14.300
So, if I was to just run this, you'll see 
we get all the names of all the employees.

02:55:14.300 --> 02:55:17.800
And if I was to run this, we get the names 
of the all the branches.

02:55:17.800 --> 02:55:21.500
But how can we combine those together 
into one single statement 

02:55:21.500 --> 02:55:24.400
and then just get a list with 
all that information in it?

02:55:24.400 --> 02:55:26.000
Well, I can use the UNION keyword.

02:55:26.000 --> 02:55:28.300
So, over here I can just say UNION.

02:55:28.300 --> 02:55:30.500
I’m going to actually going to 
get rid of this semicolon.

02:55:30.500 --> 02:55:34.800
So, now we have one single SQL query which is going 
to ask the relational database management system 

02:55:34.800 --> 02:55:41.000
to return not only the employee first names, 
but also the branch names in a single column.

02:55:41.000 --> 02:55:42.600
So, I’m going to go ahead and run this.

02:55:42.600 --> 02:55:46.700
And you'll see down here 
that we get this big column right here.

02:55:46.700 --> 02:55:48.100
Just says first_name.

02:55:48.100 --> 02:55:50.200
And then we have all of the names of the employees, 

02:55:50.200 --> 02:55:52.600
but down here we also have the names 
of the branches.

02:55:52.600 --> 02:55:54.900
So, Corporate, Scranton and Stamford.

02:55:54.900 --> 02:55:57.100
We have all that information.

02:55:57.100 --> 02:56:01.400
And it’s all combined 
with the names of the employees.

02:56:01.400 --> 02:56:02.900
So, this is actually pretty cool.

02:56:02.900 --> 02:56:07.400
And when we're using unions, 
you can do unions on a bunch of different things.

02:56:07.400 --> 02:56:08.700
There are a couple rules though.

02:56:08.700 --> 02:56:12.800
So, the first rule is that you have to 
have the name number of columns 

02:56:12.800 --> 02:56:15.300
that you're getting in each SELECT statement.

02:56:15.300 --> 02:56:18.100
So, in this first SELECT statement, 
I’m grabbing one column.

02:56:18.100 --> 02:56:20.600
In the second SELECT statement, 
I’m grabbing one column.

02:56:20.600 --> 02:56:24.100
If I was to come up here 
and also try to grab like last_name, 

02:56:24.100 --> 02:56:26.500
now when I run this, we're going to get an error 

02:56:26.500 --> 02:56:30.000
because up here we have two columns 
and down here we only have one.

02:56:30.000 --> 02:56:31.200
So, that’s the first rule.

02:56:31.200 --> 02:56:32.500
You can do that.

02:56:32.500 --> 02:56:34.100
They have to have the same number of columns.

02:56:34.100 --> 02:56:36.700
They also have to have a similar datatype.

02:56:36.700 --> 02:56:40.600
So, first_name and branch_name, 
they’re both strings, right?

02:56:40.600 --> 02:56:46.200
So, they're both of a similar datatype, 
so we're able to return them in the same result.

02:56:46.200 --> 02:56:48.800
But if you had two things 
that were very different datatypes 

02:56:48.800 --> 02:56:52.100
then it might not necessarily work out as well.

02:56:52.100 --> 02:56:54.400
So, that is basically how we can do these.

02:56:54.400 --> 02:56:57.400
And that kind of just show you guys 
how we can use the UNION operator 

02:56:57.400 --> 02:56:59.900
in order to combine the results 
from multiple statements.

02:56:59.900 --> 02:57:02.500
If we wanted, we could add in another one too.

02:57:02.500 --> 02:57:04.400
So, I could say like UNION again.

02:57:04.400 --> 02:57:13.000
And now we can UNION all of that 
with like SELECT client_name FROM client.

02:57:13.000 --> 02:57:16.900
So, now I'm grabbing not just the names of the 
employees and the branches, but also the clients.

02:57:16.900 --> 02:57:22.800
So, when we run this now, you can see we're getting 
this really long list that has all these names.

02:57:22.800 --> 02:57:26.900
Now one thing you might notice is that 
the column name up here is first_name.

02:57:26.900 --> 02:57:33.500
So, this is saying that it’s first_name when in reality, 
you know, that’s not necessarily what this is.

02:57:33.500 --> 02:57:37.100
And so, over here you'll see that it’s first_name 

02:57:37.100 --> 02:57:41.800
because the first SELECT statement, the column 
that we were grabbing was called first_name.

02:57:41.800 --> 02:57:43.900
So, that’s why that’s showing up as first_name.

02:57:43.900 --> 02:57:46.100
But if you wanted, you could change that.

02:57:46.100 --> 02:57:48.400
So, I could say first_name AS 
and then I can change the name.

02:57:48.400 --> 02:57:50.800
So, I can just say like Company_Names.

02:57:50.800 --> 02:57:57.400
And so, now the column name is going to be 
Company_Names instead of first_name.

02:57:57.400 --> 02:58:02.300
So, now this is just like all the different names 
that we have stored in the company database.

02:58:02.300 --> 02:58:05.400
So, hopefully that kind of makes sense 
and that’s kind of how we can use UNIONs.

02:58:05.400 --> 02:58:08.000
I’m going to show you guys a couple 
other cool ones that we can do.

02:58:08.000 --> 02:58:10.400
So, I’m going to pull up another prompt here.

02:58:10.400 --> 02:58:12.300
Why don’t we take a look?

02:58:12.300 --> 02:58:15.900
It says find a list of 
all clients &amp; branch suppliers’ names.

02:58:15.900 --> 02:58:18.200
So, this is actually pretty similar.

02:58:18.200 --> 02:58:23.500
So, we can SELECT client_name FROM client.

02:58:23.500 --> 02:58:33.800
And then we can also UNION this with SELECT 
supplier_name FROM branch_supplier.

02:58:33.800 --> 02:58:37.900
And so, now we're going to get a table with 
all the client names and all of the supplier names.

02:58:37.900 --> 02:58:39.100
So, let’s run this.

02:58:39.100 --> 02:58:41.100
And you'll see over here we get all that.

02:58:41.100 --> 02:58:45.200
So, we have all of the clients 
and then we have all of the suppliers.

02:58:45.200 --> 02:58:49.700
And you'll notice over here on the Client table 
and the Branch Supplier table, 

02:58:49.700 --> 02:58:53.200
both of these have a branch_id column.

02:58:53.200 --> 02:58:55.400
So, the Branch Supplier has a branch_id column.

02:58:55.400 --> 02:58:57.700
And the client has a branch_id column.

02:58:57.700 --> 02:58:59.700
So, what we could do is we could use that.

02:58:59.700 --> 02:59:01.400
So, I could say like SELECT client_name.

02:59:01.400 --> 02:59:06.100
And we could also SELECT branch_id FROM client.

02:59:06.100 --> 02:59:13.100
And then we can SELECT supplier_name and the branch_id from the Branch Supplier.

02:59:13.100 --> 02:59:17.100
And that will give us now not only 
the client names and the supplier names, 

02:59:17.100 --> 02:59:19.800
but also the branches that they're associated with.

02:59:19.800 --> 02:59:22.700
So, you can see down here 
we get all of that information.

02:59:22.700 --> 02:59:25.100
Now, one thing I do want to point to 
is you'll notice over here 

02:59:25.100 --> 02:59:29.200
we have branch_id up here 
and branch_id down here.

02:59:29.200 --> 02:59:35.400
So, the Branch Supplier table and the Client table 
both have a column called branch_id.

02:59:35.400 --> 02:59:38.000
And sometimes in a situation like this 
it can get a little bit confusing 

02:59:38.000 --> 02:59:40.900
because we have the same column name.

02:59:40.900 --> 02:59:43.300
But associated with different tables.

02:59:43.300 --> 02:59:49.500
And so, what a lot of people will do is they'll prefix 
these column names with the table name.

02:59:49.500 --> 02:59:52.100
So, let’s say like client.branch_id.

02:59:52.100 --> 02:59:57.900
Or they'll say branch_supplier.branch_id.

02:59:57.900 --> 03:00:01.400
And what that does is it basically 
just makes it a little bit more readable.

03:00:01.400 --> 03:00:04.400
So, now I know that this branch ID 
comes from the Client table.

03:00:04.400 --> 03:00:07.300
And this branch ID 
comes from the Branch Supplier table.

03:00:07.300 --> 03:00:08.500
You don’t have to do that.

03:00:08.500 --> 03:00:12.000
But in a lot of circumstances, 
it can be extremely useful.

03:00:12.000 --> 03:00:15.300
So, I want to show you guys 
one more thing we can do with UNION, 

03:00:15.300 --> 03:00:17.100
so I’m going to give us another prompt here.

03:00:17.100 --> 03:00:21.600
And it basically just says find a list of all money spent 
or earned by the company.

03:00:21.600 --> 03:00:23.100
So, this one is kind of interesting.

03:00:23.100 --> 03:00:28.000
All the money that the company either spends 
or earns, we want to combine into a single list.

03:00:28.000 --> 03:00:33.300
So, basically, the company earns money 
through the total sales down here.

03:00:33.300 --> 03:00:37.800
And the company spends money by paying 
its employees in the salary field up here.

03:00:37.800 --> 03:00:39.600
So, we can combine those two.

03:00:39.600 --> 03:00:46.000
So, I can just say like SELECT salary FROM employee.

03:00:46.000 --> 03:00:58.100
And again, we can UNION that 
with SELECT total_sales FROM works_with.

03:00:58.100 --> 03:01:01.100
And so, now this will give us that column.

03:01:01.100 --> 03:01:03.600
So, you'll see over here it’s all of this money 

03:01:03.600 --> 03:01:06.700
that’s either going in or out of 
the branch combined together.

03:01:06.700 --> 03:01:09.900
Hopefully, that gives you a good idea 
of how UNIONs work.

03:01:09.900 --> 03:01:12.200
Now, obviously, these are very simple examples.

03:01:12.200 --> 03:01:17.100
But you can take this and kind of extrapolate it out 
to more complex examples.

03:01:17.100 --> 03:01:20.400
Union basically just combines 
the results from two SELECT statements.

03:01:20.400 --> 03:01:22.000
That’s essentially all it does.

03:01:22.000 --> 03:01:26.300
But again, there are certain rules like you have to have 
the same number of columns in both statements.

03:01:26.300 --> 03:01:29.600
And they have to be like similar datatype 
and stuff like that.

03:01:29.600 --> 03:01:35.700
But UNIONs can be very useful for sort of combining 
all this data into a single place.

03:01:35.700 --> 03:01:39.600
[Joins]

03:01:39.600 --> 03:01:42.700
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys 
about JOINs in SQL.

03:01:42.700 --> 03:01:47.600
Well, JOIN is basically used to combine rows 
from two or more tables 

03:01:47.600 --> 03:01:50.500
based on a related column between them.

03:01:50.500 --> 03:01:55.000
So, JOINs can be really useful for combining 
information from different tables 

03:01:55.000 --> 03:02:00.300
into a single result 
which we can then use to, you know, 

03:02:00.300 --> 03:02:03.600
obviously find out specific information 
that’s stored in our database.

03:02:03.600 --> 03:02:07.600
So, in order to teach JOINs, 
I’m actually going to have us do one thing.

03:02:07.600 --> 03:02:11.100
We're going to insert another branch 
into the Branch table.

03:02:11.100 --> 03:02:13.400
So, over here I just have the code to do that.

03:02:13.400 --> 03:02:15.600
I’m inserting INTO branch the VALUES.

03:02:15.600 --> 03:02:17.700
So, the branch is going to have an ID of 4.

03:02:17.700 --> 03:02:19.900
The branch is going to be called Buffalo.

03:02:19.900 --> 03:02:24.200
And it’s not going to have a manager ID 
and it’s not going to have a manager start date.

03:02:24.200 --> 03:02:30.000
So, down here in this table that we've been using 
so far, we have branches 1, 2, and 3.

03:02:30.000 --> 03:02:31.800
Corporate, Scranton, and Stamford.

03:02:31.800 --> 03:02:33.200
Now we're adding a Buffalo branch.

03:02:33.200 --> 03:02:38.400
But the Buffalo branch doesn’t have a manager ID 
and it doesn’t have a manager start date.

03:02:38.400 --> 03:02:39.600
Both of those are NULL.

03:02:39.600 --> 03:02:42.700
So, I went ahead and inserted this into my database.

03:02:42.700 --> 03:02:46.400
And you'll see down here 
this is now our branch database.

03:02:46.400 --> 03:02:48.400
So, we have 1, 2, 3, 4.

03:02:48.400 --> 03:02:49.700
All of these pieces of information.

03:02:49.700 --> 03:02:54.000
And then the manager ID for Buffalo is NULL 
and the manager start date is also NULL.

03:02:54.000 --> 03:02:56.000
So, that’s actually going to come in handy 

03:02:56.000 --> 03:02:58.100
for what I’m going to be showing you guys 
with these joins.

03:02:58.100 --> 03:03:00.300
So, if I want to follow along, go ahead and do that.

03:03:00.300 --> 03:03:01.800
But if you're not going to be following along, 

03:03:01.800 --> 03:03:06.100
just know that Buffalo doesn’t have a manager ID 
or a manager start date.

03:03:06.100 --> 03:03:09.800
So, now that we've inserted that into the database, 
let’s get started.

03:03:09.800 --> 03:03:17.100
So, I want to kind of show you guys what a JOIN is 
before I actually talk to you about what it does.

03:03:17.100 --> 03:03:19.400
So, I’m just going to go ahead 
and show you guys an example.

03:03:19.400 --> 03:03:20.800
I’m just going to paste in here.

03:03:20.800 --> 03:03:22.900
And then we'll talk about the example 

03:03:22.900 --> 03:03:25.300
and you'll be able to see exactly 
what the JOIN is doing.

03:03:25.300 --> 03:03:28.200
So, over here I have this.

03:03:28.200 --> 03:03:32.100
It says Find all branches 
and the names of their managers.

03:03:32.100 --> 03:03:35.600
So, we want to find all the branches in the database.

03:03:35.600 --> 03:03:39.500
And for each of those branches 
we want to find the name of the manager.

03:03:39.500 --> 03:03:43.600
So, down here you'll notice 
that I have this branch table.

03:03:43.600 --> 03:03:48.700
And the branch has this column here mgr_id, right?

03:03:48.700 --> 03:03:51.900
And inside the mgr_id 
we have ID’s of different employees.

03:03:51.900 --> 03:03:57.300
So, these ID’s relate to employee ID’s 
which are stored up here.

03:03:57.300 --> 03:04:04.000
Now, I want you guys to notice that 
the employee ID column and the manager ID column 

03:04:04.000 --> 03:04:07.100
that we have down here are similar, right?

03:04:07.100 --> 03:04:09.300
They’re both storing employee IDs.

03:04:09.300 --> 03:04:13.200
And so, this is basically a column that is shared 

03:04:13.200 --> 03:04:16.300
between the Employee table 
and the Branch table, right?

03:04:16.300 --> 03:04:20.100
Both of those tables have a column 
which stores employee ID’s.

03:04:20.100 --> 03:04:25.400
And anytime you have a situation like that, 
you can use something called a JOIN.

03:04:25.400 --> 03:04:32.800
And like I said, a JOIN is used to combine rows from 
two or more tables based on the related column.

03:04:32.800 --> 03:04:37.300
So, in our case, the related column 
is the ID’s of the employees.

03:04:37.300 --> 03:04:43.200
So, over here, in order to find all the branches 
and the names of their managers we can use a JOIN.

03:04:43.200 --> 03:04:44.700
So, here I’m saying SELECT.

03:04:44.700 --> 03:04:47.700
And I’m selecting employee.emp_id.

03:04:47.700 --> 03:04:52.700
employee.first_name and branch.branch_name.

03:04:52.700 --> 03:04:55.100
Now, up to this point in the course, 

03:04:55.100 --> 03:05:02.100
we’ve never used multiple table’s columns 
up here in the SELECT statement.

03:05:02.100 --> 03:05:06.600
So, normally we just say like employee.emp_id, 
employee.first_name.

03:05:06.600 --> 03:05:09.400
We wouldn't use – or we wouldn't specify anything 
from the Branch table.

03:05:09.400 --> 03:05:11.900
But because we're joining the tables, 

03:05:11.900 --> 03:05:17.400
we can actually specify that we want to 
grab a column from the Branch table as well.

03:05:17.400 --> 03:05:18.700
So, just keep that in mind.

03:05:18.700 --> 03:05:22.900
So, when we get this table back, 
we're going to be getting the employee ID, 

03:05:22.900 --> 03:05:26.000
the employee’s first name, 
the branch’s name.

03:05:26.000 --> 03:05:30.500
So, that will basically give us the branch 
and the name of the branch manager.

03:05:30.500 --> 03:05:33.600
And I want to grab that information from employee.

03:05:33.600 --> 03:05:36.300
And then I’m going to say JOIN branch.

03:05:36.300 --> 03:05:38.500
And so, when I say JOIN branch, 

03:05:38.500 --> 03:05:44.200
what this is going to do is it’s going to join 
the Employee table and the Branch table together.

03:05:44.200 --> 03:05:46.900
So, it’s going to join them together into one table.

03:05:46.900 --> 03:05:51.200
And it’s going to join them together on a specific column.

03:05:51.200 --> 03:05:55.100
Now, this is the column that both of these 
are going to have in common.

03:05:55.100 --> 03:05:57.500
So, I’m going to say Join branch ON.

03:05:57.500 --> 03:06:05.700
And then over here, I can basically just say 
employee.emp_id is equal to branch.mgr_id.

03:06:05.700 --> 03:06:08.600
So, basically I want to combine these two tables.

03:06:08.600 --> 03:06:12.300
Or I want to combine all of the rows 
from the Employee table 

03:06:12.300 --> 03:06:14.500
and all of the rows from the Branch table, 

03:06:14.500 --> 03:06:22.700
as long as the manager ID of the branch row 
is equal to the employee ID of the employee row.

03:06:22.700 --> 03:06:25.400
So, I’m going to go ahead and run this 
and you guys will see what happens.

03:06:25.400 --> 03:06:27.600
And this should kind of clear up 
exactly what’s happening.

03:06:27.600 --> 03:06:30.100
So, down here we get three columns.

03:06:30.100 --> 03:06:32.700
We get emp_id, first_name and branch_name.

03:06:32.700 --> 03:06:35.000
And remember, we specified that up here.

03:06:35.000 --> 03:06:39.900
I said employee – I want the ID, the first name, 
and then I want the name of the branch.

03:06:39.900 --> 03:06:42.600
So, down here, we're getting the employee ID.

03:06:42.600 --> 03:06:43.800
So, it’s 100.

03:06:43.800 --> 03:06:46.900
We’re getting the first name, which is David.

03:06:46.900 --> 03:06:49.000
And we're getting the branch name Corporate.

03:06:49.000 --> 03:06:54.100
So, this tells me that the manager 
of the Corporate branch is named David.

03:06:54.100 --> 03:06:57.500
The manager of the Scranton branch 
is named Michael.

03:06:57.500 --> 03:07:00.500
And the manager of the Stamford branch 
is named Josh.

03:07:00.500 --> 03:07:04.200
And if you look over there in our database table, 
that’s exactly right.

03:07:04.200 --> 03:07:10.600
And so, essentially, we combined a row 
from the Branch table, this branch name, 

03:07:10.600 --> 03:07:16.700
with the rows from the Employee table, emp_id 
and first_name into one single table, right?

03:07:16.700 --> 03:07:23.100
But we only combined them when the employee ID 
was equal to the branch’s manager ID, right?

03:07:23.100 --> 03:07:25.300
We have a bunch of employees over here.

03:07:25.300 --> 03:07:28.200
Michael, Angela, Kelly, Stanley, Josh, Andy, Jim.

03:07:28.200 --> 03:07:33.900
But not all of these employees have their ID 
down here in the mgr_id column.

03:07:33.900 --> 03:07:39.000
So, only employees whose ID’s match 
the value here in the this mgr_id column 

03:07:39.000 --> 03:07:43.700
were joined together into this combined table 
that we got down here.

03:07:43.700 --> 03:07:46.600
So, that is basically what a JOIN is.

03:07:46.600 --> 03:07:50.700
We can take rows from one table, 
combine them with rows from another table.

03:07:50.700 --> 03:07:52.100
And it gives us information.

03:07:52.100 --> 03:07:53.800
So, this is pretty useful information, right?

03:07:53.800 --> 03:07:57.200
We got the name of the manager for a specific branch 

03:07:57.200 --> 03:08:00.900
even though that information 
was stored in different tables.

03:08:00.900 --> 03:08:04.800
So, this is kind of the most basic version of a JOIN.

03:08:04.800 --> 03:08:07.900
And this is like just the normal join.

03:08:07.900 --> 03:08:10.800
You can see I’m just using 
this normal JOIN keyword up here.

03:08:10.800 --> 03:08:13.800
But there’s actually a couple other types of JOINs.

03:08:13.800 --> 03:08:16.700
So, there’s actually four basic types 
of JOINs that we can use.

03:08:16.700 --> 03:08:18.900
The first is just this general JOIN.

03:08:18.900 --> 03:08:21.200
And this is what’s referred to as inner JOIN.

03:08:21.200 --> 03:08:26.300
And the inner JOIN is going to combine rows 
from the Employee table and the Branch table 

03:08:26.300 --> 03:08:29.600
whenever they have the shared column in common.

03:08:29.600 --> 03:08:32.900
So, whenever the employee ID 
is equal to the manager ID, 

03:08:32.900 --> 03:08:38.000
then that is going to get included 
in the table that gets returned back to us.

03:08:38.000 --> 03:08:40.000
But there’s a couple of other types of joins.

03:08:40.000 --> 03:08:43.700
And I want to show you guys what they do.

03:08:43.700 --> 03:08:45.300
So, there’s another which is called a LEFT JOIN.

03:08:45.300 --> 03:08:48.600
And you can just say LEFT JOIN just like that.

03:08:48.600 --> 03:08:51.300
And so, now instead of doing a normal JOIN 
we're going to do what’s called a LEFT JOIN.

03:08:51.300 --> 03:08:53.900
And I want to show you guys 
what happens when I run this.

03:08:53.900 --> 03:08:56.000
So, I’m going to go ahead and run this.

03:08:56.000 --> 03:09:01.500
And you'll see over here that instead of 
just getting David, Michael, and Josh, 

03:09:01.500 --> 03:09:05.600
we also got all of the other employees 
from the Employee table.

03:09:05.600 --> 03:09:08.500
So, we got Jan, Angela, Kelly, 
Stanley, Andy, and Jim.

03:09:08.500 --> 03:09:11.800
All of the employees got included in the results, right?

03:09:11.800 --> 03:09:14.600
Not just the employees who are branch managers.

03:09:14.600 --> 03:09:20.300
So, when we just use an inner JOIN, only the 
employees who are branch managers got included.

03:09:20.300 --> 03:09:24.700
But when we used the LEFT JOIN, 
all of the employees got included.

03:09:24.700 --> 03:09:26.100
And here’s the reason why.

03:09:26.100 --> 03:09:31.000
With the LEFT JOIN, 
we include all of the rows from the left table.

03:09:31.000 --> 03:09:34.900
So, in our case, the left table 
is the table over here, right?

03:09:34.900 --> 03:09:36.300
It’s the employee table.

03:09:36.300 --> 03:09:40.400
And the left table is basically 
the one that is included in the FROM statement.

03:09:40.400 --> 03:09:45.300
So, whenever we use this LEFT JOIN, 
that means all of the rows in the Employee table 

03:09:45.300 --> 03:09:46.800
are going to get included in the results.

03:09:46.800 --> 03:09:50.600
But only the rows in the Branch table that matched 

03:09:50.600 --> 03:09:53.700
are going to get included 
because the Branch table is like the right table.

03:09:53.700 --> 03:09:57.000
There’s another type of JOIN we can use 
which is called a RIGHT JOIN.

03:09:57.000 --> 03:09:58.800
And so, I can just say RIGHT.

03:09:58.800 --> 03:10:00.900
Now, this is going to do the opposite.

03:10:00.900 --> 03:10:06.000
So, instead of including all of the rows 
from the Employee table, no matter what, 

03:10:06.000 --> 03:10:09.700
now it’s going to include all of the rows 
from the Branch table, no matter what.

03:10:09.700 --> 03:10:11.100
So, I’m going to go ahead and run this.

03:10:11.100 --> 03:10:15.400
And down here you'll see we have all of the rows 
from the Branch table.

03:10:15.400 --> 03:10:21.300
So, not only did we get the rows with had managers, 
but we also got the rows which didn’t.

03:10:21.300 --> 03:10:27.000
So, down here, remember, the Buffalo branch 
which we just added didn’t have a manager.

03:10:27.000 --> 03:10:29.500
So, it didn’t actually get linked to an employee.

03:10:29.500 --> 03:10:31.700
So, therefore, both of these things were NULL.

03:10:31.700 --> 03:10:34.200
So, in the LEFT JOIN, we got all of the employees.

03:10:34.200 --> 03:10:36.400
In the RIGHT JOIN, we got all of the branches.

03:10:36.400 --> 03:10:40.900
In other words, in the LEFT JOIN 
we got all of the rows from the LEFT table.

03:10:40.900 --> 03:10:44.200
In the RIGHT JOIN we got all the rows 
from the RIGHT table.

03:10:44.200 --> 03:10:48.300
So, in certain circumstances 
you're going to want to do either one of those.

03:10:48.300 --> 03:10:53.000
So, those are the three basic types of JOINs 
that we can use in MySQL.

03:10:53.000 --> 03:10:56.700
There’s actually a fourth type of JOIN 
that I want to talk to you guys about.

03:10:56.700 --> 03:11:01.800
Unfortunately, we can’t do it in MySQL 
but it’s called a FULL OUTER JOIN.

03:11:01.800 --> 03:11:07.900
And basically, in a FULL OUTER JOIN, it’s basically 
a LEFT JOIN and a RIGHT JOIN combined.

03:11:07.900 --> 03:11:11.600
So, remember, in the LEFT JOIN, 
we grabbed all the employees 

03:11:11.600 --> 03:11:13.100
and the RIGHT JOIN 
we grabbed all the branches.

03:11:13.100 --> 03:11:16.900
In a FULL OUTER JOIN you would grab 
all of the employees and all of the branches 

03:11:16.900 --> 03:11:21.200
no matter if they met this condition or not.

03:11:21.200 --> 03:11:27.800
So, like I said, there’s not like – we can’t just come 
over here and say like FULL JOIN in MySQL.

03:11:27.800 --> 03:11:29.200
We're not going to be able to do that.

03:11:29.200 --> 03:11:34.300
But I just wanted to let you guys know about it 
so that you kind of have context for it.

03:11:34.300 --> 03:11:38.000
So, these JOINs are really simple 
and they're really easy.

03:11:38.000 --> 03:11:40.900
And they can be really useful 
if you want to combine information.

03:11:40.900 --> 03:11:43.300
So, something as simple as getting the names 
of all the branch managers 

03:11:43.300 --> 03:11:49.100
and what branches they manage 
is really, really easy if we just use JOINs.

03:11:49.100 --> 03:11:53.000
[Nested Queries]

03:11:53.000 --> 03:11:56.400
In this tutorial I'm going to talk to you guys 
about nested queries in SQL.

03:11:56.400 --> 03:12:02.600
Now, nested query is basically a query where 
we're going to be using multiple select statements 

03:12:02.600 --> 03:12:05.000
in order to get a specific piece of information.

03:12:05.000 --> 03:12:08.700
So, a lot of times we're going to 
want to get very specific information 

03:12:08.700 --> 03:12:12.200
and we're going to need to use 
the results of one SELECT statement 

03:12:12.200 --> 03:12:16.100
to inform the results of another SELECT statement.

03:12:16.100 --> 03:12:20.600
So, this is a little bit more advanced and this is kind of 
when we're getting into more advanced query writing.

03:12:20.600 --> 03:12:23.700
But I want to show you guys how this works 

03:12:23.700 --> 03:12:28.600
because a lot of information that you’re going to 
want to get is going to involve using nested queries.

03:12:28.600 --> 03:12:32.100
So, let’s go ahead and put a prompt up on the screen.

03:12:32.100 --> 03:12:38.400
It says Find names of all employees 
who have sold over $30,000 to a single client.

03:12:38.400 --> 03:12:44.300
So, we want to get the names of the employees, 
if they've sold more than 50k to a client.

03:12:44.300 --> 03:12:47.400
So, the first thing I would do 
if we were trying to figure this out, 

03:12:47.400 --> 03:12:51.900
figure out how to write this query 
is let’s just look at the information that we have.

03:12:51.900 --> 03:12:54.400
So, down here we have this Works With table.

03:12:54.400 --> 03:12:57.900
And the Works With table has total sales, right?

03:12:57.900 --> 03:13:01.700
And each one of these rows defines 
how much a particular employee 

03:13:01.700 --> 03:13:03.800
has sold to a particular client, right?

03:13:03.800 --> 03:13:08.700
So, employee 105 sold $55,000 
to client 400, etc, right?

03:13:08.700 --> 03:13:12.800
So, over here we have part 
of the information, right?

03:13:12.800 --> 03:13:15.500
In other words, here we have the total sales.

03:13:15.500 --> 03:13:21.000
But what we don’t have is the employee’s first name 
and they're last name, right?

03:13:21.000 --> 03:13:22.800
We don’t have the actual employee’s name.

03:13:22.800 --> 03:13:26.700
What we do have though is the ID 
of the employee who did it, right?

03:13:26.700 --> 03:13:28.600
So, we have the employee’s ID.

03:13:28.600 --> 03:13:34.300
And we can use the employee’s ID 
in order to get their first name and their last name.

03:13:34.300 --> 03:13:38.400
So, in this case we had part of the data here 
on the Works With table.

03:13:38.400 --> 03:13:41.400
And we have part of the data up here 
on the Employee table.

03:13:41.400 --> 03:13:45.100
And this is a situation 
where we can use a nested query.

03:13:45.100 --> 03:13:47.800
So, the first thing I’m going to do 
is I’m going to write a query 

03:13:47.800 --> 03:13:54.300
which is going to get me all of the employee ID’s 
that have sold more than $30,000 to a single client.

03:13:54.300 --> 03:13:56.800
All right, so we're going to start with Step 1, 

03:13:56.800 --> 03:14:01.700
which means we're getting all of the employee ID’s 
from here if they’ve sold more than 30k.

03:14:01.700 --> 03:14:04.200
So, let’s go ahead and write that query.

03:14:04.200 --> 03:14:06.600
Shouldn't be too hard considering 
all the stuff that we know.

03:14:06.600 --> 03:14:14.200
So, I’m going to SELECT emp_id FROM works_with.

03:14:14.200 --> 03:14:23.400
And I’m going to select it WHERE total_sales 
is greater than 30,000.

03:14:23.400 --> 03:14:25.300
And we'll go ahead and end this.

03:14:25.300 --> 03:14:28.100
And actually, up here, 
I’m just going to prefix this with the table name.

03:14:28.100 --> 03:14:31.400
So, I’m going to say works_with.emp_id.

03:14:31.400 --> 03:14:36.700
And then down here we'll say works_with.total_sales just so it’s more clear.

03:14:36.700 --> 03:14:38.800
Especially when we get into nested queries 

03:14:38.800 --> 03:14:44.100
it’s usually useful to prefix everything with the table 
name just in case we have repeated column names.

03:14:44.100 --> 03:14:45.500
So, over here I’m going to run this.

03:14:45.500 --> 03:14:49.800
And this should give us all the ID’s of the employees 
who have sold more than 30,000.

03:14:49.800 --> 03:14:53.300
So, you’ll see we get 102 and 105 
shows up three times.

03:14:53.300 --> 03:14:56.200
So, it looks like 105 has sold a lot of paper.

03:14:56.200 --> 03:15:02.900
And so, now we have all of the ID’s of the employees 
who have sold more than $30,000 worth of products.

03:15:02.900 --> 03:15:08.000
And so, what we can do now is we can figure out 
from this information, 

03:15:08.000 --> 03:15:10.700
we want to get those employee’s 
first names and last name.

03:15:10.700 --> 03:15:13.900
And so, I’m going to go ahead 
and write another query up here.

03:15:13.900 --> 03:15:15.300
I’m going to say SELECT.

03:15:15.300 --> 03:15:19.000
And I’m going to say employee.first_name.

03:15:19.000 --> 03:15:22.500
And why don’t we do employee.last_name.

03:15:22.500 --> 03:15:26.000
And we're going to SELECT this FROM employee.

03:15:26.000 --> 03:15:28.800
And then over here we're going to say WHERE.

03:15:28.800 --> 03:15:31.900
And this is where we're going to go ahead 
and use a nested query.

03:15:31.900 --> 03:15:39.200
So, basically I want to select all of the employees 
whose ID’s we got from this query right here.

03:15:39.200 --> 03:15:44.500
So, what I could do is I could say 
employee.emp_id IN – 

03:15:44.500 --> 03:15:49.200
and remember, the IN keyword is going to 
give us a result if the employee ID 

03:15:49.200 --> 03:15:53.700
is in values that we specify 
inside of these parenthesis.

03:15:53.700 --> 03:15:58.000
So, what I could do is 
I can actually nest this query inside of there.

03:15:58.000 --> 03:16:03.700
So, I can go ahead and take this and I can just paste it 
right in here in between these parenthesis.

03:16:03.700 --> 03:16:06.900
And one thing you want to keep in mind 
is just how this is formatted.

03:16:06.900 --> 03:16:11.200
So, you’ll see I formatted this 
and it’s indented from this query over here.

03:16:11.200 --> 03:16:15.100
And then I’m going to get rid of this semicolon 
and we'll put a semicolon over here.

03:16:15.100 --> 03:16:17.400
So, basically what this is saying is I want to get 

03:16:17.400 --> 03:16:20.700
the first name and the last name 
FROM the Employee table 

03:16:20.700 --> 03:16:26.200
where the employee ID is IN the result of this query.

03:16:26.200 --> 03:16:29.900
So, if the employee ID got returned from this query, 

03:16:29.900 --> 03:16:34.800
which gave us the ID’s of all of the employees 
who have sold over 30,000, 

03:16:34.800 --> 03:16:37.400
then we're going to return 
their first name and last name.

03:16:37.400 --> 03:16:38.900
So, let’s go ahead and run this.

03:16:38.900 --> 03:16:43.000
And you'll see over here, 
now we're getting the names of the two employees.

03:16:43.000 --> 03:16:48.300
So, Michael Scott sold over 30,000 
and Stanley Hudson also sold over 30,000.

03:16:48.300 --> 03:16:53.400
So, that is actually a really cool way 
where we can find out that information.

03:16:53.400 --> 03:16:55.400
So, that’s how we can use a nested query, right?

03:16:55.400 --> 03:16:57.700
A lot of times you'll use this IN keyword.

03:16:57.700 --> 03:17:00.400
I’ll also show you some other examples 
where we use other things.

03:17:00.400 --> 03:17:05.700
But in that case, we're basically checking 
to see if the employee ID is IN this result.

03:17:05.700 --> 03:17:08.800
All right, so now I have another prompt here.

03:17:08.800 --> 03:17:13.900
It says Find all clients who are handled 
by the branch that Michael Scott manages.

03:17:13.900 --> 03:17:15.800
So, this is another interesting one.

03:17:15.800 --> 03:17:18.100
It says assume you know Michael’s ID.

03:17:18.100 --> 03:17:20.300
So, we're going to assume that we know 
what Michael Scott’s ID is.

03:17:20.300 --> 03:17:24.800
This is another one where again, 
we're going to need to grab data from one table 

03:17:24.800 --> 03:17:27.900
in order to inform the data from another table.

03:17:27.900 --> 03:17:30.600
So, the first thing that we want to be able to do 

03:17:30.600 --> 03:17:34.700
is figure out the branch ID of the branch 
that Michael Scott manages, right?

03:17:34.700 --> 03:17:39.600
So, over here we have our branches 
and each one has a manager ID, right?

03:17:39.600 --> 03:17:44.500
And so, what we need to do is able to figure out 
which of these branches Michael Scott manages.

03:17:44.500 --> 03:17:51.600
Then once we have that information we can figure out 
all of the clients that use that branch ID, right?

03:17:51.600 --> 03:17:55.300
So, over here, the manager ID will map us 
to Michael Scott.

03:17:55.300 --> 03:17:59.800
And the branch ID will actually map us 
to the Client table over here 

03:17:59.800 --> 03:18:02.400
because it has the branch ID as a foreign key.

03:18:02.400 --> 03:18:06.100
So, the first thing we'll do is we'll figure out 
what branch Michael Scott manages.

03:18:06.100 --> 03:18:07.700
So, that should be easy enough.

03:18:07.700 --> 03:18:09.200
We can just say SELECT.

03:18:09.200 --> 03:18:18.400
And actually we'll just do the branch.branch_id 
FROM branch WHERE.

03:18:18.400 --> 03:18:21.700
And remember, we’re going to assume 
that we know Michael Scott’s ID.

03:18:21.700 --> 03:18:30.100
So, I can just say branch.branch_id is equal to – 
and Michael Scott’s ID is 102.

03:18:30.100 --> 03:18:32.800
So, I can just say is equal to 102.

03:18:32.800 --> 03:18:36.900
And so, what this should do, is it should give us 
the branch ID of the branch that he manages.

03:18:36.900 --> 03:18:38.900
In this case – actually, whoops.

03:18:38.900 --> 03:18:41.400
Instead of branch_id this needs to be mgr_id.

03:18:41.400 --> 03:18:44.500
And this is going to give us 2, right?

03:18:44.500 --> 03:18:50.600
Because 2 is the Scranton branch 
which is the branch that Michael Scott manages.

03:18:50.600 --> 03:18:52.200
So, now that we have this piece of information, 

03:18:52.200 --> 03:18:57.800
all we want to do is just get all of the clients 
that are handled by that branch.

03:18:57.800 --> 03:19:00.200
So, we can just say SELECT.

03:19:00.200 --> 03:19:02.500
And why don’t we just get the client name?

03:19:02.500 --> 03:19:10.200
So, it’ll say client.client_name FROM client WHERE.

03:19:10.200 --> 03:19:18.800
And over here, we're basically just going to say 
WHERE client.branch_id is equal to.

03:19:18.800 --> 03:19:23.300
And over here we're going to set 
an equal to the result of this query.

03:19:23.300 --> 03:19:28.700
So, we're going to set it equal to the result of getting 
the ID of the branch that Michael Scott manages.

03:19:28.700 --> 03:19:31.300
So, down here, we'll put this statement 

03:19:31.300 --> 03:19:34.000
and you'll see, again, 
I’m just embedding this in here.

03:19:34.000 --> 03:19:37.300
So, what’s going to happen is when 
the relational database management system 

03:19:37.300 --> 03:19:41.800
sees an embedded SQL statement like this, 
it’s going to execute this first.

03:19:41.800 --> 03:19:44.600
And then it’s going to execute the outer one.

03:19:44.600 --> 03:19:47.300
So, it starts inner and then it slowly goes outer.

03:19:47.300 --> 03:19:53.000
So, we'll be able to get the branch ID 
where Michael Scott is the manager.

03:19:53.000 --> 03:19:56.300
And then we can use that information 
to find all of the clients.

03:19:56.300 --> 03:19:57.900
So, over here, I’m just going to click Run.

03:19:57.900 --> 03:20:00.600
And I’m going to go ahead 
and get rid of this semicolon right here.

03:20:00.600 --> 03:20:03.100
And now we can go ahead and run this.

03:20:03.100 --> 03:20:04.700
And you'll see we get all of these clients.

03:20:04.700 --> 03:20:08.200
So, we get like Dunmore Highschool, Lackawana 
County, Scranton White pages, and FedEx.

03:20:08.200 --> 03:20:11.200
So, those are all the clients that are managed 
by the Scranton branch.

03:20:11.200 --> 03:20:13.500
Now, there is one more thing I want to point out 

03:20:13.500 --> 03:20:18.000
which is you’ll notice down here we're setting 
client.branch_id equal to.

03:20:18.000 --> 03:20:22.100
We're checking to see 
if it’s equal to the result of this statement.

03:20:22.100 --> 03:20:27.800
But here’s the problem, is this statement isn’t 
necessarily guaranteed to only return one value.

03:20:27.800 --> 03:20:32.900
So, if this – so, let’s say that Michael Scott 
was the manager at like multiple branches, 

03:20:32.900 --> 03:20:35.600
it’s possible that this would return multiple values.

03:20:35.600 --> 03:20:38.900
So, what we want to do is just come down here 
and say, LIMIT 1.

03:20:38.900 --> 03:20:42.100
And that’ll make sure that we only get 1 of these.

03:20:42.100 --> 03:20:45.200
So, now if I click Run, you'll see it does the same thing.

03:20:45.200 --> 03:20:48.900
Although, now we're just making sure 
that we only have 1.

03:20:48.900 --> 03:20:51.400
So, anytime you're using something like a quality, 

03:20:51.400 --> 03:20:55.700
it’s always a good idea to limit it down to 1 
unless you’re looking for a situation 

03:20:55.700 --> 03:20:59.300
where Michael Scott is going to 
be managing multiple branches.

03:20:59.300 --> 03:21:01.200
In which case, we can use IN instead.

03:21:01.200 --> 03:21:04.900
All right, so that’s kind of a little dip 
into nested queries.

03:21:04.900 --> 03:21:07.500
Now, obviously these can get very complex.

03:21:07.500 --> 03:21:12.800
And really, you know, what’s important is that you 
have a solid understanding of the fundamentals.

03:21:12.800 --> 03:21:18.000
If you don’t understand the fundamentals that we've 
kind of talked about up to this point in this video, 

03:21:18.000 --> 03:21:21.200
then using nested queries is really going to 
confuse the crap out of you.

03:21:21.200 --> 03:21:25.700
All nested queries are is it’s just kind of like 
one query informing another query, 

03:21:25.700 --> 03:21:27.800
maybe informing another query, right?

03:21:27.800 --> 03:21:32.500
We just use the results from one query 
to get results from another query, etc.

03:21:32.500 --> 03:21:38.000
And as long as you can break the nested query 
up into its individual parts, 

03:21:38.000 --> 03:21:40.900
you should have absolutely no problem writing these.

03:21:40.900 --> 03:21:46.000
And really, the best way to get good at writing more 
complex queries like this is just to practice.

03:21:46.000 --> 03:21:48.900
So, the more you practice writing nested queries 

03:21:48.900 --> 03:21:52.200
and using all these things in combination, 
the better you’re going to get at it.

03:21:52.200 --> 03:21:56.000
[On Delete]

03:21:56.000 --> 03:21:59.900
In this tutorial, I’m going to talk to you guys 
about deleting entries in the database 

03:21:59.900 --> 03:22:03.600
when they have foreign keys associated to them.

03:22:03.600 --> 03:22:07.300
So, this is actually a pretty interesting topic.

03:22:07.300 --> 03:22:12.200
And over here in our company database we have 
a pretty complex database schema, right?

03:22:12.200 --> 03:22:17.300
We have all sorts of foreign keys 
that are linking between all sorts of places.

03:22:17.300 --> 03:22:19.600
And I want to pose to you guys a scenario.

03:22:19.600 --> 03:22:24.300
So, imagine over here I have my Branch table 
and I have my Employee table.

03:22:24.300 --> 03:22:30.000
So, imagine that I came over here in my Employee 
table and I deleted one of the employees, right?

03:22:30.000 --> 03:22:32.900
So, let’s say that I deleted Michael Scott.

03:22:32.900 --> 03:22:36.000
So, over here we have this employee, Michael Scott.

03:22:36.000 --> 03:22:38.000
And his branch ID is 2.

03:22:38.000 --> 03:22:44.100
So, Michael Scott has a foreign key here defined, 
which is branch_id, right?

03:22:44.100 --> 03:22:46.800
So, branch_id, Michael Scott’s branch ID is 2 

03:22:46.800 --> 03:22:52.900
which means that Michael Scott belongs to the 
Scranton branch which is right down there, right?

03:22:52.900 --> 03:22:54.800
But let’s pose something.

03:22:54.800 --> 03:22:59.300
Like imagine that we were to delete 
Michael Scott from the database, right?

03:22:59.300 --> 03:23:04.600
Well, what’s going to happen this manager ID 
down here?

03:23:04.600 --> 03:23:09.900
So, if we delete Michael Scott, 
we delete the employee with ID 102.

03:23:09.900 --> 03:23:11.800
What’s going to happen to the manager ID?

03:23:11.800 --> 03:23:18.300
The manager ID is supposed to be linking us 
to an actual row in the Employee table.

03:23:18.300 --> 03:23:23.400
But if we delete Michael Scott, then all of a sudden 
102, that doesn’t mean anything, right?

03:23:23.400 --> 03:23:24.900
Because Michael Scott is gone.

03:23:24.900 --> 03:23:29.400
His employee ID is no longer inside of 
our Employee table.

03:23:29.400 --> 03:23:32.900
And this is what I’m going to talk to you guys 
about today, 

03:23:32.900 --> 03:23:37.400
which is different things that we can do 
in order to handle this situation.

03:23:37.400 --> 03:23:40.400
So, specifically, I’m going to talk to you guys 
about two things.

03:23:40.400 --> 03:23:42.100
One is called ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:23:42.100 --> 03:23:44.700
And the other is called ON DELETE CASCADE.

03:23:44.700 --> 03:23:50.000
So, there’s really two things that we can do 
when this situation occurs.

03:23:50.000 --> 03:23:52.900
And so, the first thing would be ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:23:52.900 --> 03:23:58.700
And ON DELETE SET NULL is basically 
where if we delete one of these employees, 

03:23:58.700 --> 03:24:05.200
that means that the manager ID that was associated 
to that employee is going to get set to NULL.

03:24:05.200 --> 03:24:10.300
ON DELETE CASCADE is essentially 
where if we delete the employee 

03:24:10.300 --> 03:24:13.000
whose ID is stored in the manager ID column, 

03:24:13.000 --> 03:24:16.700
then we're just going to delete 
this entire row in the database.

03:24:16.700 --> 03:24:20.800
So, I’m going to go ahead 
and show you guys basically how this works.

03:24:20.800 --> 03:24:25.700
So, over here I actually have the code 
for creating this branch table.

03:24:25.700 --> 03:24:28.700
And this is the code that we used 
in one of the previous videos 

03:24:28.700 --> 03:24:31.600
when I was showing you guys 
how to create this database.

03:24:31.600 --> 03:24:34.300
You'll notice over here on the Branch table it says 

03:24:34.300 --> 03:24:40.000
FOREIGN KEY (mgr_id) REFERENCES 
employee(emp_id).

03:24:40.000 --> 03:24:43.500
And over here I said ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:24:43.500 --> 03:24:49.800
Basically, what I’m saying here is that if 
the employee ID in the Employee table gets deleted, 

03:24:49.800 --> 03:24:53.700
I want to set the manager ID equal to NULL.

03:24:53.700 --> 03:24:56.400
And so, let me show you guys 
how this is going to work.

03:24:56.400 --> 03:24:59.900
So, over here I’m going to go ahead 
and delete Michael Scott from the database 

03:24:59.900 --> 03:25:01.900
and we'll see what happens.

03:25:01.900 --> 03:25:10.900
And so, I’m just going to go ahead and type out 
DELETE FROM employee WHERE emp_id is equal to – 

03:25:10.900 --> 03:25:14.400
and we're just going to put Michael Scott’s 
employee ID which is 102.

03:25:14.400 --> 03:25:15.800
And I’m going to go ahead and run this.

03:25:15.800 --> 03:25:18.900
And you'll see over here it says 1 rows affected.

03:25:18.900 --> 03:25:23.000
But I want to show you guys 
what happened inside of the branch.

03:25:23.000 --> 03:25:26.300
So, I’m just going to SELECT ALL from branch.

03:25:26.300 --> 03:25:28.500
And let’s go ahead and run this.

03:25:28.500 --> 03:25:34.000
You'll see down here the manager ID 
is now set to NULL.

03:25:34.000 --> 03:25:38.700
And that’s because over here – 
because we deleted Michael Scott, right?

03:25:38.700 --> 03:25:42.400
So, we deleted the Michael Scott, 
the 102 entry in the employee table.

03:25:42.400 --> 03:25:48.800
And so, now the manager ID which was storing that as 
a foreign key is just going to be set equal to NULL.

03:25:48.800 --> 03:25:51.600
And that’s because that’s what we defined up here.

03:25:51.600 --> 03:25:56.500
So, in a situation like that, 
because we said ON DELETE SET NULL, 

03:25:56.500 --> 03:25:59.400
now that entry is just going to be equal to NULL.

03:25:59.400 --> 03:26:03.100
And the same thing actually happened 
inside of the Employee table.

03:26:03.100 --> 03:26:10.300
So, if I was to SELECT ALL from 
the Employee table and I ran this, 

03:26:10.300 --> 03:26:14.800
you'll see now that a lot of these supervisor ID’s 
are also set equal to NULL.

03:26:14.800 --> 03:26:18.900
And if you remember back to 
when we created the company database, 

03:26:18.900 --> 03:26:26.200
when we created the employee table, the super_id 
also had ON DELETE SET NULL associated to it.

03:26:26.200 --> 03:26:29.900
And so, that’s why when we deleted Michael Scott, 
all of the employees, 

03:26:29.900 --> 03:26:34.600
namely these three employees right here 
who had Michael Scott as their supervisor, 

03:26:34.600 --> 03:26:38.300
you can see all these employees were 
at branch number 2.

03:26:38.300 --> 03:26:42.600
All of their super ID’s ended up getting set equal to 
NULL because we had ON DELETE SET NULL there.

03:26:42.600 --> 03:26:45.300
So, that is ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:26:45.300 --> 03:26:46.900
And that’s basically how that works.

03:26:46.900 --> 03:26:49.800
I want to show you guys also 
how ON DELETE CASCADE works.

03:26:49.800 --> 03:26:51.800
So, we have this Branch Supplier table.

03:26:51.800 --> 03:26:55.300
And the Branch Supplier table also 
had a foreign key like this, 

03:26:55.300 --> 03:26:59.200
but instead of saying ON DELETE SET NULL, 
we said ON DELETE CASCADE.

03:26:59.200 --> 03:27:05.500
And when we use ON DELETE CASCADE, 
what that means is that if the branch ID 

03:27:05.500 --> 03:27:09.800
that’s stored as the foreign key 
in the Branch Supplier table gets deleted, 

03:27:09.800 --> 03:27:13.700
then we're just going to delete the entire row 
in the database.

03:27:13.700 --> 03:27:19.000
So, down here in Branch Supplier, 
you'll see that I have all of this stuff, right?

03:27:19.000 --> 03:27:24.800
So, I have like Hammer Mill supplies paper 
to branch_id 2.

03:27:24.800 --> 03:27:28.500
Or Uni-ball supplies writing utensils 
to branch_id number 2.

03:27:28.500 --> 03:27:34.600
If I was to delete Branch 2, in other words, 
if I was to delete the branch that had an ID of 2, 

03:27:34.600 --> 03:27:39.500
then all of the rows that had branch_id 2 here 
would just get deleted.

03:27:39.500 --> 03:27:41.300
So, I’m going to show you guys how that works.

03:27:41.300 --> 03:27:44.200
So, over here, we're going to go ahead and do that.

03:27:44.200 --> 03:27:50.600
So, we're just going to DELETE FROM branch 
WHERE branch_id is equal to 2.

03:27:50.600 --> 03:27:54.200
And so, when I go ahead and delete this, 
what’s going to happen 

03:27:54.200 --> 03:28:00.000
is all of those branch supplier rows are going to 
get deleted that had 2 as their foreign key.

03:28:00.000 --> 03:28:04.100
So, now I’ll just SELECT ALL from branch_supplier.

03:28:04.100 --> 03:28:11.200
And when I run this query, you'll notice 
that there’s no longer any branch ID’s 2 in here.

03:28:11.200 --> 03:28:16.800
In other words, we got rid of all of the suppliers 
that supplied Branch 2 when we deleted Branch 2.

03:28:16.800 --> 03:28:19.400
And that what’s ON DELETE CASCADE 
is going to do for us.

03:28:19.400 --> 03:28:23.500
Instead of just setting those equal to NULL, 
it’s going to go ahead and just delete them entirely.

03:28:23.500 --> 03:28:26.600
So, now that we kind of understand 
the difference between those two, 

03:28:26.600 --> 03:28:29.500
I want to talk to you guys about the different 
situations where we might use them.

03:28:29.500 --> 03:28:36.000
And actually, the Branch Supplier table 
and the Branch table are actually really good examples.

03:28:36.000 --> 03:28:39.900
So, in the Branch table we used ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:28:39.900 --> 03:28:43.800
And it was okay for us to use ON DELETE SET NULL 

03:28:43.800 --> 03:28:48.900
because the manager ID on the Branch table 
is just a foreign key.

03:28:48.900 --> 03:28:51.100
It’s not actually a primary key.

03:28:51.100 --> 03:28:57.100
And so, the manager ID isn’t like 
absolutely essential for the Branch Table.

03:28:57.100 --> 03:29:02.000
However, if we look down here 
in the Branch Supplier table, 

03:29:02.000 --> 03:29:08.300
you'll notice that the branch_id, in other words, 
the foreign key here is also part of the primary key.

03:29:08.300 --> 03:29:11.800
Which means the branch ID 
on the Branch Supplier table 

03:29:11.800 --> 03:29:16.100
is absolutely crucial for this row 
in the database, right?

03:29:16.100 --> 03:29:20.400
And so, if the branch ID here, 
if this branch disappears, 

03:29:20.400 --> 03:29:27.000
we can’t set this to NULL because 
a primary key can’t have a NULL value, right?

03:29:27.000 --> 03:29:28.800
And so, this can’t be NULL.

03:29:28.800 --> 03:29:30.900
You have to just delete the entire thing.

03:29:30.900 --> 03:29:35.400
And so, that’s why we use ON DELETE CASCADE 
as opposed to ON DELETE SET NULL.

03:29:35.400 --> 03:29:39.500
And honestly, you know, 
it’s really up to you which one you want to use.

03:29:39.500 --> 03:29:42.700
But just know that if you have a situation 
like Branch Supplier, 

03:29:42.700 --> 03:29:47.800
where a foreign key is also a primary key 
or also a component of a primary key, 

03:29:47.800 --> 03:29:53.400
then it always has to be ON DELETE CASCADE 
otherwise you're going to run into trouble.

03:29:53.400 --> 03:29:56.600
So, that’s the basics of those different ON DELETEs.

03:29:56.600 --> 03:29:58.900
So, ON DELETE SET NULL and ON DELETE CASCADE.

03:29:58.900 --> 03:30:04.600
And both of those are extremely useful when we're 
defining foreign key relationships between tables.

03:30:04.600 --> 03:30:08.100
[Triggers]

03:30:08.100 --> 03:30:12.400
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys 
about using triggers in SQL and MySQL.

03:30:12.400 --> 03:30:18.500
Well, a trigger is basically a block of SQL code 
which we can write 

03:30:18.500 --> 03:30:22.600
which will define a certain action that should happen 

03:30:22.600 --> 03:30:26.500
when a certain operation gets performed 
on the database.

03:30:26.500 --> 03:30:30.800
So, I could write a trigger which would 
basically tell MySQL to do something 

03:30:30.800 --> 03:30:36.300
when like an entry was added 
into a particular table in the database.

03:30:36.300 --> 03:30:38.600
Or when something was deleted 
from a database table.

03:30:38.600 --> 03:30:43.900
And basically I can say like Hey, anytime, you know, 
a row gets deleted from this table, 

03:30:43.900 --> 03:30:46.300
I want you to like insert something 
into something else.

03:30:46.300 --> 03:30:50.600
So, triggers can be extremely 
powerful and extremely useful.

03:30:50.600 --> 03:30:53.300
So, I'm going to show you guys 
basically how they work 

03:30:53.300 --> 03:30:55.400
and we'll just talk about like 
setting them up and everything.

03:30:55.400 --> 03:31:00.700
So, the first thing we have to do, at least to follow 
along with this tutorial is we're going to create a table.

03:31:00.700 --> 03:31:02.800
And you don’t have to create this table.

03:31:02.800 --> 03:31:05.200
I’m just doing this so we can illustrate 
what’s happening.

03:31:05.200 --> 03:31:07.700
But this is not necessary for triggers.

03:31:07.700 --> 03:31:09.900
But I’m creating a table called trigger_test.

03:31:09.900 --> 03:31:13.800
And it’s just going to have one column 
which is just going to be a message.

03:31:13.800 --> 03:31:15.700
And I’m just going to go ahead and create this.

03:31:15.700 --> 03:31:18.500
So, we're creating this table trigger test.

03:31:18.500 --> 03:31:22.200
And now what we can do 
is we can start writing out some triggers.

03:31:22.200 --> 03:31:28.500
Now, when we're using MySQL – and up to this point 
in this course we've been using this program PopSQL 

03:31:28.500 --> 03:31:33.300
which is actually an awesome program for, you know, 
writing out different SQL commands.

03:31:33.300 --> 03:31:36.900
And it’s been really great because it’s, you know, 
a bit easy for us to visual stuff.

03:31:36.900 --> 03:31:39.000
But when we're going to write triggers, 

03:31:39.000 --> 03:31:42.900
we're going to have to define 
the triggers over here in the command line.

03:31:42.900 --> 03:31:47.600
And that’s just because 
there’s one special thing that we have to do 

03:31:47.600 --> 03:31:52.100
which is change the SQL delimiter 
that we're going to use.

03:31:52.100 --> 03:31:53.700
And I’ll talk to you guys about that in a second.

03:31:53.700 --> 03:31:56.800
But in order to do that, we're going to 
have to do it inside of the terminal.

03:31:56.800 --> 03:32:03.100
So, if you're on Windows you can just go down 
and open up the MySQL Command Line Client.

03:32:03.100 --> 03:32:05.000
So, it’s this guy right here.

03:32:05.000 --> 03:32:06.200
That’s what I have open.

03:32:06.200 --> 03:32:07.900
It might ask you to log in.

03:32:07.900 --> 03:32:12.600
If you're on the OSX and you're using Terminal, 
you can just type in – 

03:32:12.600 --> 03:32:18.900
if you just type in MySQL -u root -p.

03:32:18.900 --> 03:32:22.800
And then hit Enter, it should prompt you for your 
password and then you should be logged in.

03:32:22.800 --> 03:32:25.900
And so, that’s how you can 
get to this screen over here.

03:32:25.900 --> 03:32:28.500
And then once we're in here 
we're going to want to use the database.

03:32:28.500 --> 03:32:30.600
So, I’m just going to say use giraffe.

03:32:30.600 --> 03:32:35.100
And giraffe is the name of the database 
that I created like in the first tutorial.

03:32:35.100 --> 03:32:38.400
So, whatever the database you created was, 
you can just use that.

03:32:38.400 --> 03:32:41.400
And then over here, so once we have all that set up, 

03:32:41.400 --> 03:32:43.700
now we're ready to go 
and start creating these triggers.

03:32:43.700 --> 03:32:50.400
So, I need to actually execute the trigger code 
over here inside of the command line, 

03:32:50.400 --> 03:32:54.700
but we can actually just write it out over here inside 
PopSQL so it’s a little bit easier to see.

03:32:54.700 --> 03:32:58.800
I’m actually just going to show you guys 
some different triggers 

03:32:58.800 --> 03:33:00.500
and then I’ll kind of talk to you about it.

03:33:00.500 --> 03:33:02.900
So, I’m going to go ahead and paste one over here.

03:33:02.900 --> 03:33:06.600
And this is actually a very simple trigger.

03:33:06.600 --> 03:33:09.800
So, the trigger is actually right here, 
what I have highlighted.

03:33:09.800 --> 03:33:12.200
And then you'll see over here, I’m saying DELIMITER.

03:33:12.200 --> 03:33:15.100
So, I'm going to talk to you guys about the trigger first 

03:33:15.100 --> 03:33:17.800
and then I’ll talk to you guys 
about what that delimiter is doing.

03:33:17.800 --> 03:33:21.700
So, we can basically create a trigger 
by saying CREATE and then I can say TRIGGER.

03:33:21.700 --> 03:33:22.600
We're going to give this a name.

03:33:22.600 --> 03:33:24.200
I’m just going to call it my_trigger.

03:33:24.200 --> 03:33:31.200
And I can say BEFORE INSERT ON employee, 
FOR EACH ROW BEGIN INSERT INTO trigger_test.

03:33:31.200 --> 03:33:32.600
So, what does all this mean?

03:33:32.600 --> 03:33:34.100
Basically, I’m defining my trigger.

03:33:34.100 --> 03:33:35.100
I’m giving it a name.

03:33:35.100 --> 03:33:40.200
And I’m saying that before something 
gets inserted on the Employee table, 

03:33:40.200 --> 03:33:44.900
so before anything, you know, any new items 
gets inserted on the Employee table, 

03:33:44.900 --> 03:33:49.400
for each of the new items that are getting inserted, 

03:33:49.400 --> 03:33:54.900
I want to insert into the trigger test table 
the values ‘added new employee’.

03:33:54.900 --> 03:33:58.400
So, basically what happens 
is when I define this trigger, 

03:33:58.400 --> 03:34:02.700
that means that before anything gets 
inserted on the employee table now, 

03:34:02.700 --> 03:34:06.200
I’m going to go ahead 
and preform whatever is down here.

03:34:06.200 --> 03:34:10.300
And in our case, I’m just inserting into trigger tests, 
the values, ‘added new employee’.

03:34:10.300 --> 03:34:11.500
So, that’s basically all it is.

03:34:11.500 --> 03:34:17.000
We're basically configuring MySQL to insert 
a value into the Trigger Test table 

03:34:17.000 --> 03:34:20.700
whenever a value gets inserted 
into the Employee table.

03:34:20.700 --> 03:34:24.600
And this can be really useful 
because it automates things, right?

03:34:24.600 --> 03:34:30.600
I can automate something that happens every time 
a record gets inserted into the employee table.

03:34:30.600 --> 03:34:33.400
Now, over here we have these little DELIMITERs.

03:34:33.400 --> 03:34:36.100
And this DELIMITER is actually 
a special keyword in MySQL.

03:34:36.100 --> 03:34:39.800
What this will do is it’ll change the MySQL delimiter.

03:34:39.800 --> 03:34:43.900
So, normally, the MySQL delimiter 
is a semicolon, right?

03:34:43.900 --> 03:34:47.100
So, if I said like SELECT ALL from employee.

03:34:47.100 --> 03:34:49.200
I would end this off with a semicolon.

03:34:49.200 --> 03:34:51.000
That’s the delimiter, right?

03:34:51.000 --> 03:34:53.100
That delimits the different SQL commands.

03:34:53.100 --> 03:34:57.000
But when we're writing these triggers out, 
you'll notice that over here 

03:34:57.000 --> 03:35:02.300
inside of these FOR EACH and this END 
I have to use this semicolon over here.

03:35:02.300 --> 03:35:08.000
And so, because I’m using the semicolon 
to end off this SQL command right here, 

03:35:08.000 --> 03:35:14.800
I can’t actually use that same delimiter 
in order to end off the trigger creation.

03:35:14.800 --> 03:35:19.500
So, you have to put the semicolon here 
in order for this to work.

03:35:19.500 --> 03:35:24.800
But if I don’t change the delimiter, 
then this semicolon is basically going to tell SQL 

03:35:24.800 --> 03:35:27.700
that we're done creating the trigger, 
even though we're clearly not.

03:35:27.700 --> 03:35:31.800
And so, what I’m doing up here 
is I’m changing the delimiter to two dollar signs.

03:35:31.800 --> 03:35:35.000
So, basically now instead of the delimiter 
being a semicolon, 

03:35:35.000 --> 03:35:36.700
the delimiter is going to be two dollar signs.

03:35:36.700 --> 03:35:41.200
And you'll see, I create the trigger 
and then I'm using the two dollar signs 

03:35:41.200 --> 03:35:43.800
to delineate that the trigger is done being created.

03:35:43.800 --> 03:35:48.100
And then I can just delinear back to a semicolon.

03:35:48.100 --> 03:35:51.400
Now, the reason that I have to 
do this over here in the terminal 

03:35:51.400 --> 03:35:55.100
is because in PopSQL you can’t 
actually configure the delimiter.

03:35:55.100 --> 03:36:00.600
So, the delimiter is actually something 
that’s defined not on the like text editor level.

03:36:00.600 --> 03:36:01.800
It’s defined like over here.

03:36:01.800 --> 03:36:04.500
So, basically we have to execute this over there.

03:36:04.500 --> 03:36:10.000
So, what I’m going to do now is I’m actually going 
to execute all of these pieces of SQL code over here.

03:36:10.000 --> 03:36:11.800
So, I’m just going to change the delimiter.

03:36:11.800 --> 03:36:13.000
So, I'm going to paste this in.

03:36:13.000 --> 03:36:14.000
I'll hit Enter.

03:36:14.000 --> 03:36:18.900
And now I’m going to paste in the actually part 
where I’m creating the trigger.

03:36:18.900 --> 03:36:21.000
So, over here we'll paste this.

03:36:21.000 --> 03:36:22.800
And I’m just going to hit Enter.

03:36:22.800 --> 03:36:25.600
And then finally, we're going to change 
the DELIMITER back.

03:36:25.600 --> 03:36:27.900
So, I’m going to change this back to a semicolon.

03:36:27.900 --> 03:36:31.600
So, hopefully now this trigger is all set up 
inside of MySQL.

03:36:31.600 --> 03:36:35.700
So, one thing we can do to test it 
is just to add in another employee.

03:36:35.700 --> 03:36:40.400
So, I'm going to go ahead 
and add another employee into the Employees table.

03:36:40.400 --> 03:36:43.000
So, we're going to add in Oscar Martinez.

03:36:43.000 --> 03:36:45.900
And let’s go ahead and do that.

03:36:45.900 --> 03:36:47.500
And so, we added in Oscar.

03:36:47.500 --> 03:36:52.400
And now what I’m going to do is SELECT 
from the Trigger Test table.

03:36:52.400 --> 03:36:55.900
So, assuming our trigger got set up correctly, 

03:36:55.900 --> 03:36:59.200
when we inserted an employee 
into the Employee table, 

03:36:59.200 --> 03:37:04.100
it should have also inserted something 
into Trigger Test that said Added New Employee.

03:37:04.100 --> 03:37:07.600
So, let’s go ahead and run this SELECT statement 
and we'll see what happens.

03:37:07.600 --> 03:37:11.100
So, you'll see down here we get a message that says 
added new employee.

03:37:11.100 --> 03:37:12.300
So, it looks like it worked, right?

03:37:12.300 --> 03:37:17.800
The trigger got set up correctly and therefore when 
we inserted something into the Employee table 

03:37:17.800 --> 03:37:22.500
we actually ended up updating the Trigger Test table 
with a new entry as well.

03:37:22.500 --> 03:37:26.800
And so, that is basically how we can use 
triggers to do something like that.

03:37:26.800 --> 03:37:30.100
So, I want to show you guys 
a couple other things we can do with triggers.

03:37:30.100 --> 03:37:32.800
I’ll show you guys another one right now.

03:37:32.800 --> 03:37:36.300
I’m actually going to, again, paste it 
and then we'll kind of talk about it.

03:37:36.300 --> 03:37:40.300
So, this one is actually very similar 
to the one which just made.

03:37:40.300 --> 03:37:45.100
But instead of over here, 
saying like added new employee.

03:37:45.100 --> 03:37:48.500
Instead, I'm saying NEW.first_name.

03:37:48.500 --> 03:37:50.300
And so, what this is allowing me to do 

03:37:50.300 --> 03:37:55.900
is it’s actually allowing me to access a particular 
attribute about the thing that we just inserted.

03:37:55.900 --> 03:37:59.100
So, again, we're inserting something 
on the employee table.

03:37:59.100 --> 03:38:02.200
NEW is going to refer to the row 
that’s getting inserted.

03:38:02.200 --> 03:38:05.700
And then I can access specific columns from that row.

03:38:05.700 --> 03:38:11.200
So, NEW.first_name will give me the first name 
of the employee that’s getting inserted.

03:38:11.200 --> 03:38:16.300
So, now if I was to come down here and 
I’m actually just going to insert another employee.

03:38:16.300 --> 03:38:18.800
So, we're going to insert Kevin Malone.

03:38:18.800 --> 03:38:20.800
And let’s go ahead and do that.

03:38:20.800 --> 03:38:21.900
And actually, whoops.

03:38:21.900 --> 03:38:24.300
I have to update the trigger over here.

03:38:24.300 --> 03:38:26.400
So, once again, I’m going to do the same thing.

03:38:26.400 --> 03:38:30.900
I’m just going to paste in all of this code over here 
on the command line.

03:38:30.900 --> 03:38:32.300
So, we'll paste in the trigger.

03:38:32.300 --> 03:38:35.000
And actually need to change the name 
on this real quick.

03:38:35.000 --> 03:38:38.600
So, we'll say my_trigger1 
is what we're going to call that.

03:38:38.600 --> 03:38:40.300
And that’s going to go ahead.

03:38:40.300 --> 03:38:44.400
And then we'll change the delimiter 
back to a semicolon.

03:38:44.400 --> 03:38:48.100
All right, so now let’s go ahead 
and add in our Kevin Malone employee.

03:38:48.100 --> 03:38:49.300
So, I’m going to run this.

03:38:49.300 --> 03:38:50.500
So, we added Kevin.

03:38:50.500 --> 03:38:52.600
Now if we SELECT ALL from Trigger Test, 

03:38:52.600 --> 03:38:57.900
you'll see down here not only did we add 
a new employee, it says added new employee.

03:38:57.900 --> 03:38:59.700
That was that first trigger that we set up.

03:38:59.700 --> 03:39:02.800
But we also added the employees name 
which was Kevin, right?

03:39:02.800 --> 03:39:06.700
So, we were able to grab a specific piece 
of information from the new row 

03:39:06.700 --> 03:39:09.800
that got inserted and that’s going 
to show up down there.

03:39:09.800 --> 03:39:13.400
All right, so there’s one more thing 
I want to show you with these triggers.

03:39:13.400 --> 03:39:15.900
And it’s actually going to be a more complex trigger.

03:39:15.900 --> 03:39:18.900
So, this is how we can use conditionals.

03:39:18.900 --> 03:39:21.800
So, I can use something like IF, ELSEIF, and ELSE.

03:39:21.800 --> 03:39:26.300
So, over here we have this trigger.

03:39:26.300 --> 03:39:29.800
So, it’s basically the same exact thing 
as we did before.

03:39:29.800 --> 03:39:32.800
TRIGGER my_trigger BEFORE INSERT ON employee.

03:39:32.800 --> 03:39:34.100
And then for each row.

03:39:34.100 --> 03:39:35.700
This time we're using an IF statement.

03:39:35.700 --> 03:39:39.200
So, I’m saying IF NEW.sex is equal to male, 

03:39:39.200 --> 03:39:43.100
THEN INSERT INTO trigger_test VALUES 
added male employee.

03:39:43.100 --> 03:39:48.800
ELSEIF NEW.sex is equal to F, INSERT 
INTO trigger_test added female.

03:39:48.800 --> 03:39:52.500
ELSE INSERT INTO trigger_test, 
added other employee.

03:39:52.500 --> 03:39:53.500
So, we're using IF ELSE logic.

03:39:53.500 --> 03:39:57.800
And basically, it’s just if this condition up here is true, 
then we do this.

03:39:57.800 --> 03:39:59.900
Otherwise, we check this condition.

03:39:59.900 --> 03:40:01.100
If that’s true, we do this.

03:40:01.100 --> 03:40:02.300
Otherwise, we do that.

03:40:02.300 --> 03:40:06.000
So, if you’ve ever programmed before, 
then you're probably familiar with an IF statement.

03:40:06.000 --> 03:40:12.100
So, this is a very special type of trigger 
because we're using conditionals.

03:40:12.100 --> 03:40:17.500
So, I’m going to go ahead 
and we'll put this one over here on the terminal.

03:40:17.500 --> 03:40:19.100
So, change the DELIMITER.

03:40:19.100 --> 03:40:23.200
And then we're going to put this guy over here.

03:40:23.200 --> 03:40:24.400
And whoops.

03:40:24.400 --> 03:40:26.300
Again, I forgot to change the name.

03:40:26.300 --> 03:40:27.500
So, this will be called trigger2.

03:40:27.500 --> 03:40:29.300
And put this over here.

03:40:29.300 --> 03:40:33.200
And then finally, we're just going to 
change the DEMILITER back.

03:40:33.200 --> 03:40:36.500
All right, so now lets – again, 
we're going to insert an employee.

03:40:36.500 --> 03:40:39.000
So, I’m going to go ahead 
and insert a female employee.

03:40:39.000 --> 03:40:41.200
So, why don’t we insert Pam Beesly.

03:40:41.200 --> 03:40:44.300
So, Pam Beesly is going to be a female.

03:40:44.300 --> 03:40:51.200
Which means when we insert Pam Beesly, hopefully 
it should say added female into the Trigger Test table.

03:40:51.200 --> 03:40:52.500
So, I’m going to run this.

03:40:52.500 --> 03:40:54.100
And we added the employee.

03:40:54.100 --> 03:40:55.900
Now, let’s SELECT ALL FROM trigger_test.

03:40:55.900 --> 03:40:59.000
And so all of these triggers are actually going to 
compound on each other.

03:40:59.000 --> 03:41:01.500
So, we should have quite a few entries in here.

03:41:01.500 --> 03:41:05.000
So, we'll see when we added Pam, 
it said added new employee, Pam.

03:41:05.000 --> 03:41:06.200
Added female.

03:41:06.200 --> 03:41:09.900
So, that third trigger that we just created 
actually ended up working.

03:41:09.900 --> 03:41:13.100
So, you'll notice over here 
we've been creating triggers for INSERT.

03:41:13.100 --> 03:41:18.000
But you can also create triggers for UPDATE 
and you can also make one for DELETE.

03:41:18.000 --> 03:41:21.900
So, anytime they're trying to INSERT, UPDATE, 
or DELETE, you can create a trigger.

03:41:21.900 --> 03:41:26.000
So, you can also do – in addition to BEFORE 
you could also do AFTER.

03:41:26.000 --> 03:41:31.400
So, in certain circumstances 
you won’t want to insert into trigger_test before.

03:41:31.400 --> 03:41:32.600
You’d want to INSERT AFTER.

03:41:32.600 --> 03:41:35.200
And you can go ahead and control it just like that.

03:41:35.200 --> 03:41:39.700
So, but basically, that’s all the, you know, 
the main stuff that we can do with triggers.

03:41:39.700 --> 03:41:41.500
These are very, very useful.

03:41:41.500 --> 03:41:44.800
And they'll allow you to do a bunch of cool stuff.

03:41:44.800 --> 03:41:46.800
We can also drop a trigger.

03:41:46.800 --> 03:41:51.666
So, I can say like over here in the terminal 
I can just say DROP TRIGGER.

03:41:46.800 --> 03:41:56.200
And it would be like my_trigger.

03:41:56.200 --> 03:41:58.000
So, this will drop my_trigger.

03:41:58.000 --> 03:42:01.700
And now, my_trigger is no longer going to be active.

03:42:01.700 --> 03:42:04.100
So, triggers are, like I said, very useful.

03:42:04.100 --> 03:42:08.000
And it’s a really great way to kind of control 
what happens when other stuff happens.

03:42:08.000 --> 03:42:11.700
And you can automate a lot of the stuff 
on the backend of your database.

03:42:11.700 --> 03:42:16.000
[ER Diagrams Intro]

03:42:16.000 --> 03:42:18.700
In this tutorial I’m going to talk to you guys 
about ER diagrams.

03:42:18.700 --> 03:42:22.600
More specifically I’m just going to give you guys 
an introduction to ER diagrams.

03:42:22.600 --> 03:42:26.000
And we'll talk about how ER diagrams 
are put together, 

03:42:26.000 --> 03:42:29.500
all the different symbols in the ER diagrams 
and what they represent.

03:42:29.500 --> 03:42:32.400
Now, when you're designing a database, 

03:42:32.400 --> 03:42:36.100
one of the most important things 
is designing a database schema.

03:42:36.100 --> 03:42:41.300
And a database schema is basically just all 
the different tables and the different attributes 

03:42:41.300 --> 03:42:43.400
that are going to be on those tables.

03:42:43.400 --> 03:42:46.000
So, maybe you have some requirements 
for the different data 

03:42:46.000 --> 03:42:48.300
that needs to get stored inside of your database 

03:42:48.300 --> 03:42:51.300
and the different relationships that 
that data is going to have.

03:42:51.300 --> 03:42:55.000
Well, you can use an ER diagrams 
to act as a middleman 

03:42:55.000 --> 03:43:00.400
between database or storage requirements 
and the actual database schema 

03:43:00.400 --> 03:43:03.000
that’s going to get implemented 
in the database management system.

03:43:03.000 --> 03:43:07.300
So, an ER diagram is a great way 
to kind of take, you know, 

03:43:07.300 --> 03:43:10.500
data storage requirements 
like business requirements.

03:43:10.500 --> 03:43:15.600
And sort of convert them 
into an actual database schema.

03:43:15.600 --> 03:43:18.900
So, we can use the ER diagram to map out 
the different relationships 

03:43:18.900 --> 03:43:22.800
and the different entities 
and the different attributes for those entities.

03:43:22.800 --> 03:43:27.700
And it can just be a really great way to organize 
our data into a database schema.

03:43:27.700 --> 03:43:34.000
So, an ER diagram is basically just a little diagram that 
consists of different shapes and symbols and text.

03:43:34.000 --> 03:43:40.000
And it all kind of gets combined together to end up 
defining, you know, a relationship model.

03:43:40.000 --> 03:43:42.600
So, without further ado, let’s get started.

03:43:42.600 --> 03:43:48.300
I’m going to show you guys all the different 
basic parts of an ER diagram 

03:43:48.300 --> 03:43:49.700
and we'll kind of construct our own ER diagram.

03:43:49.700 --> 03:43:54.000
And it’ll kind of give you guys an idea of all the 
different symbols and stuff that we're going to use.

03:43:54.000 --> 03:43:59.000
So, in this example I'm going to be using 
the example of like a school.

03:43:59.000 --> 03:44:03.000
So, let’s say that I’m working for a school 
and my boss comes to me and he’s like, 

03:44:03.000 --> 03:44:10.000
“Hey Mike, I need you to design a database schema or 
I need you to design an ER diagram for our database.”

03:44:10.000 --> 03:44:13.100
So, maybe this database is going to store information 
about different students.

03:44:13.100 --> 03:44:17.000
And then maybe information 
about like the classes that those students take.

03:44:17.000 --> 03:44:20.000
So, let’s start looking at the different parts 
of the ER diagram.

03:44:20.000 --> 03:44:23.700
So, the first thing I want to talk to you guys 
about are entities.

03:44:23.700 --> 03:44:27.200
And an entity is just an object that we want 
to model and store information about.

03:44:27.200 --> 03:44:32.300
So, for our school database we might want 
to store information about a particular student.

03:44:32.300 --> 03:44:37.600
So, inside of our ER diagram we can put an entity 
which is just going to be a square, just like this.

03:44:37.600 --> 03:44:40.900
And then we're going to 
have the name of the entity that we're storing.

03:44:40.900 --> 03:44:42.400
So, it’s going to be student.

03:44:42.400 --> 03:44:44.400
Next we can define attributes.

03:44:44.400 --> 03:44:48.300
So, attributes are specific pieces 
of information about an entity.

03:44:48.300 --> 03:44:50.300
So, over here we have our student entity.

03:44:50.300 --> 03:44:54.600
And then we might want to store like 
the student’s name, the student’s grade number.

03:44:54.600 --> 03:44:56.100
So, like, what grade are they in.

03:44:56.100 --> 03:44:57.300
And then their GPA.

03:44:57.300 --> 03:44:59.300
So, we can store all different types of attributes.

03:44:59.300 --> 03:45:03.200
And we're going to make these little ovals and we're 
going to connect them to the entity just like that.

03:45:03.200 --> 03:45:06.100
So, the attribute is going to have the name 
of the attribute inside of an oval, 

03:45:06.100 --> 03:45:08.600
connected to our square entity.

03:45:08.600 --> 03:45:11.000
We can also define a primary key.

03:45:11.000 --> 03:45:17.200
A primary key is going to be an attribute that’s going 
to uniquely identify an entry in the database table.

03:45:17.200 --> 03:45:20.700
So, you'll see over here I actually colored 
the primary key different.

03:45:20.700 --> 03:45:23.700
Now, generally, for an ER diagram, 
you’re not going to be using colors.

03:45:23.700 --> 03:45:26.600
I just did that so it’s kind of easier 
for us to wrap our minds around.

03:45:26.600 --> 03:45:30.300
But whenever we're defining a primary key, 
we're always going to underline.

03:45:30.300 --> 03:45:33.500
So, a primary key is just like a normal attribute, 
but we're going to underline.

03:45:33.500 --> 03:45:35.700
So, here, our primary key is a student ID.

03:45:35.700 --> 03:45:37.600
And then I just have the GPA.

03:45:37.600 --> 03:45:40.000
So, you know, obviously I could put 
all of those attributes here.

03:45:40.000 --> 03:45:42.500
But I’m just using two for now just  to keep it simple.

03:45:42.500 --> 03:45:46.200
So, we have our primary key, student ID, 
which is underlined, and then we have our GPA.

03:45:46.200 --> 03:45:48.000
And they're both connected to our entity.

03:45:48.000 --> 03:45:51.100
Next, we can define composite attributes.

03:45:51.100 --> 03:45:55.200
So, these would be attributes 
that could be broken up into sub attributes.

03:45:55.200 --> 03:45:57.900
So, for example, if we wanted to store 
the student’s name, 

03:45:57.900 --> 03:46:01.900
but we can also store their first name 
and they're last name.

03:46:01.900 --> 03:46:05.800
So, name could be broken up further 
into first name and last name.

03:46:05.800 --> 03:46:10.300
And so, in the case of a composite attribute you'll 
notice that we have the main attribute here.

03:46:10.300 --> 03:46:11.800
It’s connected to the entity.

03:46:11.800 --> 03:46:15.000
And then off of that main attribute 
we have two other attributes 

03:46:15.000 --> 03:46:17.900
fname and lname 
for first name and last name.

03:46:17.900 --> 03:46:21.800
We can also define a multi-valued attribute.

03:46:21.800 --> 03:46:26.900
So, if there’s any attributes in your data model 
that could have more than one value, 

03:46:26.900 --> 03:46:30.000
then you can put them in a multi-valued attribute 

03:46:30.000 --> 03:46:32.600
which looks just like an attribute 
except we have an extra circle.

03:46:32.600 --> 03:46:33.900
So, it’s just two circles.

03:46:33.900 --> 03:46:35.900
And then inside, the name of the attribute.

03:46:35.900 --> 03:46:36.900
So, clubs, for example.

03:46:36.900 --> 03:46:39.800
Like a student might be involved 
in a bunch of different clubs.

03:46:39.800 --> 03:46:43.300
And so, clubs would be a multi-valued attribute.

03:46:43.300 --> 03:46:45.500
In other words, it could have more than one value.

03:46:45.500 --> 03:46:48.000
Like a student is not going 
to have more than one GPA.

03:46:48.000 --> 03:46:49.600
They're not going to have more than one name.

03:46:49.600 --> 03:46:51.300
They're not going to have more than one student ID.

03:46:51.300 --> 03:46:54.500
But they might have more than 
one club that they belong to.

03:46:54.500 --> 03:46:56.100
So, next step is a derived attribute.

03:46:56.100 --> 03:47:00.600
And a derived attribute is an attribute 
that can be derived from the other attributes 

03:47:00.600 --> 03:47:01.800
that we're keeping track of.

03:47:01.800 --> 03:47:05.300
So, we're not going to actually 
keep track of the derived attribute.

03:47:05.300 --> 03:47:09.400
But it’s just a way that we can sort of notate attributes 

03:47:09.400 --> 03:47:12.100
that could be derived from the attributes 
that we're storing.

03:47:12.100 --> 03:47:14.100
So, down here I have my derived attribute.

03:47:14.100 --> 03:47:16.600
And you'll notice that it’s just an oval 
with these dashed lines.

03:47:16.600 --> 03:47:18.000
It’s called has_honors.

03:47:18.000 --> 03:47:22.900
So, has_honors is an attribute 
that we could derive from this GPA.

03:47:22.900 --> 03:47:27.800
So, maybe the school is going to say 
that anybody with 

03:47:27.800 --> 03:47:29.800
a GPA of 3,500 or above is going to have honors.

03:47:29.800 --> 03:47:33.100
Well, we could derive that just from the GPA.

03:47:33.100 --> 03:47:36.800
So, we're not actually going to be 
keeping track of this attribute, 

03:47:36.800 --> 03:47:41.900
but it’s an attribute that we can derive 
from the GPA that we are keeping track of.

03:47:41.900 --> 03:47:43.500
So, we can just denote it like that.

03:47:43.500 --> 03:47:46.000
And sometimes it’s useful to denote 
our derived attributes.

03:47:46.000 --> 03:47:49.100
So, we can also have multiple entities.

03:47:49.100 --> 03:47:51.300
So, over here you'll see I have my student entity.

03:47:51.300 --> 03:47:54.400
But I can also define another entity 
which would be like class.

03:47:54.400 --> 03:47:59.500
And so, a class would be like a particular class 
that a student is taking, right?

03:47:59.500 --> 03:48:03.400
So, if I was in school, I might take like biology 
or chemistry, right?

03:48:03.400 --> 03:48:05.800
That would be what this class is over here.

03:48:05.800 --> 03:48:11.000
And then you'll see over here we have our primary key 
which is just going to be class ID.

03:48:11.000 --> 03:48:14.000
And so, when we have multiple entities, 

03:48:14.000 --> 03:48:17.300
we’re going to want to define relationships 
between those entities.

03:48:17.300 --> 03:48:19.800
So, what I can do is I can define a relationship.

03:48:19.800 --> 03:48:22.200
And a relationship is basically 
just this diamond over here.

03:48:22.200 --> 03:48:27.500
And the relationship would basically 
denote a student is going to take a class.

03:48:27.500 --> 03:48:30.800
So, a relationship is kind of like a verb, right?

03:48:30.800 --> 03:48:34.500
It’s the student is related to the class 
in some way, right?

03:48:34.500 --> 03:48:36.100
So, the student is going to take a class.

03:48:36.100 --> 03:48:39.200
And a class can be taken by students.

03:48:39.200 --> 03:48:41.200
So, you can read this both ways.

03:48:41.200 --> 03:48:46.200
You can say the student takes a class 
or you can say that the class is taken by a student.

03:48:46.200 --> 03:48:49.400
And we can also define participation.

03:48:49.400 --> 03:48:53.900
So, you'll notice that the relationship 
I’m connecting the two entities using these lines.

03:48:53.900 --> 03:48:56.800
So, the student is connected to the relationship 
using a single line.

03:48:56.800 --> 03:48:59.900
And the classes connected to the relationship 
using a double line.

03:48:59.900 --> 03:49:01.700
So, when you're defining relationships 

03:49:01.700 --> 03:49:06.600
you can define the participation 
of the particular entities to that relationship.

03:49:06.600 --> 03:49:11.000
So, when I use a single line, 
this indications partial participation.

03:49:11.000 --> 03:49:15.100
What this  means is that 
not all students need to take a class.

03:49:15.100 --> 03:49:17.000
So, when I use the single line.

03:49:17.000 --> 03:49:21.100
I’m basically saying that only some 
of the students have to take a class, right?

03:49:21.100 --> 03:49:23.800
Not all students necessarily have to be taking a class.

03:49:23.800 --> 03:49:28.100
When I use this double line, 
it indicates total participation.

03:49:28.100 --> 03:49:34.000
Which means that all of the classes need to be taken 
by at least a single student, right?

03:49:34.000 --> 03:49:40.000
So, that means all classes must participate 
in this Takes relationship.

03:49:40.000 --> 03:49:43.000
So, all classes need to have students 
that are taking them.

03:49:43.000 --> 03:49:46.500
So, you couldn't have a class 
that has no students taking it.

03:49:46.500 --> 03:49:50.000
All classes have to have students that are taking it.

03:49:50.000 --> 03:49:53.500
And, you know, maybe that’s not 
what you’d want in your database.

03:49:53.500 --> 03:49:56.500
But in this case, that’s how 
we can denote something like that.

03:49:56.500 --> 03:50:03.300
So, I could use total participation to denote that 
all classes need to participate in this relationship.

03:50:03.300 --> 03:50:07.800
In other words, all classes need 
to have a student taking the class.

03:50:07.800 --> 03:50:11.700
So, that’s basically how we can define relationships.

03:50:11.700 --> 03:50:14.700
And then obviously, partial participation 
and total participation.

03:50:14.700 --> 03:50:20.300
And so, over here, we can also define attributes 
about a particular relationship.

03:50:20.300 --> 03:50:21.900
So, we have our Takes relationship.

03:50:21.900 --> 03:50:27.400
And you'll notice that I’m defining at attribute 
about this relationship which is grade.

03:50:27.400 --> 03:50:33.100
So, a student will take a class and the student 
will get a particular grade for that class, right?

03:50:33.100 --> 03:50:36.300
So, I might take biology and maybe I get 
like a B+ in biology.

03:50:36.300 --> 03:50:39.800
Well, that grade isn’t necessarily stored 
on the student entity 

03:50:39.800 --> 03:50:42.300
and it isn’t necessarily stored on the class entity.

03:50:42.300 --> 03:50:44.100
It’s stored on the relationship, right?

03:50:44.100 --> 03:50:48.400
So, the only way I can get a grade from a class 
is if I take it, right?

03:50:48.400 --> 03:50:53.200
And so, that’s why the relationship attribute 
is stored on the relationship.

03:50:53.200 --> 03:50:55.300
And sometimes that'll come in handy.

03:50:55.300 --> 03:50:59.400
And so, we can also define relationship cardinality.

03:50:59.400 --> 03:51:03.900
And relationship cardinality is the number of instances 
of an entity from a relationship 

03:51:03.900 --> 03:51:05.700
that can be associated with the relation.

03:51:05.700 --> 03:51:08.200
Now, I understand that’s 
a very confusing definition.

03:51:08.200 --> 03:51:11.700
And I think relationship cardinality 
is something that trips a lot of people up, 

03:51:11.700 --> 03:51:14.100
so I’m going to try to give you guys 
a good explanation of it.

03:51:14.100 --> 03:51:17.800
So, over here we have a student 
and a student can take a class.

03:51:17.800 --> 03:51:21.100
But we can define relationship cardinalities on that.

03:51:21.100 --> 03:51:26.600
Basically, what this means is that a student 
can take any number of classes.

03:51:26.600 --> 03:51:29.300
So, when we say M, that refers to any number.

03:51:29.300 --> 03:51:33.100
So, a student could take 
basically multiple classes, right?

03:51:33.100 --> 03:51:35.600
A student could take 2, or 3, or 4 classes.

03:51:35.600 --> 03:51:38.100
And we can define the same thing for the class.

03:51:38.100 --> 03:51:42.600
So, we could say a class is taken 
by any number of students, right?

03:51:42.600 --> 03:51:45.800
So, a class can be taken by 5 or 10 or 30 students.

03:51:45.800 --> 03:51:48.100
That’s basically what that would define.

03:51:48.100 --> 03:51:51.600
So, this would be an NM cardinality relationship.

03:51:51.600 --> 03:51:54.400
But we can also define 
other cardinality relationships.

03:51:54.400 --> 03:51:56.300
So, we could say like 1 to 1.

03:51:56.300 --> 03:52:01.600
So, in a 1 to 1 cardinality relationship 
we would say that a student can take one class 

03:52:01.600 --> 03:52:05.300
and a class can be taken by one student.

03:52:05.300 --> 03:52:09.800
We can also say like 1 to N, 
the cardinality relationship, 

03:52:09.800 --> 03:52:15.900
which would be a student could take one class 
and a class could be taken by many students.

03:52:15.900 --> 03:52:20.200
Or, you could reverse it and say a class 
can be taken by one student, 

03:52:20.200 --> 03:52:23.700
but a student can take any number of classes.

03:52:23.700 --> 03:52:28.800
And then again, you guys saw NM which 
would be a student can take any number of classes 

03:52:28.800 --> 03:52:30.900
and a class can be taken by any number of students.

03:52:30.900 --> 03:52:35.400
So, it’s useful to define that relationship 
cardinality in an ER diagram 

03:52:35.400 --> 03:52:41.900
because that’s actually going to relate to 
how we want to design our database schema 

03:52:41.900 --> 03:52:43.800
when it’s eventually time to do that.

03:52:43.800 --> 03:52:48.300
And also, like this is something that 
could be defined in data modeling requirements.

03:52:48.300 --> 03:52:52.800
So, if the requirements comes to you and says 
a student can only take one class at a time, 

03:52:52.800 --> 03:52:56.900
well, that’s something that you want to be 
able to represent inside of the ER diagram.

03:52:56.900 --> 03:52:59.900
So, that’s kind of how 
we can represent relationship cardinality.

03:52:59.900 --> 03:53:03.200
And then finally, the last thing 
I want to show you guys 

03:53:03.200 --> 03:53:06.500
are weak entity types 
and identifying relationships.

03:53:06.500 --> 03:53:08.300
So, actually, I think I’m in the way here.

03:53:08.300 --> 03:53:10.600
But where my head is, it just says class.

03:53:10.600 --> 03:53:12.200
So, you guys kind of saw it before.

03:53:12.200 --> 03:53:17.100
So, a weak entity as entity that cannot 
be uniquely identified by it’s attributes alone.

03:53:17.100 --> 03:53:23.700
Basically, a weak entity is an entity 
that’s going to rely on or depend on another entity.

03:53:23.700 --> 03:53:27.400
So, over here I have an example 
of a weak entity which would be an exam.

03:53:27.400 --> 03:53:31.200
So, a class can have an exam, right?

03:53:31.200 --> 03:53:34.500
So, an exam is something – 
it’s sort of like an entity, right?

03:53:34.500 --> 03:53:37.200
You know, a test or whatever 
that someone might be taking.

03:53:37.200 --> 03:53:39.200
An exam might have an exam ID.

03:53:39.200 --> 03:53:44.100
But in this case, an exam 
can’t exist without a class, right?

03:53:44.100 --> 03:53:50.100
In other words, for an exam to exist, 
it has to be associated with a class, right?

03:53:50.100 --> 03:53:52.900
An exam, you're not just going to like 
have an exam stored, right?

03:53:52.900 --> 03:53:57.000
We're only going to have an exam 
that’s going to be associated with a class.

03:53:57.000 --> 03:54:00.100
So, this is what would we call a weak entity type.

03:54:00.100 --> 03:54:04.800
It’s an entity that cannot be uniquely identified 
by its attributes alone.

03:54:04.800 --> 03:54:08.500
And we can also define an identifying relationship.

03:54:08.500 --> 03:54:13.600
And an identifying relationship is a relationship 
that serves to uniquely identify the weak entity.

03:54:13.600 --> 03:54:18.300
So, an exam can be uniquely identified 
when it’s paired with a class.

03:54:18.300 --> 03:54:22.200
Which I realize, my head is in the way of, 
but you guys know it’s there.

03:54:22.200 --> 03:54:25.300
So, I can say that a class has an exam.

03:54:25.300 --> 03:54:27.700
And an exam is had by a class.

03:54:27.700 --> 03:54:30.400
The exam doesn’t exist on its own.

03:54:30.400 --> 03:54:32.900
It only exists in the context of a class.

03:54:32.900 --> 03:54:35.500
And this is kind of more of an abstract idea.

03:54:35.500 --> 03:54:40.000
And actually, in the next video 
we're going to look more at weak entity types.

03:54:40.000 --> 03:54:44.900
But this should be at least a little bit of an example 
and an introduction into weak entity types 

03:54:44.900 --> 03:54:49.600
and identifying the relationships 
which we're just notating by these double lines.

03:54:49.600 --> 03:54:54.100
So, the exam has double square and the identifying 
relationship has a double triangle.

03:54:54.100 --> 03:54:55.800
And also, one more thing to note.

03:54:55.800 --> 03:54:59.100
Then whenever we have a weak entity 
and identifying relationship, 

03:54:59.100 --> 03:55:03.800
the weak entity always has to have total participation 
in the identifying relationship.

03:55:03.800 --> 03:55:10.100
In other words, all exams must have a class, 
but not all classes need to have an exam.

03:55:10.100 --> 03:55:16.300
All right, so that is kind of all of the sort of basic things 
that you're going to encounter in an ER diagram.

03:55:16.300 --> 03:55:21.800
And really, everything that you see right here 
is kind of like all of the stuff 

03:55:21.800 --> 03:55:23.400
that you might seen an ER diagram.

03:55:23.400 --> 03:55:30.400
And really, you can use all of these different symbols 
in order to represent a data model.

03:55:30.400 --> 03:55:32.600
And what you'll see is we can take this ER diagram 

03:55:32.600 --> 03:55:37.700
and we can actually convert into 
an actual database schema.

03:55:37.700 --> 03:55:41.000
And that’s why these are really 
useful sort of middle-man 

03:55:41.000 --> 03:55:43.500
between requirements 
and the actual database schema.

03:55:43.500 --> 03:55:44.900
So, hopefully that makes sense.

03:55:44.900 --> 03:55:49.800
In the next video we're going to actually walk through 
an example of constructing our own ER diagram, 

03:55:49.800 --> 03:55:51.300
so that should be kind of cool.

03:55:51.300 --> 03:55:53.500
And that should kind of give you guys 
more of an idea of how these work.

03:55:53.500 --> 03:55:57.200
[Designing an ER Diagram]

03:55:57.200 --> 03:56:00.900
In this tutorial I’m going to walk you guys 
through creating an ER diagram 

03:56:00.900 --> 03:56:03.500
based off of some data requirements.

03:56:03.500 --> 03:56:06.200
So, over here I have this document.

03:56:06.200 --> 03:56:08.000
It’s called Company Data Requirements.

03:56:08.000 --> 03:56:13.400
And basically, what this document does 
is it describes all of the different data, 

03:56:13.400 --> 03:56:17.100
all the pieces of information and the relationships 
between those pieces of information.

03:56:17.100 --> 03:56:20.800
And this is a good example 
of a document that, you know, 

03:56:20.800 --> 03:56:24.200
you might get if you’re working for a company 
and they want you to design a database.

03:56:24.200 --> 03:56:27.700
Let’s say that they want you to design a database 
to store information about a company.

03:56:27.700 --> 03:56:30.400
Well, they might give you this document.

03:56:30.400 --> 03:56:36.100
And then your job would be to take this document 
and convert it into a database schema 

03:56:36.100 --> 03:56:38.800
which you can then, you know, 
store information in and all that.

03:56:38.800 --> 03:56:44.300
So, this document will describe all the data and 
it will describe the relationships between the data.

03:56:44.300 --> 03:56:47.200
But it will do it in, you know, in English, right?

03:56:47.200 --> 03:56:49.000
It’ll do it in a very high-level manner.

03:56:49.000 --> 03:56:53.300
You know, it’s not going to get into 
database specifics or anything like that.

03:56:53.300 --> 03:57:00.500
So, your job would be to take this information and 
then, you know, design database schema from it.

03:57:00.500 --> 03:57:04.000
And so, what you can do is you can take this.

03:57:04.000 --> 03:57:05.400
You can convert it into an ER diagram 

03:57:05.400 --> 03:57:09.300
and then you can take that ER diagram 
and convert it into a database schema.

03:57:09.300 --> 03:57:12.100
So, I’m going to show you guys the first step of that 

03:57:12.100 --> 03:57:16.000
which would be to take a document like this 
and convert it into an ER diagram.

03:57:16.000 --> 03:57:21.200
Which in the last video, I kind of walked you guys 
through what an ER diagram was and all that stuff.

03:57:21.200 --> 03:57:23.600
So, let’s go ahead and take a look at this document.

03:57:23.600 --> 03:57:25.400
I’m going to read through it.

03:57:25.400 --> 03:57:28.900
And then what we're going to do is we're going to 
design an ER diagram based off of it.

03:57:28.900 --> 03:57:33.200
So, over here it’s just Company Data Requirements.

03:57:33.200 --> 03:57:35.300
So, we're defining the data 
and all that stuff in a company.

03:57:35.300 --> 03:57:37.500
So, it says the company is organized into branches.

03:57:37.500 --> 03:57:42.200
Each branch has a unique number, a name, 
and a particular employee who manages it.

03:57:42.200 --> 03:57:45.300
The company makes its money by selling to clients.

03:57:45.300 --> 03:57:48.600
Each client has a name 
and a unique number to identify it.

03:57:48.600 --> 03:57:51.200
The foundation of the company is its employees.

03:57:51.200 --> 03:57:55.500
Each employee has a name, 
birthday, sex, salary, and a unique number.

03:57:55.500 --> 03:57:58.300
An employee can work for one branch at a time 

03:57:58.300 --> 03:58:01.200
and each branch will be managed 
by one of the employees that work there.

03:58:01.200 --> 03:58:05.300
We’ll also want to keep track of 
when the current manager started as manager.

03:58:05.300 --> 03:58:09.200
An employee can act as a supervisor 
for other employees at the branch.

03:58:09.200 --> 03:58:13.500
An employee may also act as a supervisor 
for employees at other branches.

03:58:13.500 --> 03:58:16.500
An employee can have at most one supervisor.

03:58:16.500 --> 03:58:18.800
A branch may handle a number of clients, 

03:58:18.800 --> 03:58:22.500
with each client having a name 
and a unique number to identify it.

03:58:22.500 --> 03:58:26.000
A single client may only be handled 
by one branch at a time.

03:58:26.000 --> 03:58:30.100
Employees can work with clients controlled 
by their branch to sell them stuff.

03:58:30.100 --> 03:58:33.500
If necessarily, multiple employees can work 
with the same client.

03:58:33.500 --> 03:58:36.600
We'll want to keep track of 
how many dollars worth of stuff 

03:58:36.600 --> 03:58:39.100
each employee sells to each client they work with.

03:58:39.100 --> 03:58:42.300
Many branches will need to work with suppliers 
to buy inventory.

03:58:42.300 --> 03:58:47.200
For each supplier we'll keep track of their name 
and the type of product they're selling the branch.

03:58:47.200 --> 03:58:49.900
A single supplier may supply products 
to multiple branches.

03:58:49.900 --> 03:58:54.200
So, this is our Company Data Requirements document.

03:58:54.200 --> 03:58:55.400
And there’s a lot here, right?

03:58:55.400 --> 03:58:58.700
It kind of took me like over a minute 
to go through and read all of this.

03:58:58.700 --> 03:59:01.600
And so, if you're given a document like this, 

03:59:01.600 --> 03:59:05.300
how do you go about converting this 
into a database schema?

03:59:05.300 --> 03:59:08.700
So, the first thing we want to do 
is create an ER diagram.

03:59:08.700 --> 03:59:10.800
So, what I’m going to do is I’m going to walk through 

03:59:10.800 --> 03:59:16.300
and show you guys how we can create an ER diagram 
for these data requirements, okay?

03:59:16.300 --> 03:59:20.000
And then in the next video I’ll show you guys 
how you can convert that ER diagram 

03:59:20.000 --> 03:59:21.800
into an actual database schema.

03:59:21.800 --> 03:59:24.100
So, let’s go ahead and take a look.

03:59:24.100 --> 03:59:28.600
I’m going to walk you guys through each line 
in that requirements document 

03:59:28.600 --> 03:59:30.400
and we'll convert it into our ER diagram.

03:59:30.400 --> 03:59:33.900
So, over here it says the company 
is organized into branches.

03:59:33.900 --> 03:59:36.200
Each branch has a unique number and a name.

03:59:36.200 --> 03:59:39.000
So, you'll notice that I’ve mode bold branches.

03:59:39.000 --> 03:59:41.100
So, branch is going to be our entity, right?

03:59:41.100 --> 03:59:42.500
We're defining an entity branch.

03:59:42.500 --> 03:59:46.600
And it’s going to have two attributes, a branch ID 
which is going to be our primary key, right?

03:59:46.600 --> 03:59:48.400
The branch has a unique number.

03:59:48.400 --> 03:59:51.500
So, that, to me, tells me 
that it’s going to be the primary key.

03:59:51.500 --> 03:59:53.600
And then obviously, the branch name.

03:59:53.600 --> 03:59:57.900
Next we have the company 
makes its money by selling to clients.

03:59:57.900 --> 04:00:01.100
So, right away there we have another entity.

04:00:01.100 --> 04:00:03.600
Each client has a name 
and a unique number to identify it.

04:00:03.600 --> 04:00:08.500
So, here we have our client 
which has their client ID which identifies it.

04:00:08.500 --> 04:00:12.400
And then their client name 
which is just going to be the name.

04:00:12.400 --> 04:00:16.500
And then over here it says the foundation 
of the company is its employees.

04:00:16.500 --> 04:00:21.200
Each employee has a name, birthday, sex, salary 
and a unique number to identify it.

04:00:21.200 --> 04:00:26.100
So, over here we have our employee and we have 
the employee ID which is the primary key.

04:00:26.100 --> 04:00:28.600
Birthday, name, so we get first and last name.

04:00:28.600 --> 04:00:30.800
And salary and then sex.

04:00:30.800 --> 04:00:34.900
And then so over here we also have a derived 
attribute which is going to be age.

04:00:34.900 --> 04:00:40.200
So, from the employees birth date we could derive 
how old they are at any given point.

04:00:40.200 --> 04:00:46.200
So, here we have our three entities 
that we got from this requirements document.

04:00:46.200 --> 04:00:51.500
So, now over here it says the employee can work 
for one branch at a time.

04:00:51.500 --> 04:00:55.800
So, over here we have a relationship, Work For, right?

04:00:55.800 --> 04:00:56.600
This is like a verb.

04:00:56.600 --> 04:01:04.600
So, an employee over here can work for a branch and 
a branch can have an employee working for it, right?

04:01:04.600 --> 04:01:06.000
So, that’s our relationship.

04:01:06.000 --> 04:01:11.300
And you'll notice over here 
I defined these as total participation.

04:01:11.300 --> 04:01:15.600
So, I’m saying that all branches 
must have employees working for them.

04:01:15.600 --> 04:01:17.700
That’s this double line right here.

04:01:17.700 --> 04:01:21.400
And I’m also saying all employees 
must work for a branch.

04:01:21.400 --> 04:01:26.900
So, both of those entities have a total participation 
in the Works For relationship.

04:01:26.900 --> 04:01:30.400
And that wasn’t rigorously defined 
inside of the document.

04:01:30.400 --> 04:01:35.700
But that’s just something that I kind of, you know 
set there as the person designing the ER diagram.

04:01:35.700 --> 04:01:38.400
And then over here 
we have our cardinality relationship.

04:01:38.400 --> 04:01:40.800
So, I'm saying that – basically what this says 

04:01:40.800 --> 04:01:44.900
is that a branch can have 
any number of employees working for it.

04:01:44.900 --> 04:01:48.300
And an employee can work for one branch.

04:01:48.300 --> 04:01:50.600
So, I’m going to say that one more time.

04:01:50.600 --> 04:01:53.100
A branch can have any number 
of employees working for it.

04:01:53.100 --> 04:01:56.100
And an employee can work for one branch.

04:01:56.100 --> 04:01:59.400
That’s what that cardinality relationship 
is defining right there.

04:01:59.400 --> 04:02:02.700
So, next we have another relationship.

04:02:02.700 --> 04:02:06.600
It says each branch will be managed 
by one of the employees that work there.

04:02:06.600 --> 04:02:11.100
We’ll also want to keep track of 
when the current manager started as manager.

04:02:11.100 --> 04:02:14.300
So, over here we have another relationship 
which is manage, right?

04:02:14.300 --> 04:02:17.000
An employee can manage a branch.

04:02:17.000 --> 04:02:22.900
So, employee can manage a branch 
and a branch can be managed by an employee.

04:02:22.900 --> 04:02:27.900
And then you'll also notice over here that 
on this relationship we've defined an attribute.

04:02:27.900 --> 04:02:32.200
So, we want to keep track of when 
the employee started as the manager, right?

04:02:32.200 --> 04:02:36.400
So, when does the employee start as the manager 
and that’s what we're defining over here.

04:02:36.400 --> 04:02:39.900
So, we're defining this attribute 
on the actual relationship.

04:02:39.900 --> 04:02:42.000
And now let’s take a look at the participation.

04:02:42.000 --> 04:02:46.500
So, all branches must have someone managing them.

04:02:46.500 --> 04:02:49.300
So, you'll notice that we have 
this full participation here, right?

04:02:49.300 --> 04:02:53.200
Every branch is going to participate 
in that Manages relationship.

04:02:53.200 --> 04:02:54.900
All branches need to have a manager.

04:02:54.900 --> 04:02:58.400
But over here on the employee 
it’s partial participation, right?

04:02:58.400 --> 04:03:01.600
Not all employees need to be managers of a branch.

04:03:01.600 --> 04:03:06.900
In fact, by a large majority most employees 
will not be the manager of a branch.

04:03:06.900 --> 04:03:11.400
And so, that’s why we define this as single 
participation or partial participation.

04:03:11.400 --> 04:03:16.600
So, not all employees are going to manage a branch, 
but all branches will be managed by employees.

04:03:16.600 --> 04:03:19.400
And then over here 
we have our cardinality relationships.

04:03:19.400 --> 04:03:24.300
So, we're saying that an employee 
can manage one branch 

04:03:24.300 --> 04:03:27.000
and a branch can be managed by one employee.

04:03:27.000 --> 04:03:29.100
So, that kind of makes sense.

04:03:29.100 --> 04:03:31.900
So, down here we also have another relationship.

04:03:31.900 --> 04:03:36.200
It says an employee can act as a supervisor 
for other employees at the branch.

04:03:36.200 --> 04:03:40.300
An employee may also act as a supervisor 
for employees at other branches.

04:03:40.300 --> 04:03:42.800
That employee can have at most one supervisor.

04:03:42.800 --> 04:03:45.200
So, over here we get this supervision relationship.

04:03:45.200 --> 04:03:51.200
Now you'll notice that the supervision relationship is 
actually a relationship that an employee has to itself.

04:03:51.200 --> 04:03:54.000
So, this is a relationship between employees.

04:03:54.000 --> 04:03:59.800
So, over here we have an employee 
can be supervised by another employee 

04:03:59.800 --> 04:04:02.600
and an employee can be the supervisor 
of another employee, right?

04:04:02.600 --> 04:04:10.000
So, over here, basically we're saying that an employee 
can be the supervisee of only one supervisor.

04:04:10.000 --> 04:04:12.400
So you can only have one supervisor.

04:04:12.400 --> 04:04:16.500
But an employee can supervise 
any number of employees.

04:04:16.500 --> 04:04:23.500
So, one more time, I’ll just say that an employee can 
be supervised by one other employee, one supervisor.

04:04:23.500 --> 04:04:28.200
And a supervisor can be the supervisor 
of any number of employees.

04:04:28.200 --> 04:04:30.900
All right, so over here we have another relationship.

04:04:30.900 --> 04:04:33.300
It says a branch may handle a number of clients, 

04:04:33.300 --> 04:04:38.200
however a single client may only be handled 
by one branch at a time.

04:04:38.200 --> 04:04:43.200
So, over here we have the new relationship 
between the branch and the client.

04:04:43.200 --> 04:04:48.900
So, I’m saying that a branch can handle a client 
and a client can be handled by a branch, right?

04:04:48.900 --> 04:04:53.000
So, maybe a branch might have a bunch of clients 
that it works with, or whatever.

04:04:53.000 --> 04:04:56.200
And so, the client has a total participation 
in this relationship.

04:04:56.200 --> 04:04:59.500
That means that every client must be handled 
by a branch.

04:04:59.500 --> 04:05:02.600
But if the branch has a partial participation, 

04:05:02.600 --> 04:05:05.900
which means that not all branches 
need to have clients, right?

04:05:05.900 --> 04:05:09.600
Maybe you’d have like a corporate branch 
that doesn’t work with any clients.

04:05:09.600 --> 04:05:12.400
Or maybe you’d have like an RND branch 
that doesn’t work with clients.

04:05:12.400 --> 04:05:14.300
But you'd have other branches that do.

04:05:14.300 --> 04:05:16.300
And so, that’s why we would define that relationship.

04:05:16.300 --> 04:05:18.600
And then also we have our cardinality relationship 

04:05:18.600 --> 04:05:22.400
which would be a branch can handle 
any number of clients, right?

04:05:22.400 --> 04:05:24.500
So, the branch can handle N clients.

04:05:24.500 --> 04:05:26.900
And a client can be handled by one branch.

04:05:26.900 --> 04:05:29.400
So, if you're a client, you can only work 
with one branch.

04:05:29.400 --> 04:05:32.100
But if you’re branch, you can work 
with multiple clients.

04:05:32.100 --> 04:05:34.500
And that’s what we're defining over here.

04:05:34.500 --> 04:05:37.600
All right, and then over here 
we have another relationship.

04:05:37.600 --> 04:05:40.500
And I realize this is getting a little cluttered 
and I’m actually in the way.

04:05:40.500 --> 04:05:45.800
But it says employees can work with clients controlled 
by their branch to sell them stuff.

04:05:45.800 --> 04:05:50.500
If necessary, multiple employees can work 
with the same client.

04:05:50.500 --> 04:05:54.000
So, now we have a relationship 
between employees and clients.

04:05:54.000 --> 04:05:58.500
So, we have an employee works with a client.

04:05:58.500 --> 04:06:01.100
And then a client can work with an employee.

04:06:01.100 --> 04:06:03.000
Now, notice the participation.

04:06:03.000 --> 04:06:06.500
So, all clients must work with an employee.

04:06:06.500 --> 04:06:09.700
But not all employees must work with clients, right?

04:06:09.700 --> 04:06:15.100
So, all clients need to interact with the branch 
through an employee, 

04:06:15.100 --> 04:06:17.900
but not all employees need to interact with clients.

04:06:17.900 --> 04:06:22.800
You'll also see down here it says we'll want to keep 
track of how many dollars worth of stuff 

04:06:22.800 --> 04:06:25.100
each employee sells to each client they work with.

04:06:25.100 --> 04:06:30.400
So, on this Works With relationship, 
we're defining this attribute, right?

04:06:30.400 --> 04:06:35.900
So, the employee can sell to the client 
and the client can buy from the employee 

04:06:35.900 --> 04:06:38.600
and that’s where we're getting this from.

04:06:38.600 --> 04:06:40.200
And finally, we'll look at the cardinality.

04:06:40.200 --> 04:06:44.300
So, a client can work with any number of employees.

04:06:44.300 --> 04:06:46.600
And an employee can work 
with any number of clients.

04:06:46.600 --> 04:06:51.500
And so, that’s basically the relationship 
that we get from this line up here.

04:06:51.500 --> 04:06:57.000
All right, so over here we have our final 
little section of this requirements document.

04:06:57.000 --> 04:07:00.700
It says many branches will need to work 
with suppliers to buy inventory.

04:07:00.700 --> 04:07:06.200
For each supplier we'll keep track of their name 
and the type of product they're selling the branch.

04:07:06.200 --> 04:07:09.500
A single supplier may supply products 
to multiple branches.

04:07:09.500 --> 04:07:16.100
So, this is an example where we would need 
to use a weak entity and an identifying relationship.

04:07:16.100 --> 04:07:18.500
So, over here we have this 
weak entity branch supplier.

04:07:18.500 --> 04:07:21.200
And it has a supplier name and a supply type, 

04:07:21.200 --> 04:07:25.500
but the branch supplier is going to 
supply a specific branch.

04:07:25.500 --> 04:07:30.800
Now, we want to keep track of which branch suppliers 
are supplying which branches.

04:07:30.800 --> 04:07:35.500
And in order to do that, we're going to 
have to use this identifying relationship.

04:07:35.500 --> 04:07:40.200
So, we can say the branch supplier supplies 
a branch and a branch gets supplied by a supplier.

04:07:40.200 --> 04:07:43.900
And you can see that we have 
these cardinality ratios over here.

04:07:43.900 --> 04:07:48.800
So, this is basically our entire ER diagram, right?

04:07:48.800 --> 04:07:54.900
We have from that requirements document we've 
been able to map out all of the different entities, 

04:07:54.900 --> 04:07:59.000
all the different attributes on the entities 
and all the different relationships.

04:07:59.000 --> 04:08:01.500
And basically, we get this diagram.

04:08:01.500 --> 04:08:05.800
And this diagram is – it’s just linking 
all that information together, right?

04:08:05.800 --> 04:08:10.900
It’s visually representing all of that information 
in a way that is defining it.

04:08:10.900 --> 04:08:14.800
So, what we can do now 
is we can take this ER diagram 

04:08:14.800 --> 04:08:18.900
and depending on the different relationships, 
the different cardinality ratios, 

04:08:18.900 --> 04:08:20.500
the different participations, 

04:08:20.500 --> 04:08:24.600
we can actually go ahead and convert this 
into a database schema 

04:08:24.600 --> 04:08:28.400
which I'm going to show you guys 
how to do in the next video.

04:08:28.400 --> 04:08:33.300
So, stick around for that and we'll go ahead and 
design our database based off this ER diagram.

04:08:33.300 --> 04:08:37.200
[Converting ER Diagrams to Schemas]

04:08:37.200 --> 04:08:43.200
In this tutorial I’m going to show you guys how to 
convert an ER diagram into a database schema.

04:08:43.200 --> 04:08:47.500
So, basically we're going to take 
all of this information inside this ER diagram 

04:08:47.500 --> 04:08:49.700
which we created in the last tutorial.

04:08:49.700 --> 04:08:55.000
And we're actually going to take this 
and use it to create an actually database schema.

04:08:55.000 --> 04:09:01.400
So, from this diagram right here we'll be able to, 
you know, create and define actually database tables 

04:09:01.400 --> 04:09:04.700
that we can use in our relational database.

04:09:04.700 --> 04:09:06.300
So, let’s go ahead and get started.

04:09:06.300 --> 04:09:09.000
I’m going to show you guys basically step by step 

04:09:09.000 --> 04:09:14.500
how we can start converting this 
into database tables and database relations.

04:09:14.500 --> 04:09:19.200
So, the first step, Step 1, 
is the mapping of regular entity types.

04:09:19.200 --> 04:09:23.100
So, for each regular entity type, 
we want to create a relation 

04:09:23.100 --> 04:09:28.400
which is just a table, that includes 
all of the simple attributes of that entity.

04:09:28.400 --> 04:09:31.100
So, here we have all of our regular entities.

04:09:31.100 --> 04:09:33.300
We have branch, client, and employee.

04:09:33.300 --> 04:09:36.800
And so, what we want to do 
is just create relations or, you know, 

04:09:36.800 --> 04:09:40.300
basically database tables for each one 
of those regular entities.

04:09:40.300 --> 04:09:45.600
And then the columns of those relations are going to 
be all of these attributes that we defined.

04:09:45.600 --> 04:09:50.500
So, from that, from everything that’s inside of the 
green squares we're going to get something like this.

04:09:50.500 --> 04:09:52.700
So, we have our employee relation.

04:09:52.700 --> 04:09:55.000
We have the employee ID which is the primary key.

04:09:55.000 --> 04:09:58.100
First name, last name, birthdate, sex, and salary.

04:09:58.100 --> 04:10:01.600
And we have the Branch with that information.

04:10:01.600 --> 04:10:02.600
And then we have the Client.

04:10:02.600 --> 04:10:06.700
So, I just want to show you guys 
when we had a composite attribute, 

04:10:06.700 --> 04:10:11.200
so this name attribute over here, 
we're just storing the sub-attribute.

04:10:11.200 --> 04:10:13.800
So, we're just storing first name and last name.

04:10:13.800 --> 04:10:17.100
All right, so here we have three relations 
and lets see if we can start adding to those.

04:10:17.100 --> 04:10:20.400
So, Step 2 is the mapping of weak entity types.

04:10:20.400 --> 04:10:24.300
So, over here we have one weak entity type 
which is inside of this green square.

04:10:24.300 --> 04:10:28.200
For weak entity type we want to create 
a relation or a table 

04:10:28.200 --> 04:10:33.500
that includes all of the simple attributes 
of the weak entity.

04:10:33.500 --> 04:10:37.100
And the primary key for the new relation should be 

04:10:37.100 --> 04:10:40.800
the partial key of the weak entity 
plus the primary key of its owner.

04:10:40.800 --> 04:10:47.500
In this case, the primary key of the owner is going to 
be branch ID because the branch is the owner.

04:10:47.500 --> 04:10:49.300
In other words, the branch is the entity 

04:10:49.300 --> 04:10:54.400
that’s participating in the identifying relationship 
with branch supplier.

04:10:54.400 --> 04:10:56.700
So, over here we'll see what happens.

04:10:56.700 --> 04:10:59.300
So, you'll see we get this new table, Branch Supplier.

04:10:59.300 --> 04:11:04.900
And the primary key is branch_id supplier_name, 
and supply_type.

04:11:04.900 --> 04:11:09.100
So, for this table, we included the supplier’s name 
and the branch ID.

04:11:09.100 --> 04:11:12.600
Both of those come together 
to make our composite key.

04:11:12.600 --> 04:11:13.800
It’s a compound key.

04:11:13.800 --> 04:11:17.900
And then we have supply type 
and then we end up with this.

04:11:17.900 --> 04:11:21.000
So, now we have four tables, Employee, 
Branch, Client, and Branch Supplier.

04:11:21.000 --> 04:11:23.800
And they all have their associated attributes.

04:11:23.800 --> 04:11:28.700
So, Step 3 is the mapping of 
binary 1 to 1 relationship types.

04:11:28.700 --> 04:11:33.900
Now, a binary relationship is a relationship 
that has two entities participating in it.

04:11:33.900 --> 04:11:37.800
For example, all of the actual relationships up here 
are binary.

04:11:37.800 --> 04:11:40.600
In other words, there’s two parties 
that are participating.

04:11:40.600 --> 04:11:43.700
And what we want to do is map 1 to 1 relationships.

04:11:43.700 --> 04:11:47.400
So, we only have a single 1 to 1 relationship here.

04:11:47.400 --> 04:11:48.600
It’s this manages relationship.

04:11:48.600 --> 04:11:52.700
So, a branch can be managed by one employee 
and an employee can manage 1 branch.

04:11:52.700 --> 04:11:59.700
So, for each 1 to 1 binary relationship 
we want to include one side of the relationship 

04:11:59.700 --> 04:12:02.300
as a foreign key in the other.

04:12:02.300 --> 04:12:05.200
And we want to favor total participation.

04:12:05.200 --> 04:12:11.500
So, in this case, we want to basically include 
the primary key of one of these entities 

04:12:11.500 --> 04:12:15.600
as a foreign key in the other entity’s relation.

04:12:15.600 --> 04:12:18.500
And we always want to favor the total participation.

04:12:18.500 --> 04:12:22.200
So, if a particular entity has total participation 
in their relationship, 

04:12:22.200 --> 04:12:25.200
then you want to add the foreign key 
onto that entity.

04:12:25.200 --> 04:12:27.700
So, in this case, branch has a total participation, 

04:12:27.700 --> 04:12:32.600
so we're going to add the employee’s ID 
as a foreign key in the branch relation.

04:12:32.600 --> 04:12:36.500
If both of them are partial participation 
or both of them are total participation, 

04:12:36.500 --> 04:12:38.500
then you can just use your own discretion.

04:12:38.500 --> 04:12:41.600
But in this case, it’s pretty clear 
that we're going to use branch.

04:12:41.600 --> 04:12:47.600
So, over here on the branch relation, I added in 
a foreign key which is just manager ID.

04:12:47.600 --> 04:12:54.100
And this is a foreign key which points to 
this employee’s ID up here.

04:12:54.100 --> 04:12:56.800
So, that’s how we're going to link 
those two together.

04:12:56.800 --> 04:13:00.500
Step 4 is the mapping of 
binary 1 to N relationship types.

04:13:00.500 --> 04:13:04.700
So, unlike a 1 to 1 relation, 
now we're looking for 1 to N.

04:13:04.700 --> 04:13:07.600
And you'll see that we have three of them here.

04:13:07.600 --> 04:13:09.000
So, branch handles a client.

04:13:09.000 --> 04:13:12.500
An employee supervises or is supervised 
by another employee.

04:13:12.500 --> 04:13:15.800
And a branch has employees working for it.

04:13:15.800 --> 04:13:17.600
So, basically what we want to do 

04:13:17.600 --> 04:13:25.800
is we want to include the one side’s primary key 
as a foreign key on the inside relation or table.

04:13:25.800 --> 04:13:28.900
So, basically, what this means is that – 

04:13:28.900 --> 04:13:32.400
okay, so for example, 
in this case we have a branch and an employee.

04:13:32.400 --> 04:13:35.000
I want to include the one side’s primary key, right?

04:13:35.000 --> 04:13:38.300
In other words, I want to include 
the branch’s primary key 

04:13:38.300 --> 04:13:42.800
because that’s on the one side as a foreign key 
on the employee relation.

04:13:42.800 --> 04:13:45.700
So, basically on the employee relation, 

04:13:45.700 --> 04:13:50.200
now we're going to have a branch ID column 
that will store a foreign key to the branch.

04:13:50.200 --> 04:13:53.100
Same thing down here for a client and branch.

04:13:53.100 --> 04:13:55.500
So, the branch over here is on the 1 side.

04:13:55.500 --> 04:14:02.300
And basically, what that means is that we're going to 
store in the Client table a foreign key to the branch.

04:14:02.300 --> 04:14:05.800
And then same goes for this supervisor relationship.

04:14:05.800 --> 04:14:11.500
So, on the Employee table we want to 
store a foreign key to the supervisor.

04:14:11.500 --> 04:14:14.100
And so, over here let’s take a look.

04:14:14.100 --> 04:14:17.800
So, employee we have a super_id 
and we have branch_id.

04:14:17.800 --> 04:14:23.100
And that’s because the branch was 
on the one side of the relationship 

04:14:23.100 --> 04:14:25.600
and the employee was on the N side of the relationship.

04:14:25.600 --> 04:14:30.100
Which means that we're going to go ahead 
and store the branch’s ID as a foreign key 

04:14:30.100 --> 04:14:31.500
on the Employee table.

04:14:31.500 --> 04:14:34.400
And then the same goes for the supervisor ID.

04:14:34.400 --> 04:14:37.500
So, obviously, with the supervisor ID, 
it’s pointing to the Employee table.

04:14:37.500 --> 04:14:39.500
So, we have to store it on the Employee table.

04:14:39.500 --> 04:14:44.600
And then down here in the Client table 
we stored as a foreign key the branch ID.

04:14:44.600 --> 04:14:50.600
And again, that’s because the branch 
was on the 1 side of that cardinality relationship.

04:14:50.600 --> 04:14:54.200
You see, branch is on the one side 
and the client was on the N side.

04:14:54.200 --> 04:14:59.800
And so, that’s why we stored branch_id 
as a foreign key on the Client table.

04:14:59.800 --> 04:15:04.900
All right, and then Step 5 is the mapping 
of binary M to N relationship types.

04:15:04.900 --> 04:15:08.600
So, in this case, we only have one instance of this.

04:15:08.600 --> 04:15:12.300
A client can work with an employee 
and an employee can work with a client.

04:15:12.300 --> 04:15:16.600
So, what we want to do in this case 
is we want to create a new relation.

04:15:16.600 --> 04:15:18.600
So, we're actually going to create a new table 

04:15:18.600 --> 04:15:23.900
whose primary key is a combination 
of both entity’s primary keys.

04:15:23.900 --> 04:15:27.400
And we’re also going to 
include any relationship attributes.

04:15:27.400 --> 04:15:30.700
So, over here, the client’s primary key is client_id.

04:15:30.700 --> 04:15:32.900
The employee’s primary key is emp_id.

04:15:32.900 --> 04:15:35.600
So, what we're going to do is create a new table 

04:15:35.600 --> 04:15:40.300
where we have a composite key 
which is the employee ID and the client ID.

04:15:40.300 --> 04:15:44.200
And actually, this would be 
what we would call a compound key 

04:15:44.200 --> 04:15:49.900
because both of it’s keys are actually foreign keys.

04:15:49.900 --> 04:15:53.900
And we want to store in this relationship 
the attribute on the relationship, 

04:15:53.900 --> 04:15:57.800
or any attributes on the relationship 
which in this case is just sales.

04:15:57.800 --> 04:16:01.500
So, down here we created a new table 
or a new relation which is Works On.

04:16:01.500 --> 04:16:05.300
And you'll notice the key 
is employee ID and client ID.

04:16:05.300 --> 04:16:10.300
So, both of these individual parts of the primary key 
are actually foreign keys themselves.

04:16:10.300 --> 04:16:12.300
So, this is a special situation.

04:16:12.300 --> 04:16:14.300
And then over here we have total sales 

04:16:14.300 --> 04:16:19.500
which was the attribute that we stored 
on the relationship like that.

04:16:19.500 --> 04:16:28.500
And so, basically that, in essence, is going to allow us 
to take this ER diagram and convert it into relations.

04:16:28.500 --> 04:16:34.600
Now if you have more complex relationships, 
like if you have nonbinary relationships 

04:16:34.600 --> 04:16:38.300
then it gets a little bit more complex 
when we're mapping them.

04:16:38.300 --> 04:16:42.000
In this case, I’m just kind of looking at 
basic ER diagrams.

04:16:42.000 --> 04:16:43.600
I don’t want to get too complex.

04:16:43.600 --> 04:16:50.800
So, in this case, in five steps, we're able to basically 
convert the ER diagram into a set of relations.

04:16:50.800 --> 04:16:56.300
But if you do have more advanced types of ER 
diagrams, then there are going to be more steps.

04:16:56.300 --> 04:17:02.000
But for our cases, there’s only going to be five steps 
that we need to basically convert this into relations.

04:17:02.000 --> 04:17:07.000
And so, now basically what we have here 
is we have our database tables, right?

04:17:07.000 --> 04:17:10.000
Each of these relations is itself a database table.

04:17:10.000 --> 04:17:12.700
So, when I'm designing my database now, 

04:17:12.700 --> 04:17:16.800
I know I have to have an Employee table with 
all this stuff, a Branch table with all this stuff.

04:17:16.800 --> 04:17:18.400
Client table, etc.

04:17:18.400 --> 04:17:21.700
And so, what we can do also 
and what you'll see a lot of times 

04:17:21.700 --> 04:17:25.000
is people will draw little arrows 
to define the relationships.

04:17:25.000 --> 04:17:29.500
So, this can get a little bit messy 
which is why I kind of saved it for the end.

04:17:29.500 --> 04:17:32.700
But you can see over here, for employee, 

04:17:32.700 --> 04:17:38.400
on the employee’s foreign keys 
I’m drawing arrows to what they relate to.

04:17:38.400 --> 04:17:42.300
So, for example, super_id, 
I have an arrow going back to emp_id.

04:17:42.300 --> 04:17:46.200
branch_id I have a little line here going over 
to branch_id.

04:17:46.200 --> 04:17:50.300
Mgr_id over here, we have a line 
going up to emp_id, etc.

04:17:50.300 --> 04:17:54.600
So, this is basically just like mapping out 
all the different relationships.

04:17:54.600 --> 04:17:56.800
This, like I said, it gets a little messy.

04:17:56.800 --> 04:18:01.100
And it’s pretty difficult to read 
if you have more than a couple tables.

04:18:01.100 --> 04:18:06.200
But you also – you'll see people doing this a lot, 
so I just wanted to show you guys how that works.

04:18:06.200 --> 04:18:10.300
So, now that we have our, you know, 
essentially our relations, our database tables, 

04:18:10.300 --> 04:18:12.200
we can actually create a database.

04:18:12.200 --> 04:18:17.100
So, over here I have an example of what 
a database might look like from these relations.

04:18:17.100 --> 04:18:19.300
So, we have our actually database.

04:18:19.300 --> 04:18:21.200
So, up here we have all our employees.

04:18:21.200 --> 04:18:23.200
And you'll notice we have our employee ID 

04:18:23.200 --> 04:18:25.900
so we can define like the supervisor 
of each employee.

04:18:25.900 --> 04:18:30.000
So, like Angela Martin’s supervisor 
is employee number 101.

04:18:30.000 --> 04:18:32.600
So, Angela Martin’s supervisor 
is Michael Scott, right.

04:18:32.600 --> 04:18:35.500
You'll see how it easy it is now for us 
to define all this stuff.

04:18:35.500 --> 04:18:38.600
Angela Martin also works at branch_id number 2.

04:18:38.600 --> 04:18:40.400
So, that links over here to the branch.

04:18:40.400 --> 04:18:42.100
So, branch 2 is Scranton, etc.

04:18:42.100 --> 04:18:45.100
And then we have our Client table over here.

04:18:45.100 --> 04:18:47.000
And we have our Works With table.

04:18:47.000 --> 04:18:50.600
So, the Works With table 
has the employee ID and the client ID.

04:18:50.600 --> 04:18:53.300
And then we have our Branch Supplier table.

04:18:53.300 --> 04:18:59.900
So, all of these got basically put into our database 
and then we started putting information in there.

04:18:59.900 --> 04:19:05.800
And so, really what this is, is it’s a way for us 
to go from just a set of requirements 

04:19:05.800 --> 04:19:11.900
like we saw in the last video, to our actual 
finished database table, what you see here.

04:19:11.900 --> 04:19:15.000
In designing relational database schemas, 

04:19:15.000 --> 04:19:17.900
and you know, the schema 
is just like this whole thing, right?

04:19:17.900 --> 04:19:19.700
It’s not super easy.

04:19:19.700 --> 04:19:24.800
If you have a very simple database, you know, 
if you have a very simple set of database storage 

04:19:24.800 --> 04:19:28.000
or requirements, then obviously the schema 
is going to be very simple 

04:19:28.000 --> 04:19:30.800
and you might not need something like an ER diagram.

04:19:30.800 --> 04:19:35.700
But with something like this, 
an ER diagram is hugely useful.

04:19:35.700 --> 04:19:38.400
So, here’s the thing, you don’t necessarily 
need the ER diagram, right?

04:19:38.400 --> 04:19:39.800
You don’t need it.

04:19:39.800 --> 04:19:43.900
But it’s a really, really great way 
to convert requirements 

04:19:43.900 --> 04:19:48.600
into an actual database schema 
or a set of relations.

04:19:48.600 --> 04:19:51.300
And so, that kind of show you guys 
how you can do that.

04:19:51.300 --> 04:19:53.300
Now, here’s the thing with ER diagram.

